(window.MUSEUM_MATCH_T = window.MUSEUM_MATCH_T || {})["en"] = {"ui":{"lead":"Great games, one move at a time. Follow famous games on the board while Musowl explains each move.","all":"All","k_chess":"Chess","k_shogi":"Shogi","k_go":"Go","k_xiangqi":"Xiangqi","side_r":"Red","res_r":"Red won","back":"Back to Great Games","start":"Start","prev":"Back one move","next":"Forward one move","end":"End","play":"Play","pause":"Pause","moves":"Moves","move":"Move {n}","startpos":"Start","nextnote":"Next note (move {n})","quiet":"No note for this move.","guide":"Musowl","keys":"← → step through the moves","result":"Result","r_event":"Game","r_date":"Year","r_place":"Place","side_w":"White","side_b":"Black","side_s":"Sente","side_g":"Gote","side_B":"Black","side_W":"White","res_w":"White won","res_b":"Black won","res_d":"Draw","res_s":"Sente won","res_g":"Gote won","credit":"The moves are the public record of each game. The notes are by the museum’s Musowl.","caps":"Captured","hand":"In hand","pnote":"Previous note","nnote":"Next note","plies":"{n} moves","about":"About this game","ev":"Evaluation","even":"Even","evby":"Read by this site’s engine (a rough guide): the first player’s chance of winning","r_dp":"When & where","k_reversi":"Reversi","pass":"Pass","discs":"{n} discs"},"opera":{"t":"The Opera Game","w":"Paul Morphy","b":"Duke Karl II of Brunswick and Count Isouard","ev":"Played in a box at the Paris Opera (the two allies consulting)","d":"1858","pl":"France (Paris)","lead":"The young American champion against two noblemen, during a performance at the opera: seventeen moves. Bring the pieces out fast, give them up without a second thought, and finish with very few. No game appears in more chess books.","c":{"0":"Paris, 1858. Twenty-one-year-old American Paul Morphy had been beating Europe's strong players one after another. That evening, in a box at the opera, the Duke of Brunswick and Count Isouard took Black together and played him, talking over each move. It is said Morphy wanted to finish quickly so he could watch the stage. These seventeen moves are full of the basics: bring pieces out fast, keep your king safe. Morphy was born in New Orleans, in the American South. His father was a lawyer who became a judge on the state's Supreme Court, and Morphy also studied law at university. But when he graduated he was still too young to work as a lawyer, so in the meantime he set off on a chess journey to Europe.","1":"White pushes the king's pawn two squares. It eyes d5 and e5 in the centre and opens paths for the bishop and queen. That night's venue was the Italian opera house in Paris (the Salle Ventadour). The opera being performed is said to have been Rossini's The Barber of Seville, or perhaps Bellini's Norma; the sources disagree, and it is still not certain today.","2":"Black also plays e5. The pawns face each other in the centre, and White's pawn cannot go further. On this 1858 trip, Morphy caused a sensation at the Café de la Régence in Paris by playing eight opponents at once while blindfolded, and beating them. In December of the same year he also won a match against Anderssen, thought to be Europe's best, with 7 wins, 2 losses and 2 draws.","3":"The knight comes to f3 and attacks the e5 pawn. A good move: it develops a piece and makes Black think about defending. The start 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 is, almost 200 years later, still one of the most common beginnings of all, used by everyone from beginners to world champions.","4":"Black guards e5 with d6. This is called the Philidor Defence. It is solid, but it makes the path of the bishop on f8 a little narrow. Philidor, who gave it its name, was an 18th‑century Frenchman: a chess master and also a musician who composed eleven operas. He recommended this move in a book he published in 1749. Isn't it a fun coincidence that the Opera Game begins with a defence named after an opera composer? Today it is thought of as solid but a little cramped.","5":"White plays d4, bringing another pawn to the centre to hit e5. Black must decide whether to take or defend. Against the Philidor Defence, hitting the centre at once with 3.d4 like this has always been, and still is, the main line.","6":"Black's bishop goes to g4. It presses the knight on f3, which stands in line with White's queen on d1. If the knight moves, the queen can be taken, so the knight cannot go after e5. However, this 3...Bg4 has long been considered a poor move. Later world champions Steinitz and Fischer both judged it weak. In today's opening theory, the usual choices are 3...exd4, taking the pawn, or 3...Nf6, developing the knight.","7":"White takes on e5 with the pawn. If Black took back at once with the d6 pawn, White would trade queens and then the knight would grab the e5 pawn. So Black first wants to get rid of the knight on f3.","8":"Black takes the knight with the bishop. Now e5 can be won back safely. But giving up the bishop that had just come out for the knight leaves Black behind in development. One of the Black players, the Duke of Brunswick, was the former ruler of the Duchy of Brunswick in Germany. He was driven out of his land during the upheavals of 1830, and afterwards lived mostly in Paris and London. All over Europe, people gossiped about his luxury and his huge collection of jewels.","9":"White takes back with the queen. White's queen is already working near the middle, while Black has hardly brought out any pieces. The Duke loved chess. A newspaper report from the year before even says he made such a noise playing chess at the same Italian opera house, in the middle of a performance, that he was asked to be quiet.","10":"Black takes back with a pawn, and the material is even. But White is ahead in pieces that are out and ready. The other player, Count Isouard, was a French nobleman whose family owned a château at Vauvenargues in the south of France. Even two grown men putting their heads together were no match for the 21-year-old.","11":"Bishop to c4. Together with the queen on f3 it aims at f7. Only the king guards f7, so it is a weak square; taking it would be mate.","12":"Black brings the knight to f6 and blocks the f-file. Without this, queen takes f7 would be mate. Defending while developing is the right answer.","13":"The queen swings to b3 and attacks f7 and b7 at the same time. A move with two threats is strong, because the opponent cannot defend both.","14":"Black plays the queen to e7 to guard f7, letting b7 go. But the queen now blocks the bishop on f8. In fact, Black had no good alternative to Qe7. For example, analysts have shown that defending with Qd7 lets White take on b7 and win a rook as well.","15":"Morphy does not take the pawn on b7; he develops a knight instead. Taking would send the queen far away. Choosing fast development over a pawn is typical of Morphy. It is said that if White had taken on b7, Black could check with the queen on b4 and force a queen trade, and White's attack would have fizzled out.","16":"Black plays c6, covering d5 and b5 and opening a road for the queen to b6.","17":"Bishop to g5 pins the knight on f6. If the knight moves, the queen on e7 behind it can be taken. Black's pieces find it harder and harder to move.","18":"Black plays b5 to chase the bishop from c4. It also gains room on the queen's side, but it opens a gap for White's pieces to jump in. This 9...b5 is said to be the move that lost, but even later analysis has found no move here that clearly saves Black. Black's troubles had really begun much earlier, with 3...Bg4.","19":"The knight is given up to take on b5! Instead of retreating the bishop, Morphy keeps attacking. With Black's king still in the middle, this is very dangerous for Black.","20":"Black takes the knight and is a knight ahead. But Black's king is still on e8, and White's pieces keep gathering.","21":"Bishop to b5, check! Black's king is still stuck in the middle. White has two pawns for the knight and comes straight at the king.","22":"Black blocks with the knight on d7. But this knight is pinned: if it moves, the black king is in check. White will now aim at d7.","23":"White castles queenside. The king is safe, and at the same time the rook arrives on d1 to aim at d7. One move does both defence and attack: a superb move.","24":"Black brings the rook to d8 to guard d7. Black still cannot castle, and the rook on h8 and bishop on f8 are doing nothing.","25":"The rook is given up to take on d7! White hands over one rook to remove one of Black's defenders.","26":"Black takes back with the rook. Now this rook on d7 is pinned too: if it moves, the bishop on b5 checks the black king.","27":"The other rook comes to d1 and attacks d7 again. Now more white pieces attack d7 than black pieces defend it.","28":"Black's queen goes to e6, offering a queen trade to push White's queen away from b3, and hoping to help d7. But it is too late.","29":"The bishop takes on d7 with check. White removes Black's defenders one by one.","30":"Black takes back with the knight. With the knight gone from f6, the diagonal from the bishop on g5 to d8 is open. That diagonal will matter at the very end.","31":"The queen is given up on b8, with check! This is the most famous move of the game. Black must take with the knight, and when the knight leaves d7 the d-file opens.","32":"Black takes with the knight. Now no black piece guards d8.","33":"Rook to d8, checkmate! The bishop on g5 supports d8, and the black king is boxed in by its own pieces. White gave away queen, rook and bishop, and finished with just a rook and a bishop. Develop quickly, keep your king safe, and attack while the enemy king is stuck in the middle: this famous game shows all these important ideas."}},"reti1910":{"t":"Réti's Mate","w":"Richard Réti","b":"Savielly Tartakower","ev":"A casual game (not a tournament)","d":"1910","pl":"Austria (Vienna)","lead":"Two future grandmasters, a casual game in Vienna, eleven moves. White gives up the queen and finishes with a double check: two pieces checking at once. The mating pattern is named after White: Réti's Mate.","c":{"0":"Vienna, 1910. White is twenty-year-old Richard Réti; Black is Savielly Tartakower. Both went on to become top players and are known for bringing new ideas to chess. This was not a tournament game but a casual one, played for a small stake. It is short, but it shows clearly why you should get your king safe early. Réti was born in the town of Pezinok, in today's Slovakia, and his father was an army doctor. He had come to Vienna to study mathematics at the university. Tartakower was born in Rostov-on-Don in Russia; he studied law at the universities of Geneva and Vienna, and people called him 'Doctor'.","1":"White pushes the king's pawn two squares. Taking the middle of the board makes it easier for the pieces behind to come out. The bishop and queen now have open paths. According to a newspaper chess column of the time, the stake was 10 crowns, and the game was played with a clock, at a pace of 15 moves per hour.","2":"Black plays c6. Instead of going to the centre at once, Black gets ready for d5 to hit the pawn on e4. This solid opening is called the Caro-Kann Defence. It is named after two 19th‑century players: Horatio Caro, who was active in Berlin, and Marcus Kann. Caro published an article analysing this move in a chess magazine in 1886.","3":"White puts the other pawn on d4 too. Two pawns stand side by side in the centre, and White has plenty of space. Black will try to break them up. In the spring of this year, 1910, Halley's Comet came close to the Earth, and people said the Earth would pass through its long tail. Some, afraid of poisonous gas in the tail, even rushed to buy 'anti-comet pills'.","4":"As planned, d5. The pawn challenges e4: \"I can take you.\" White must decide whether to guard it, push it or trade it.","5":"White brings out a knight to guard e4. It develops a piece and defends at the same time, so no time is wasted. Later, Réti became a leader of the new 'hypermodern' idea – controlling the centre from a distance with pieces instead of filling it with pawns – and his name lives on in the Réti Opening, which starts 1.Nf3. But in this game he still plays in the old way, lining up pawns on e4 and d4.","6":"Black takes the pawn on e4. By trading centre pawns, Black makes White's centre smaller.","7":"White takes back with the knight, which now stands in the middle of the board. A central knight reaches many squares and is a strong piece. Up to here, this is the main line of the Caro-Kann, still the most played today.","8":"Black brings out a knight to face the one on e4. Bishop to f5 is often played here, but Black invites a knight trade. Tartakower later became famous for his many witty sayings, such as 'The winner of the game is the player who makes the next-to-last mistake.'","9":"The queen comes out early to d3 to guard the knight on e4. Usually you should not bring the queen out too soon, but White has a plan. Remember that this queen stands on the d-file, in front of Black's king. Normally White trades knights here, so 5.Qd3 is an unusual move.","10":"Black plays e5, offering a pawn to open lines for the bishop and queen. It is an active move that starts a fight in the centre. However, opening the centre before castling also made it a risky move for Black's king. In fact, records show that a queen sacrifice very similar to the one coming up had already been played before this game.","11":"White takes the pawn on e5. White accepts the gift, but this also gives Black's queen more room to move.","12":"Check! Black's queen on a5 aims diagonally at the king on e1. At the same time it plans to win back the e5 pawn: two jobs in one move. White must block.","13":"White blocks the check with the bishop on d2. The queen could take the bishop, but White's queen guards it well.","14":"Black wins the pawn back, so material is equal again. And this queen looks straight down the e-file at the knight on e4 and the king behind it. If the knight moves, the king is in check, so the knight is stuck. Black seems about to win it.","15":"White castles queenside. The king leaves the e-file, so the knight is free again. At the same time a rook comes to d1: the rook on d1, the queen on d3 and the square d8 next to Black's king are all on one line. This is White's trap.","16":"Black takes the knight on e4 and seems to win a piece. But Black's king is still on e8 and has not castled. White's pieces are lined up on the d-file, and the black king is unprotected. This move, which loses the game, is the turning point.","17":"Queen to d8, check! White gives away the strongest piece for nothing. The only black piece that can take it is the king. By letting it be taken, White drags the king onto the dangerous square d8.","18":"Black must take with the king; there is no other way to stop the check. The king is pulled out to d8, with the rook on d1 waiting behind.","19":"The bishop moves from d2 to g5 with check, and at the same time it opens the way for the rook on d1, which checks along the d-file too. Two pieces checking at once is a double check. You cannot stop it by taking one piece or by blocking, so the king must run.","20":"The king runs to c7. If it went to e8 instead, the rook would come to d8 and mate. Wherever the king goes, White has a finishing move ready.","21":"Bishop to d8: checkmate on the diagonal against the king on c7! The bishop is guarded by the rook on d1, so the king cannot take it. Squares like b8, c8, b7 and c6 are blocked by Black's own pieces, and the rook watches d6 and d7. In just eleven moves, White gave up the queen and won with two small pieces. The game shows how dangerous it is to grab material before castling. Some records say Black resigned one move earlier."}},"immortal":{"t":"The Immortal Game","w":"Adolf Anderssen","b":"Lionel Kieseritzky","ev":"An informal game during a break in the London 1851 tournament","d":"1851","pl":"United Kingdom (London)","lead":"London, during the first international chess tournament: a game played in a break. White gives up a bishop, both rooks and finally the queen, and mates with the three small pieces left. Black is said to have resigned before the end; the last few moves may be the finish White showed.","c":{"0":"London, June 1851. That year the world’s first international chess tournament was being held in London. White is Adolf Anderssen, a mathematics teacher from Germany; Black is Lionel Kieseritzky, who taught chess at the Café de la Régence in Paris. This was not a tournament game but a relaxed game played during a break. Kieseritzky printed the moves in his own magazine and spread them, and the game later came to be called “the Immortal Game”. Let’s watch a battle typical of its time, full of pieces thrown into the attack. Musowl will be your guide. In May of that year, a huge building of iron and glass, the 'Crystal Palace', stood in London's Hyde Park, and the Great Exhibition, gathering new inventions and goods from all over the world, had opened. Chess players thought, 'Let's have a world event for chess too', and this international tournament was held at Staunton's call.","1":"White pushes the pawn in front of the king two squares. It now watches the central squares d5 and f5, and the paths for the bishop and queen are open. Then as now, it is the most popular first move. Anderssen was born in what is now Wrocław in Poland (then Breslau, in Germany). His father taught him chess when he was nine. In everyday life he taught mathematics at a school, and he was also known as a composer of chess puzzles (problems). In this tournament he beat Kieseritzky in the first round and went on to win the whole event.","2":"Black also plays e5. The pawns meet head-on and Black does not give up the centre. Black’s bishop and queen now have open paths too. Kieseritzky was born in Tartu, in today's Estonia. He was originally a mathematics teacher too, but he moved to Paris and made his living from chess, giving lessons or playing games for five francs an hour. So this was a game between two maths teachers!","3":"f4! White offers a pawn for free: the “King’s Gambit”. If Black takes it, Black’s e5 pawn leaves the centre and White can more easily push a pawn to d4 as well. In this era, plans like this that rush into attack were hugely popular. The weak point is that the space in front of White’s king is opened up. The King's Gambit was one of the most popular openings of the 19th century. Today it is seen as a somewhat risky choice and is rarely played in serious games between top players.","4":"Black accepts the pawn. The pawn on f4 blocks the road of White’s bishop on c1 and also watches the squares e3 and g3. Black is a pawn ahead. The name 'Immortal Game' was given in 1855 by the Austrian player Ernst Falkbeer.","5":"White brings the bishop out to c4. The square f7 next to Black’s king is weak, guarded only by the king. White aims at it so an attack can start at any moment. Playing 3.Bc4 here instead of 3.Nf3 is the form of the King's Gambit called the 'Bishop's Gambit'.","6":"Check! Because White pushed the f-pawn, a diagonal road from h4 to e1 was open. If White blocks with g3, the pawn on f4 simply takes it. White has to move the king.","7":"The king steps aside to f1. Now White can no longer castle and cannot tuck the king away safely. On the other hand, Black’s queen has come out early, so White can now chase it while bringing out pieces. Even though he lost, Kieseritzky sent the moves to his friends in Paris and printed them in La Régence, the magazine he edited. He cared more about a wonderful game than about who won.","8":"Black offers a pawn for free too. With b5 Black lures the c4 bishop away from the diagonal that aims at f7. This move was later named the “Bryan Countergambit”, and it was an attacking move Kieseritzky liked to use. Today, though, most players think this 4...b5 is not a sound move. It is lively, but it does not give enough in return for the pawn.","9":"White takes the pawn. The bishop has left c4, so it no longer aims at f7. In return White has won a pawn back, and the count is even again.","10":"Black’s knight goes to f6. It attacks the e4 pawn and also helps guard the king, a fine square. The race to bring out pieces has begun.","11":"White’s knight comes out to f3 as well. It develops a piece and threatens the queen on h4 at the same time, a very satisfying move. The queen has to run, so White has effectively gained a move.","12":"The queen drops back to h6. From here it watches the h-file and sees the rook on h1 behind White’s h2 pawn. It is still a safe square.","13":"With d3, White firmly guards the e4 pawn that was under attack. At the same time the road for the c1 bishop opens. A calm move that wastes nothing.","14":"Black’s knight goes to h5. It guards the f4 pawn and plans to jump to g3. A knight jump to g3 would give check and hit the h1 rook; if White takes with the h2 pawn, the h-file opens and the queen on h6 can capture the h1 rook. A rather sharp plan.","15":"White’s knight goes to h4. This knight blocks the h-file, so even if Black’s knight jumps to g3 the h1 rook can no longer be taken. It also prepares to jump to f5 and threaten the queen on h6.","16":"Black’s queen goes to g5, attacking the knight on h4. White has to decide whether to defend the knight or move it somewhere.","17":"The knight jumps to f5. Protected by the e4 pawn, it is a very strong post. From here it attacks the g7 pawn and keeps an eye on the squares h6 and d6. White’s pieces are closing in on Black’s king.","18":"With c6 Black tries to chase away the bishop on b5. If the bishop runs, Black can grow the centre with pawns. A normal, sensible move.","19":"But White does not save the bishop and plays g4!, threatening the knight on h5. White is willing to lose a bishop in order to chase Black’s pieces around and gain time for the attack: a bold idea. The g4 pawn is protected diagonally by the queen on d1. Later analysis judges this 10.g4 to be a bit too much. Even so, in the actual game its energy put Black under more and more pressure.","20":"The knight, attacked on h5, goes back to f6. Black decides that taking White’s bishop can wait. The c6 pawn is still attacking the bishop on b5.","21":"The rook goes to g1. It guards the g4 pawn and gets ready to fight on the g-file. The bishop on b5 is still left to be taken. White believes that chasing Black’s queen matters more than one bishop. This 11.Rg1 is still highly praised as a purposeful sacrifice, deliberately letting the bishop be taken.","22":"Black takes the bishop. In material, Black is now a whole bishop ahead, a big lead. But apart from the queen and a knight, Black’s pieces are still on their starting squares. White’s attack starts from here. The strong German player Hübner analysed that taking the bishop here was Black's big mistake, and that pushing the pawn to h5 instead would have been better.","23":"h4! White chases Black’s queen on g5 with a pawn. Just by pushing pawns, White keeps shrinking the places where Black’s queen can stand.","24":"The queen escapes to g6. It still has some squares, but it is starting to be surrounded by White’s pawns and knight.","25":"With h5 White chases the queen once more. Every time a White pawn advances, Black’s queen can only retreat.","26":"The queen goes back to g5. The knight on f5 watches h6 and the other squares are blocked, so this is almost the only place left. Black’s queen is slowly being cornered.","27":"White’s queen goes to f3. Next, if the c1 bishop takes the f4 pawn, the queen on g5 will be attacked and have almost nowhere to run. It is a move that sets a trap to catch the queen.","28":"Black brings the knight back to g8 to free the escape square f6 for the queen. A knight that was developed returning to its starting square is a sign that Black is in trouble. In the race to bring out pieces, White is far ahead.","29":"The bishop captures the f4 pawn, winning a pawn back while chasing the queen on g5. White’s bishop has finally joined the fight as well.","30":"The queen escapes to the square it prepared, f6. From here it looks diagonally all the way to the b2 pawn and can also aim at the a1 rook behind it.","31":"White’s knight goes to c3. The knight on c3 also blocks the diagonal from the f6 queen to b2. Next it plans to jump to d5 and threaten the queen on f6.","32":"Black brings the bishop out to c5 and attacks the rook on g1. At last Black has brought out a bishop and joined the attack. Should Black go grab pieces, or defend the king? It was an important moment for Black.","33":"The knight goes to d5! It threatens the f6 queen, and there is also a jump to c7 that would attack the king and rook at once. But because the knight left c3, the road from Black’s queen to b2 is open again. White does not try to protect either the g1 rook or the b2 pawn. White is ready to let pieces be taken in order to attack Black’s king.","34":"Black’s queen takes the b2 pawn and now aims at the a1 rook. The idea is to grab lots of pieces and win on numbers. But the queen is getting farther and farther away from its own king.","35":"The bishop goes to d6! Now White leaves both rooks to be taken. In return the bishop controls f8 and e7, blocking the escape routes of Black’s king. White chose not the piece count but weaving a net to catch Black’s king. This is the turning point of the game.","36":"Black takes the rook on g1. Black is now ahead by a bishop and a rook. However, later studies say another move was better here, and that Black had a stronger defence.","37":"e5! A quiet pawn move, but it is one of the deciding blows. This pawn blocks the diagonal from the b2 queen to g7 and also controls f6. Black’s queen can no longer come back to help the king, and the g8 knight cannot come out to f6. White now threatens to take on g7 with the knight, giving check, and then mate with the bishop on c7.","38":"Black takes the other rook too, with check. The whole first rank from a1 to f1 was open. Black has taken both rooks and is far ahead in material.","39":"The king steps to e2. White’s only big piece left is the queen, plus two knights and a bishop. Even so, around Black’s king White has far more pieces than Black.","40":"Black brings the knight out to a6 to guard c7. It is meant to stop the mate where the knight takes g7 with check, the king runs to d8, and the bishop finishes on c7. It is said that Kieseritzky resigned around here, and that the rest of the moves were shown by White.","41":"The knight takes the g7 pawn with check! The knight on g7 attacks the king on e8. The bishop on d6 controls e7 and f8, so the king has few places to go.","42":"The king has to run to d8. It can no longer stay on e8 or go to e7 or f8; d8 was the only square left.","43":"White sacrifices the queen on f6 with check! Even the last big piece is given away for free. It checks diagonally from f6 through e7 to d8, and Black has no choice but to take this queen.","44":"Black takes the queen with the g8 knight. The king has nowhere to run, so there is no other move. But this knight moving away completes the mating pattern.","45":"The bishop goes to e7. Checkmate! The knight on d5 controls e7 and c7, the knight on g7 controls e8, and Black’s own pieces block c8 and d7. White gave up a bishop, two rooks and even the queen, and won with just three small pieces. Black still has a queen and rooks to spare, but none of them arrive in time. It teaches us that how hard your pieces are working matters more than how many you have, and it is one of the most famous games in the history of chess. The name is said to have been given a few years later by the Austrian master Falkbeer."}},"mimiaka":{"t":"The Ear-reddening Game","w":"Honinbo Shusaku","b":"Inoue Gennan Inseki","ev":"A game in Osaka (played over three days)","d":"1846","pl":"Japan (Osaka)","lead":"Seventeen-year-old Shusaku (4-dan) took on Gennan (8-dan), one of the strongest players of the day. When Shusaku played move 127, Gennan’s ears are said to have turned red — hence the name. There was no komi then; Black won by 2 points.","c":{"0":"Osaka, 1846. Black is 17-year-old Shusaku (4-dan); White is Inoue Gennan Inseki (8-dan), about 48, one of the top players of the day who had once fought Honinbo Jowa for the post of Meijin Godokoro. The day before, Shusaku had started with two handicap stones, as their ranks required, but Gennan soon saw his strength and stopped that game; this one was played with Shusaku simply taking Black. There was no komi in those days, so only the territory on the board counts. Let us follow this three-day game move by move. Go in the Edo period was run by four “houses”: Honinbo, Inoue, Yasui and Hayashi. Each house was paid by the shogun’s government to play and teach go. Shusaku belonged to the Honinbo house and Gennan to the Inoue house, so this game also carried the pride of two houses. In the same year 1846, the American commodore Biddle sailed two warships into Uraga Bay to ask for trade, and the shogunate said no, seven years before Perry came. In Europe, the planet Neptune was discovered about two weeks after this game.","1":"Black 1 is on the 3-4 point at top right (R16). It balances corner territory and room to extend along the sides. Shusaku liked 3-4 points when he had Black. Shusaku was born in 1829 on Innoshima, an island in the Seto Inland Sea (now part of Onomichi, Hiroshima). His father served the Asano family, lords of Mihara Castle. He was very strong at go from early childhood, and in 1837, at about eight, he went to Edo to become a pupil of Honinbo Jowa.","2":"White 2 takes the 3-4 point at top left (D17). In the opening the corners come first, because territory is easiest to make there. The two sides share out the empty corners.","3":"Black 3 is the 3-4 point at bottom right (Q3). It is lined up with R16 so that the right side between the two corners can later become a big Black area.","4":"White 4 (P17) approaches Black’s top-right corner. If Black got one more move there, the corner would become very solid, so White acts first. Black can answer, or play another big point instead.","5":"Black 5 does not answer but takes the 3-4 point at bottom left (C4). Three 3-4 points at moves 1, 3 and 5 is a pattern often seen in Shusaku’s games with Black. Dealing with White’s P17 can wait. This pattern of Black 1, 3 and 5 later came to be called the “Shusaku opening”. Shusaku did not invent it, but he won so many games with it that it took his name. It is the most famous opening for Black from the days without komi.","6":"White 6 (C14) is on the left side. Approaching Black’s bottom-left corner would be normal, but Gennan builds on the left side together with D17. It is a slightly unusual move that blocks Black’s C4 from spreading up the left side. Gennan was born in 1798. He longed to become Meijin Godokoro, the highest post in go, and fought Honinbo Jowa over it for years. When Jowa retired in 1839 he tried again, but he lost the first game of a challenge match against Shuwa of the Honinbo house and gave up. So this is also a great player who never became Meijin testing the young hope of the Honinbo house.","7":"Black 7 (E4) closes the bottom-left corner with a second stone. The corner is now almost surely Black’s. By defending before White could approach, Black also limits what White 6 can do on the left.","8":"White 8 (R5) approaches Black’s Q3. With black 3-4 points at both ends of the right side, White needs a foothold there. A long fight in the bottom right begins.","9":"Black 9 (Q15) defends the top right. Placed below White’s P17, it strengthens the corner and pushes P17 towards the narrow top edge. Black wants to keep the right side on its side. Black 9 is the famous move later called “Shusaku’s kosumi” (a diagonal step). Instead of answering P17 directly, Black steps diagonally to guard the corner and the right side. Shusaku is said to have remarked that as long as the board stays the same size, this kosumi will never be a bad move. In the komi era it was sometimes called too slow, but in recent years go AI has also rated it highly, and it has been looked at again.","10":"White 10 (O4) is the taisha: instead of a simple move, White caps Black’s stones diagonally from a little distance. It has so many variations that an old saying speaks of “the taisha’s hundred changes”, and it was Gennan’s speciality. How will 17-year-old Shusaku handle his opponent’s favourite weapon? Study of the taisha began around the 1820s and flourished in the Tenpo era (1830s–40s). The Inoue house is said to have kept secret research on it: eleven years earlier, in the “Blood-vomiting Game”, Gennan’s pupil Akaboshi Intetsu played an Inoue new move inside the taisha. The new line Gennan plays in this game is judged an even result and is still a joseki today.","11":"With 11 (Q5) Black attaches to White’s R5 and accepts the fight. A quiet defence was possible, but Shusaku chooses the line with many variations. A reading contest follows.","12":"White 12 (Q4) wedges between Black’s stones, trying to separate Q3 from Q5 and weaken one of them.","15":"With 13 (P4) and 15 (P3), Black firmly connects the lower stones. White 14 (R4) dug into the corner, so the corner territory is now contested. Black prefers to stay connected and strong even if it gives up a little of the corner. This is still the path of the taisha joseki that players of the day had studied hard. Both sides know the shapes well, so even this tricky sequence goes along without hesitation.","17":"With White 16 (P5) and Black 17 (Q6), the stones grow upwards. Black pokes its head out towards the right side, while White presses from above to keep Black in.","19":"White 18 (R3) adds a stone in the corner to make life, and Black 19 (O3) holds White back on the bottom side. Black’s plan: let White have the corner, but become strong outside along the bottom and right.","20":"White 20 (P6) caps Black’s Q6 from above, trying to shut Black low on the right side. If Black cannot get out upwards, its right-side stones will only just live, with little to gain. Three years after this game, from 1849, Shusaku took part in the castle games (oshirogo), the official games played at Edo Castle, and left a record of 19 wins in 19 games. But in 1862, when cholera spread through Edo, he nursed the sick in the Honinbo house, caught it himself and died at 34.","21":"With 21 (Q7) and 23 (Q8), Black crawls up the right side looking for a way out, while White 22 (P7) presses alongside. Black’s long chain on the third line is low and makes little territory, but it is clearly alive.","24":"White 24 (N4) seals Black’s O3–P3 stones on the bottom from outside. Black’s stones now face two directions, the right side and the bottom, and protecting both at once is getting hard.","25":"Black 25 (N3) extends one step along the bottom. Later study says another move here, one using a ladder, would have been better, so this is called Black’s small mistake in the game. Black’s bottom stones stay low and easy to surround, which gives White a chance to strike back. Even Shusaku could go astray inside his opponent’s favourite pattern. With this move, Black stepped a little off the well-studied path of the taisha joseki.","26":"White 26 (P8) caps Black’s right-side stones from above. Black cannot get out upwards on the right, and is in danger of being shut in on the bottom too.","28":"White 28 (M3) attaches directly to Black’s N3 — a severe move. It stops Black from spreading along the bottom and takes away living space, while making Black add more stones. Gennan punished Black’s small slip at 25 at once.","30":"After Black 29 (M2), White 30 (L2) goes round underneath as well. Black’s bottom stones can get out neither outwards nor sideways. To live they must connect to the bottom-right corner, and a capturing fight over the corner begins.","31":"Black 31 (M4) is an important move. Instead of rushing into the corner fight, Black first creates a point White must answer. It prepares for the ko that is sure to come.","33":"With 33 (R2) the capturing race in the bottom-right corner begins. White 32 (L3) made White safe on the bottom, so Black must go for White’s corner stones. If Black captures them, its bottom stones live; if not, they are in danger.","35":"White 34 (S2) and Black 35 (S1): fine moves on the first and second lines in the corner. Each side reduces the other’s liberties (empty points around a group), trying to capture first.","36":"White 36 (N2). Before starting the ko, White first plays one move around Black’s bottom stones. Even if Black wins the ko, it will gain a little less — a well-timed move.","40":"White 40 (R1) captures a stone, and at last it is a ko. In a ko the side that was just captured may not retake at once. Black must first play a threat elsewhere that White has to answer, and only then retake. The fight is decided by who has more threats.","41":"Black 41 (O5) is the first ko threat. Ignoring it would put White’s bottom-right stones in trouble, so White answers with 42 (N5), and Black retakes with 43 (R2). It costs a little, but it is necessary here.","44":"White 44 (S7) is White’s ko threat. Played beside Black’s right-side stones, it makes Black answer at 45 (S8), and White retakes at 46 (R1). Each threat settles a little more of the board.","47":"Black 47 (M5), White 48 (N6), Black 49 (R2): another threat, another retake. Around White’s bottom stones, Black is slowly adding stones on the outside.","50":"White 50 (R7) cuts into Black’s right-side stones — a big ko threat. Normally Black would answer, but Black puts the ko first. It becomes a large trade: White gains on the right side, Black gets the bottom-right corner.","51":"Black 51 (P2) captures a stone and wins the ko. The bottom-right corner becomes living Black territory, and the bottom stones are saved. In return White continues with 52 (R8) to split Black’s right-side stones in two.","53":"Black 53 (R10) is an inventive move few would find. It comes to the aid of the upper part of Black’s split right-side stones from beyond White’s stones. Had Black answered simply, White would have taken a big share of the right side.","56":"White 54 (S10), Black 55 (S11), White 56 (S9): White makes life along the right edge, while Black holds it from above and keeps the upper half of the right side.","59":"After Black 57 (M6) and White 58 (N7), Black 59 (R11) connects the right-side stones firmly. Black’s right side is now safe from top to bottom. The big trade so far looks roughly even.","60":"White 60 (S3) is a big move. It reduces Black’s corner territory while strengthening White’s stones, and it also lets White play 62 (M1) next as a move Black must answer.","63":"White 62 (M1) captures a stone and Black 63 (O1) connects: the long bottom-right fight is over for now. White has territory on the bottom; Black has a thick wall on the right. Partly because of Black 25, White is thought to have done well so far. But Shusaku’s thick wall will later work hard in the centre.","65":"White 64 (G3) extends to the left part of the bottom to make White’s bottom area even bigger. Black 65 (G5) caps it from above so White’s territory cannot grow upwards. The pattern becomes clear: White takes territory, Black takes outside strength.","66":"White 66 (C9) is on the left side. Together with White 6 (C14), it builds a White framework there too. White is gathering territory along the bottom and the left.","67":"Black 67 (L17) is on the top side. With the thick wall behind it, Black aims for a large framework — a wide area likely to become territory — from the top towards the centre. It also pincers and attacks White’s P17.","69":"White 68 (J17) goes to the left of L17 to split Black’s top framework. Black 69 (Q17) attaches to White’s P17, attacking it while strengthening the corner.","70":"White 70 (O10) goes between Black’s right-side wall and the top framework. It stops Black’s framework from joining up through the centre and tries to link with White’s stones on the right.","72":"White 72 (P13) is a superb move. Placed just above Black’s wall, it makes a White home right in the middle of Black’s framework. Even if Black chases it, White can escape lightly. Black’s hopes for the top shrink with this one move.","73":"Black 73 (P12) blocks below P13 so it cannot connect to White’s right-side stones. White 74 (Q18) then goes into the top-right corner to trim Black’s corner territory as well.","77":"After Black 75 (R18) defends the corner, White 76 (O13) and Black 77 (N11) follow. Black caps White’s centre stones, trying to gain territory while attacking. From here to around move 100, Gennan does not make a single slack move and keeps dodging Black’s attacks.","79":"White 78 (N10) connects White’s centre stones, and Black 79 (O16) firms up the top. Black did not gain much by attacking, but holds the top territory securely.","80":"With 80 (Q12) and 82 (Q13), White links stones right beside Black’s wall. Now that White has a sound shape inside Black’s framework, Black’s top-right territory will be smaller than hoped.","83":"Black 83 (H3) goes into White’s bottom territory, which had grown too large. White 84 (H2) and Black 85 (J2) follow: a close fight over whether Black can live inside or escape outside.","87":"White 86 (J3), Black 87 (H4), White 88 (K2): Black links the invading stones to G5 above and brings them out. White’s bottom territory is trimmed, and Black adds to its central thickness.","89":"Black 89 (F17) approaches White’s D17 at top left, putting a black stone on the left of the top side and stopping White from spreading along it. White 90 (M17) and 92 (N17) try to make White’s stones live between Black’s on the top.","93":"With 93 (M18) and 95 (N16), Black squeezes White’s top stones from above and below. White can only live low on the first and second lines, and even then makes very little territory.","96":"From White 96 (R14) a sharp little fight starts beside Black’s top-right corner. White splits Black’s wall from the corner to weaken Black’s stones. Black answers at 97 (Q14), and White pushes along the right edge with 98 (R12) and 100 (S12).","101":"With 101 (T12) and 103 (T13), Black descends to the first line at the right edge, narrowing White’s living space. Edge moves look small, but here they decide life and death.","104":"White 104 (Q10) hits a weak spot in Black’s right-side wall. After Black 105 (Q11) and White 106 (P11), Black goes to capture with 107.","107":"Black 107 (P10) captures a white stone. Black’s right-side wall is now solidly connected and safe. White 108 (O11) and 110 (P9) head for the centre to protect White’s stones near the wall.","112":"From White 112 (L18), White’s top stones hurry to make life. With 113 (K18), 115 (K17) and 117 (K19), Black surrounds them so they cannot get out.","118":"White 118 (M19) captures a stone and White’s top group lives. Living inside Black’s top framework cuts Black’s territory a lot. Gennan’s play so far has been excellent.","120":"Black 119 (D18) probes White’s top-left corner and White answers with 120 (C17). Black is not trying to take the corner, but to firm up the left part of the top and create a chance to add territory.","122":"Black 121 (F15) tidies Black’s shape from the top towards the centre. White 122 (C6) plays beside Black’s bottom-left corner to trim its territory bit by bit.","124":"Black 123 (G2), White 124 (J1), Black 125 (F3): the border on the left of the bottom is settled. White captures a stone there and keeps its territory safe.","126":"White 126 (J5). White is now thick on the bottom and ready to push into the centre. With territory on the bottom and left and a living group at the top right, White leads. Most of the professionals watching are said to have thought White was ahead. Black cannot catch up by ordinary play.","127":"This is the famous ear-reddening move, Black 127 (K11). This one stone in the middle of the board is said to work in four directions at once. Upwards it widens Black’s framework; downwards it stops White’s thickness from reaching the centre; to the right it supports Black’s wall; to the left it blocks White’s expansion. One move doing four jobs. From here the game, thought to favour White, slowly starts to tip towards Black.","128":"When move 127 was played, a doctor watching nearby noticed that Gennan’s ears had turned red. He knew little about go, but he is said to have read it as a sign that Gennan was shaken, and guessed that Black would win. While the professionals thought White was better, only the doctor’s eye caught the turning point. White 128 (E18) defends the top-left corner.","131":"After Black 129 (F18) firms up the left of the top, Black 131 (D10) moves into White’s left-side framework. With stone 127 holding the centre, Black can safely invade White’s territory.","136":"With 133 (E10) and 135 (D12), Black’s left-side stones slip between White’s and take shape. White 136 (J10) comes into the centre to weaken the effect of stone 127; White cannot leave the centre to Black.","140":"Black 137 (H7) caps White’s bottom stones, and White 138 (G10) and 140 (F8) try to link White’s stones on the left of the centre. In the centre the stones are tangled, and both sides have weaknesses.","143":"White 142 (E2) slips into Black’s bottom-left corner. Black 143 (D7) and 145 (D6) connect Black’s stones while eyeing both White’s left-side stones and the white invader in the corner.","147":"Black 147 (K10) extends straight down from stone 127, cutting White’s centre stones into left and right. Here the single stone of 127 begins to show its real power.","150":"White 148 (K9), Black 149 (L9), White 150 (J11): White tries to connect its centre stones, Black cuts them off from the bottom. White 150 lands right next to stone 127, pushing into Black’s centre too.","155":"From Black 151 (J9) to Black 155 (K12), Black squeezes White’s centre stones from both sides. Black 155 connects above stone 127, joining Black’s centre stones with the top. Black has taken the initiative in the centre.","160":"After White 156 (C5) and Black 157 (D5), White plays 158 (J12) and 160 (H8). White protects its centre stones while also eyeing a weakness in Black’s bottom-left stones. The game is still close; neither side can relax.","162":"Black 161 (B8) goes underneath White’s left-side stones. White 162 (D13) caps Black’s left-side stones from above to make them suffer.","167":"After strengthening the centre with 163 (G12) and 165 (J13), Black 167 (C2) is an accurate move defending the bottom-left corner. Had Black ignored it, White’s stone at 142 (E2) could have threatened the corner. Black removes the danger first and builds a hard-to-lose position.","169":"White 166 (E17) and 168 (F16) try to cut Black’s stones at the top left. Black answers with 169 (G16) and 171 (G17), connecting the top stones firmly.","175":"Black 175 (G4) captures a white stone. Black’s stones survive inside White’s bottom area, so White’s territory there is smaller than it first looked. The gap is slowly closing.","180":"White 176 (M11), Black 177 (L13), White 178 (J6), Black 179 (M10), White 180 (M13): on the right of the centre too, White puts stones into Black’s framework to reduce it. Black answers while gathering the whole centre into its own territory.","187":"From Black 183 (K13) to 187 (M14), Black presses down on the white stones in the centre and keeps them away from the top. White fights to live there, but Black’s wall is thick and the fight is hard for White.","195":"From White 190 (G14) to 194 (M9), White widens the escape routes of its centre stones. Black 195 (L10) cuts between White’s centre stones to break their shape.","199":"With Black 199 (K7) the centre fight calms down. White’s centre stones manage to live, but Black gained territory along the way and the game now leans towards Black. Next comes the endgame — fixing the borders — where one or two points decide everything.","208":"Black 203 (E19), 205 (F2), White 208 (R19): endgame moves along the edges are played from the biggest down. In the endgame it is important to play moves the opponent must answer first.","212":"With 212 (D19) and 216 (F19), White captures black stones at the top edge, and Black makes up for it elsewhere with 213 (G11) and 217 (G19). Neither side gives an inch.","225":"Black 225 (L6) captures a white stone; Black keeps trimming White’s territory in the middle of the bottom. The last big fight, a ko in the centre, is approaching.","228":"White 228 (N12) captures a stone and creates a ko in the centre. For more than 80 moves the sides will capture and recapture at M13 and N13. Each ko threat causes a small trade somewhere on the board. This long, long ko will decide the game.","236":"White 236 (T8) captures a stone in the middle of the ko. Some later analysts think that ending the ko here instead would have kept the margin smaller. In a long ko, which trades you choose decides the last point or two.","262":"With 262 (T1) and 266 (S4), White captures black stones in the bottom-right corner. Meanwhile Black has taken back elsewhere, and the ko goes on with gains and losses piling up on both sides.","275":"Black 275 (T14) captures two white stones; this trade at the top-right edge favours Black. Adding up the trades during the ko, Black is just keeping its small lead.","299":"Black 299 (J16) captures four white stones at once. White’s threat at the top is gone and Black’s top is safe. Black has come through the last big crisis of the ko.","311":"Black 311 (M13) finally fills the ko. The centre ko that lasted more than 80 moves is over. Through all the trades it caused, Black has kept the lead. Only small endgame moves remain.","321":"From Black 317 (N1) to 321 (M2), the last borders on the bottom are fixed. Both sides keep playing accurately to the end, trying to gain even one more point.","325":"The game ends after 325 moves, and the count shows Black ahead by 2 points. Black stumbled a little in Gennan’s favourite taisha early on, and White was thought to be better up to the middle game. Shusaku turned the tide with the single stone of move 127, came through the long ko and held on to a tiny lead. Gennan is said to have remarked later that Shusaku’s strength was no less than 7-dan. This game made Shusaku’s name, and he later became heir to the Honinbo house. The taisha in the opening, the ear-reddening move 127 and the long final ko — these three are the highlights of this great game."}},"evergreen":{"t":"The Evergreen Game","w":"Adolf Anderssen","b":"Jean Dufresne","ev":"A casual game (not a tournament)","d":"1852","pl":"Germany (probably Berlin)","lead":"Anderssen, of the Immortal Game, played this other masterpiece a year later. Just as Black threatens mate, White quietly moves one rook, then gives up the queen and finishes with a double check. It is called \"evergreen\" because it never fades.","c":{"0":"Germany, 1852. White is Adolf Anderssen, who had won the London tournament the year before and was thought to be the strongest player in the world. Black is his friend Jean Dufresne, who later wrote a chess handbook that many people read. It was a relaxed game, not a tournament game; it is thought to have been played in Berlin, but there is no clear record. It is another great game, born one year after “the Immortal Game”. The name is said to come from the later champion Steinitz, who praised it as “the evergreen in Anderssen’s laurel crown”. Let’s look at it together with Musowl. Steinitz wrote those words in 1879, the year Anderssen died, in a newspaper article remembering him.","1":"White pushes the pawn in front of the king two squares. It now watches d5 and f5 in the centre, and the roads for the bishop and queen are open.","2":"Black also plays e5. It faces White’s pawn in the centre and does not give way. In this same year, 1852, Anderssen also published his second book of chess puzzles (problems). After winning the tournament the year before, he had become the most watched player in Europe.","3":"White’s knight goes to f3. It develops a piece and attacks Black’s e5 pawn. Black has to protect this pawn.","4":"Black brings the knight to c6 to protect the e5 pawn. Defending while developing a piece: a move that wastes nothing.","5":"White’s bishop goes to c4. It aims at f7 next to Black’s king. f7 is a weak square, guarded only by the king.","6":"Black likewise brings the bishop to c5 and aims at White’s f2. Both sides have the same shape; so far it is a very calm start. Up to here this is the 'Italian Game' (Giuoco Piano, meaning 'quiet game'), a beginning recorded from very early times.","7":"b4! White offers a pawn for free: the “Evans Gambit”. It is said to have been invented by a British sea captain named Evans, and it was very popular in this era. If Black takes with the bishop, White pushes pawns to c3 and d4, gaining time while taking control of the centre. William Davies Evans was born in Wales. As a young man he joined the navy, and later he became captain of a ship carrying the mail. He is said to have thought up this gambit in the mid-1820s and made it famous by beating the strong player McDonnell in London. He was also an inventor, who came up with a system of three-coloured lights so that ships would not crash into each other at night.","8":"Black takes the pawn with the bishop. It is a move that accepts what is offered, but because the bishop has moved, White can now chase it and seize the initiative.","9":"With c3 White threatens the bishop on b4. At the same time, it prepares the next move, d4.","10":"The bishop drops back to a5. From a5 it looks diagonally all the way to e1 and eyes the c3 pawn from the side.","11":"d4! White pushes a pawn into the centre. It strikes at Black’s e5 pawn and tries to make the middle of the board White’s. White’s pieces can now come out one after another. Giving up one pawn to gain time and the centre: that is the whole idea of the Evans Gambit.","12":"Black takes the d4 pawn. Black is now two pawns up. But Black has not castled yet, and few of Black’s pieces are out.","13":"White castles. The king is tucked safely into the corner, and the rook can soon join the fight. Even two pawns down, White is far ahead in the speed of developing pieces.","14":"Black plays d3. The pawn steps forward one square and drives a wedge into White’s camp. This pawn becomes a small thorn that gets in the way of White’s pieces. Other moves are also well studied in this position, but Black chose a slightly unusual one. This 7...d3 was a favourite of Dufresne. It stops White's knight from coming out to c3 and makes White spend a move winning the pawn back. Today, though, it is thought to be inferior, and 7...Nge7 and other moves are normally played.","15":"White’s queen goes to b3. Together with the bishop on c4 it aims at f7: a frightening setup. Since only the king guards f7, the threat of taking on f7 with the bishop with check is becoming serious.","16":"Black brings the queen to f6 to guard f7. While protecting f7 the queen also eyes White’s kingside: a hard-working square.","17":"e5! White chases away Black’s queen with a pawn. This pawn also blocks the road of Black’s knight to f6, cramping Black’s pieces.","18":"The queen escapes to g6. From here it aims down the g-file at g2 in front of White’s king, and it also keeps an eye on e4. The e5 pawn looks as if it can be taken, but analysis shows that if Black takes it, a rook comes to e1 with a pin and Black loses material.","19":"The rook goes to e1 and firmly protects the e5 pawn. White’s rook has now joined the fight too. However, recent analysis with computers suggests that Rd1 might have been better here.","20":"Black develops the knight to e7. Next Black would like to castle and make the king safe. The knight went to e7 instead of f6 because the e5 pawn controls f6.","21":"The bishop goes to a3! There is a straight diagonal road from a3 toward f8. This bishop aims at the knight on e7 and also looks at f8 behind it. If the knight moves, the road to f8 opens, so it has become hard for Black to castle on the kingside. A very important move that keeps Black’s king trapped in the middle. Computers also suggest Qd1 or Re3 here, but Ba3 is a clear and powerful move for a human to understand.","22":"With b5 Black offers a pawn and threatens the bishop on c4. It is a counterattack to pull White’s queen away from f7 and shake things up.","23":"White takes the pawn with the queen. The queen protects the bishop on c4 while still aiming near Black’s king.","24":"Black’s rook comes out to b8 and threatens the queen on b5. The rook has started working on the open b-file.","25":"The queen goes to a4. It attacks the bishop on a5, and along the diagonal from a4 to e8 it pins the c6 knight to Black’s king. If this knight moves, the king would be in check, so it cannot move.","26":"The bishop escapes from a5 to b6. It is back on its old diagonal, aiming toward f2.","27":"White develops the knight to d2. The last piece left at home comes out, and next it plans to jump to e4 and approach Black’s king.","28":"Black develops the bishop to b7 to protect the pinned knight on c6. The long diagonal from b7 is blocked by the c6 knight for now, but if the knight moves, the bishop will be able to aim toward f3 and g2.","29":"The knight goes to e4! From the middle of the board it threatens to jump to f6 or d6 with check. Black’s king is still in the centre and in danger.","30":"Black’s queen goes to f5. It aims at both the e4 knight and the f3 knight. The idea is to make the knights step aside and then go after White’s king.","31":"The bishop takes the pawn on d3. The pawn that was Black’s thorn is gone. What’s more, this bishop is looking at the f5 queen from behind the e4 knight: if the knight moves, the bishop will take the queen.","32":"The queen escapes to h5. From here it aims through g4 at the knight on f3. The plan is to take f3 with the queen next and use the g-file too, to mate White’s king.","33":"The knight jumps to f6 with check! It also attacks the queen on h5: a fork. So as not to lose the queen, Black decides to take this knight.","34":"Black takes the knight with the g7 pawn. The g-file is open now, so Black can aim at White’s king with the rook on g8.","35":"White recaptures on f6 with the e5 pawn. This pawn threatens the knight on e7, and since the e-file is open, the rook on e1 now looks through the e7 knight at Black’s king. The f6 pawn also controls the squares g7 and e7.","36":"Black’s rook goes to g8. On the open g-file it aims at g2 in front of White’s king. If the queen takes f3 next, g2 will be attacked by both the queen and the rook, and Black looks set to mate White.","37":"The rook goes to d1! Black has a mating threat, yet White quietly moves a rook. Between d1 and d7 stands only the bishop on d3. If the bishop moves, the rook will look straight at the square next to Black’s king. It is a deeply calculated move that aims to finish first with a series of checks, and it is the most famous move of this game. However, later studies say there were other moves for both White and Black, and it is still being examined today.","38":"Black’s queen takes the knight on f3. If it takes g2 next, it is mate together with the rook. White now has to win by giving check after check.","39":"The rook takes the knight on e7 with check! Even giving up a piece, White keeps chasing Black’s king.","40":"Black recaptures with the c6 knight. But because the knight left c6, the diagonal from the a4 queen to d7 is now open. It is said that even if Black moved the king instead of taking with the knight, White’s attack would go on.","41":"White sacrifices the queen on d7 with check! Through the diagonal that has just opened, the queen jumps right in front of Black’s king. This is the decisive blow of the Evergreen.","42":"Black takes the queen with the king. Running to f8 would allow the queen to take the e7 knight with mate, so taking was the only choice.","43":"The bishop moves to f5: double check! The bishop checks the king on d7 from f5, and the rook on d1, uncovered by the bishop, also checks along the d-file. When two pieces give check at once, you cannot defend by blocking or capturing; the king must run.","44":"The king runs to e8. If it ran to c6 instead, the bishop would come to d7 and it would be mate.","45":"The bishop comes to d7 with check. This time it is right next to the king on e8. This bishop is protected by the rook on d1, so the king cannot take it.","46":"The king goes to f8. Running to d8 would also lose: the a3 bishop would take the e7 knight with mate.","47":"The a3 bishop takes the knight on e7: checkmate! The d7 bishop controls e8, the f6 pawn controls e7 and g7, and Black’s own pieces block f7 and g8. The f6 pawn, the bishop that had not moved since it came to a3, and the rook on d1: every piece worked perfectly at the end. Black was aiming at g2 with the queen and rook, but was one move too late. Reading the opponent’s threat and then finishing first with a chain of checks: this game is a model of how to attack."}},"lasker1912":{"t":"The King Hunt","w":"Edward Lasker","b":"George Alan Thomas","ev":"A quick casual game at the City of London Chess Club","d":"1912","pl":"United Kingdom (London)","lead":"A fast, casual game at a London club. White gives up the queen and chases Black's king all the way across the board, from g8 to g1. The opening moves are written in slightly different orders in different records, but the position after move 10 is the same.","c":{"0":"October 1912, the City of London Chess Club. White is Edward Lasker, a young engineer just arrived from Germany; Black is George Alan Thomas, later British champion. It was a fast, casual game. It is famous as a \"king hunt\": Black's king is chased from g8 all the way to g1 at the other end of the board. Lasker later moved to America and became well known for his chess books. According to the records, the date was 29 October. It is described as a 'five-minute' game, but not like today's five-minute blitz: the rule was that neither player could use more than five minutes more than the opponent.","1":"White starts with d4, the queen's pawn. It covers e5 and c5 and opens the way for the bishop on c1. Lasker was born in Kępno, in today's Poland (then Germany). He studied mechanical and electrical engineering at the technical university in Berlin, had only just come to London that year, and joined this club in October. He was a distant relative of the world champion Emanuel Lasker, and people often mix the two up.","2":"Black plays e6, building a solid position and preparing f5 or d5. Thomas was born in Istanbul. He was not only a chess player but also a great badminton player, winning 21 titles in all at Britain's biggest championships (the All England). The Thomas Cup, the world team competition in badminton, is named after him. He also did well in tennis at Wimbledon – an amazing all-round sportsman.","3":"Knight to f3. It covers e5 and helps guard the king's side: the most natural square. In April of this year, 1912, the great liner Titanic struck an iceberg and sank on its first voyage from Britain to America.","4":"Black plays f5, controlling e4, like the Dutch Defence. But moving a pawn in front of the king makes Black's king a little weaker. With the order 1.d4 e6 2.Nf3 f5, the game has entered a Dutch Defence. Books give slightly different move orders for this game, but this order is thought to be the one in Lasker's own score sheet.","5":"The other knight comes to c3, getting ready for e4 to hit the f5 pawn.","6":"Black brings a knight to f6. It controls e4 even more and prepares to castle.","7":"Bishop to g5, aiming at the knight on f6. That knight is an important defender of Black's king, so White wants to remove it early.","8":"Black brings the bishop to e7 to guard the knight, and gets closer to castling.","9":"White trades the bishop for the knight. The knight that guarded Black's king is gone.","10":"Black takes back with the bishop. Now this bishop on f6 takes over the job of guarding the king's side.","11":"e4! White hits the f5 pawn and gains space in the centre. Lines open, and White's pieces can move more freely.","12":"Black takes the pawn on e4. If not, White would push e5 and drive Black's pieces back.","13":"The knight takes back and sits in the centre, eyeing the bishop on f6 and the squares g5 and d6. Two years later, in 1914, Lasker moved to America and worked as an engineer. In the 1920s he invented a breast pump, a machine that helped save the lives of babies born too early.","14":"Black plays b6, preparing to bring the bishop to b7 to aim down the long diagonal at the knight on e4.","15":"Knight to e5, a strong central square that also eyes g4 and f7. Both white knights are now close to Black's king's side. Lasker also loved Go and is known as one of the people who spread Go in America. In 1934 he wrote a book on Go and Gomoku, and he was later given an award from Japan for promoting Go.","16":"Black castles. The king is on g8. But with the f-pawn gone, there are many gaps around the king. After this game, Thomas went on to become British chess champion in 1923 and 1934.","17":"Bishop to d3. For now its own knight on e4 is in the way, but behind it lies the pawn on h7. The moment the knight on e4 moves, this bishop will aim straight at h7.","18":"Black's bishop goes to b7 and aims down the long diagonal at the knight on e4, making White's pieces less free.","19":"Queen to h5, aiming at the pawn on h7, which only Black's king defends. White's queen, bishop and knights all point at Black's king.","20":"Black puts the queen on e7. It guards the bishop on f6 and gets ready to go to c5, but Black misses White's idea. Moves like h6 or taking the knight on e5 were needed. This is the turning point.","21":"The queen is given up on h7, with check! White hands over the strongest piece for nothing. Lasker saw the whole path of chasing the king to mate.","22":"Black must take with the king. The king is pulled out to h7.","23":"The knight takes the bishop on f6 with check. At the same time e4 is empty, so the bishop on d3 also checks the king on h7: a double check. It cannot be blocked, so the king must run.","24":"The king goes to h6. On h8, the other knight would jump to g6 and mate. The king is pushed further and further forward.","25":"The other knight jumps from e5 to g4 with check. It attacks the king on h6 and drives it further forward.","26":"The king goes to g5, leaving its own camp and walking toward White's side.","27":"Check with the h-pawn! Even the pawns join the hunt. The king has to keep moving forward.","28":"The king goes to f4. It has crossed the middle of the board and is entering White's camp.","29":"Check again with the g-pawn. Two pawns now block the way back for the king.","30":"The king goes to f3, right in the middle of White's pieces.","31":"The bishop drops back from d3 to e2 with check. The king is pushed even deeper.","32":"The king goes to g2. White's own king on e1 is not far away.","33":"Rook to h2, check. At last the rook joins in.","34":"The king finally reaches g1. It started on g8 and has walked to the first row at the other end of the board.","35":"White's king steps from e1 to d2. This opens the way for the rook on a1, which now checks the king on g1 along the first row: checkmate! Castling queenside would also have mated, but Lasker moved the king. In a quick game, after giving up the queen, White chased the king for fourteen moves into the deepest corner of White's own camp. A very rare game."}},"toketsu":{"t":"The Blood-vomiting Game","w":"Akaboshi Intetsu","b":"Honinbo Jowa","ev":"Played at the residence of Lord Matsudaira of Suo (over four days)","d":"1835","pl":"Japan (Edo)","lead":"A game born of the struggle for Meijin Godokoro, the highest post in Japanese go. Inoue Gennan Inseki sent his pupil Akaboshi Intetsu to face the Meijin, Jowa. After four days of hard fighting Intetsu lost, and he is said to have coughed up blood at the board.","c":{"0":"Edo, 1835. This game was played at a go gathering in the residence of Lord Matsudaira of Suo, a senior minister. Black is Akaboshi Intetsu (7-dan) of the Inoue house; White is Honinbo Jowa, holder of Meijin Godokoro, the highest post in go, which he had won in a struggle with Inoue Gennan Inseki. Unhappy with that, Gennan sent his best pupil, Intetsu, planning to argue that a Meijin who lost to a 7-dan did not deserve the title. The game lasted four days and left one of the most famous — and saddest — stories in go history. In this year 1835, Halley’s Comet, which returns about every 76 years, was passing near the Earth, and that autumn the writer Mark Twain was born in America. In the go world, it was a time when the four houses — Honinbo, Inoue, Yasui and Hayashi — fought over the single post of Meijin Godokoro.","1":"Black 1 on the 3-4 point at bottom left (C4). Intetsu starts calmly, showing he will play his own game even against the Meijin. Intetsu was born in 1810 in Kikuchi, in Higo (now Kumamoto). At twelve he went to Edo and joined the Inoue house, and from about fifteen he was taught by Gennan. In 1833 he wrote a book of life-and-death problems, “Genran”, and in 1834 he reached 7-dan: the great young hope of the Inoue house.","2":"White 2 (Q3), Black 3 (R16), White 4 (E16): one stone goes into each corner. White 4 is on the high 4-5 point, valuing outward spread over corner territory. Jowa, born in 1787, was the 12th Honinbo and became Meijin Godokoro in 1831. His go was tremendously powerful, and he is called the “Later Saint”, beside the great master Dosaku, the “Earlier Saint”. For a long time people said he outwitted Gennan to become Meijin, but documents found in recent years have led some to say that story should be looked at again.","5":"Black 5 (P17) encloses the top-right corner with a second stone. Black makes the top right solid to prepare for the fights ahead.","6":"White 6 (E3) already approaches Black’s bottom-left corner. Jowa is aggressive from the start and gives Black no time to settle quietly. E3 is the “small knight’s approach” to a 3-4 point, one of the most common approaches from the Edo period to today.","8":"Black 7 (R5) approaches White’s Q3, and White 8 (R9) pincers it from above. White wants to make Black’s stone weak on the right side and turn it into a target. From R5 to R9 there are three empty points between, so White 8 is a “three-space pincer”.","9":"Black 9 (R11) pincers White 8 back from above. With Black’s top-right corner solid, Black can turn the tables and attack White on the right side.","10":"White 10 (P4) defends the bottom-right corner, leaving the right-side fight for later and making its own corner solid first.","11":"Black 11 (J3) goes to the middle of the bottom side, between White’s E3 and P4, so that White cannot take a big area there. From E3 to J3 there are also three empty points, so Black 11 is also a “three-space pincer” against E3: Black answers the approach to its corner by pinching it.","12":"White 12 (D6) presses down on Black’s bottom-left corner from above. Together with E3, it tries to wrap up Black’s C4. The biggest fight of the game begins here. D6 is a large knight’s move from White’s E3. This is the taisha, a move played when a small knight’s approach is pinched from three spaces away, and this game is known as an example of a taisha arising from a three-space pincer. The taisha was a shape the Inoue house had studied in secret, so Jowa walked straight into his opponents’ specialty. Eleven years later, in the “Ear-reddening Game”, Gennan would play the taisha too.","13":"Black 13 (E4) attaches right between White’s stones and splits them before Black can be surrounded. Black accepts the fight head-on.","16":"White 14 (D4), Black 15 (D5), White 16 (D3): White digs under Black’s corner while Black grows upwards. With stones cut on both sides, each has weak groups — a difficult fight. This is inside the taisha joseki, said to have “a hundred variations”. Which path each side picks here changes the whole fight that follows.","19":"Black 17 (C5), White 18 (E5), Black 19 (F4): Black holds White’s E5 from below and keeps White’s stones split. White’s bottom-left stones must either live in the corner or escape outward.","20":"White 20 (C3) slips under Black’s corner, eating its territory while trying to live there. Black 21 (B3) and White 22 (B2) continue the close exchange inside the corner.","22":"White 22 (B2) drops to the second line in the corner to shape White’s life there. Black’s corner is now smaller, but Black’s outside stones are stronger.","23":"Black 23 (C7) goes up the left side and holds White’s upper stones (like D6) from below. White 24 (G2) extends along the bottom, giving the corner stones a way to connect and live.","26":"Black 25 (H4) caps White’s bottom stones, and White 26 (E6) strengthens the upper white stones. The lower white group wants territory, the upper one an exit to the centre — and Black is eyeing both.","27":"Black 27 (B4) blocks above White’s corner stones, narrowing their living space to prepare later attacks.","28":"White 28 (C9) reaches far along the left side, trying to give the upper white stones a base to live on. It is often called an over-extension — a fierce move very typical of Jowa. Black now sets out to punish it.","31":"Black 29 (D8), White 30 (D9), Black 31 (E8): Black cuts in between White’s C9 and the upper white stones, splitting White in two. White’s C9–D9 on the left and D6–E6 in the centre are now separated.","33":"Black 33 (C2). This is said to be a new line that the Inoue house had studied in secret. It squeezes White’s corner stones and at the same time attacks White’s floating stones in the centre — two aims at once. A “secret move” prepared to defeat the Meijin, and from here Black’s advantage becomes clear.","36":"White 34 (D2), Black 35 (B1), White 36 (F3): White hurries to connect its bottom stones and make life. Meanwhile White’s centre stones get no help and become Black’s target.","38":"White 38 (A2) is a first-line move that secures eye space for White’s corner stones. Once the lower white group is settled, White can focus on the upper fight.","40":"From Black 37 (G5) to White 40 (G7), White’s centre stones run upwards while Black chases closely alongside, strengthening its own stones as it goes.","42":"Black 41 (H7), White 42 (G8): White runs upwards into the centre and Black follows from outside. The further White goes up, the firmer Black’s bottom and left become.","43":"Black 43 (E9). White’s C9–D9 on the left are surrounded and in trouble, and White’s centre stones are not yet alive either. Up to here Intetsu has played splendidly and is thought to have taken a clear lead. Against the Meijin, the Inoue plan seemed to be working.","45":"White 44 (H8) runs the centre stones upwards, and Black 45 (D11) extends up the left side, blocking above C9–D9. White’s left-side stones are losing their way out both upwards and towards the centre.","48":"Black 47 (H5), White 48 (F5): White tries to link its centre and bottom stones, while Black tries to pry them apart. For White, every connection counts.","50":"Black 49 (C12) makes the left side even stronger, and White 50 (C16) turns to the top-left corner. White leaves the hard bottom-left fight for now to gain elsewhere.","53":"Black 51 (M3) reinforces the bottom, and White 52 (Q6) presses Black’s R5 on the right. Black 53 (G17) goes into the top side to stop White’s top left from growing.","57":"After White 54 (R6) firms up the right side, Black 55 (D17), White 56 (C17), Black 57 (E17): Black plays right above White’s top-left corner, trimming it while spreading along the top.","58":"White 58 (C14) spreads from the top-left corner along the side towards Black’s left-side stones. White’s top left and Black’s left side now meet head-on.","60":"When Black 59 (J8) caps White’s centre stones, White 60 (G4) captures two black stones. White’s bottom-left stones finally get some breathing room — a struggling White takes what it can.","63":"Black 61 (O3) adds a stone on the right of the bottom, White 62 (J17) goes to the top, and Black 63 (F16) holds White’s top left from outside. Sparks of fighting spread all over the board.","66":"White 64 (M17), Black 65 (B13), White 66 (B14): Black slips under White’s stones at the left edge while White tries to settle the top-left group. White’s C9–D9 are still surrounded.","68":"White 68 (B11). The next few white moves, together with move 80, became famous as Jowa’s brilliant moves — so remarkable that people said ghosts had shown them to him. B11 looks for a path linking White’s surrounded C9–D9 on the left with the top-left group. From here, the struggling White hits back.","70":"Black 69 (D10) tries to stop the connection, but White 70 (E12) ignores the left side and takes a key point in the centre first. Hidden resources remain for White on the left, so there is no need to answer at once. Taking back the initiative while letting the attack slide — a Meijin’s move.","72":"After Black 71 (F10) presses White’s centre, White 72 (R13) turns to the right side. White pauses the centre fight to stop Black’s top-right framework from growing.","76":"Black 73 (P11), White 74 (R15), Black 75 (Q16), White 76 (O14): White slips beside Black’s top-right corner and places stones linking the right side with the centre — groundwork for splitting Black’s stones.","78":"Black 77 (P9), White 78 (Q9): White blocks the link between Black’s right-side stones (R5, P9) and the black stones above. Will Black connect, or be cut apart? A tense moment.","80":"White 80 (Q10) makes an “empty triangle”, a clump of three stones usually considered bad shape. Yet it became the base for splitting Black’s right-side stones in two and attacking them. Valuing function over looks, it is often shown as the classic bad-looking but excellent move — the last of the famous three. From here White’s splitting attack begins in earnest, and the game slowly starts to lean towards White.","83":"After Black 81 (S6) and White 82 (S7), Black 83 (R3) goes into White’s bottom-right corner. Under pressure on the right, Black seeks both life and territory by invading White’s corner.","86":"Black 85 (S2), White 86 (R4): Black reduces White’s liberties from the first line, White holds Black from inside the corner. Both are counting how many moves each group has before capture.","88":"From White 84 (R2) to White 88 (Q2), a close capturing race goes on along the first and second lines of the corner. Who captures first? One move can make the difference.","91":"Black 91 (Q4) captures a white stone and gets one step ahead in the corner race. But White is preparing to wrap Black’s stones from outside.","95":"White 92 (N4), Black 93 (R7), White 94 (Q7), Black 95 (O4): White goes round Black’s outside from the bottom, while Black tries to connect on both the right and the bottom. The bigger Black’s group grows, the harder it is to save.","98":"White 98 (M4) wraps Black’s bottom stones from outside. Black has no quick safe shape either in the bottom-right corner or towards the centre.","101":"From White 96 (N3) to Black 101 (O5), black and white stones grow side by side from the bottom towards the centre — a pushing match where each holds the other from one side.","104":"After Black 103 (P2), White 104 (R4) captures a black stone. In the bottom-right corner race, White has taken a step back.","106":"Black 105 (N2), White 106 (S5) captures a black stone. White holds the bottom-right corner; Black’s stones cannot live there and must run outward.","107":"Black 107 (Q11) protects Black’s upper right-side stones: with the lower group in trouble, Black at least makes the upper one safe. White 108 (M2) and Black 109 (O1) continue a small capturing fight at the bottom edge.","113":"After White 110 (J4), Black 111 (K2) and White 112 (H3), Black 113 (K6): Black’s bottom stones poke out towards the centre looking for life. They must secure an exit before White closes the net.","116":"White 114 (L5), Black 115 (L3), White 116 (L6): White puts a lid over Black’s stones and narrows the exit to the centre. Black cuts White’s bottom stones while trying to get out.","120":"Black 117 (K7), White 118 (L7), Black 119 (J9), White 120 (N9): Black leaps out into the centre and White runs ahead to block from the right. It is now a race over whose stones weaken first.","125":"With White 122 (J2) and Black 123 (K3), Black’s bottom stones just manage to connect. Black 125 (N11) tries to link the upper right-side group with the centre stones.","128":"White 126 (O7), Black 127 (N8), White 128 (L11): White goes round above Black’s centre stones too, pressing them from above and below. The splitting plan prepared since move 80 is taking shape.","132":"From Black 129 (M10) to White 132 (K10), the two sides cut each other in the centre. Black tries to break White’s ring; White closes Black’s exits one by one.","137":"From Black 133 (L8) to White 136 (P10), the centre gets even more tangled. Black 137 (N13) jumps far upwards, trying to link the centre stones with Black’s top.","138":"White 138 (K8) captures a stone and a ko begins in the centre. For about 30 moves the sides take and retake at K8 and L8. This ko will decide the fate of Black’s centre stones.","143":"From Black 139 (K9) to Black 143 (J6), threats and retakes continue. Black 143 captures a white stone and eases the pressure on Black’s bottom stones.","148":"From White 144 (K5) to White 148 (K8), ko threats and retakes continue at the bottom and centre. With each threat, White gains a little around Black’s bottom stones.","152":"White 152 (E11) is a ko threat, hitting a weak point in Black’s left-side stones. Throughout the ko, White keeps finding moves all over the board that Black has to answer.","158":"From Black 155 (H2) to White 158 (C6), ko threats in the bottom and bottom left cause small trades. White 158 threatens Black’s bottom-left corner, and Black defends with 159 (B6).","162":"Black 161 (G1), White 162 (F1): captures happen at the bottom edge too. During the ko, small fights spread to every corner of the board.","167":"After White 166 (K8) retakes again, Black 167 (M1) does not continue the ko but plays elsewhere on the bottom. White thus wins the centre ko and can connect its centre stones. Black gains something on the bottom instead, but its centre stones are now in trouble.","170":"After White 168 (K11) and Black 169 (H10), White 170 (P15) heads for the top right. White settles the centre for now and aims at the big black group there, which is not yet fully alive.","175":"From Black 171 (L13) to Black 175 (K14), Black extends towards the top to link the top-right group with the centre stones. White 174 (O17) narrows Black’s living space near the top-right corner.","178":"White 178 (J14) jumps into the middle of the top to split Black’s stones left and right. The splitting attack prepared since move 80 now begins in earnest. Black must defend both sides at once — a very hard position.","182":"From Black 179 (K15) to White 182 (G14), White presses Black from outside on the left of the top as well. Black’s top-left stones also have to look for living space.","190":"From Black 183 (F14) to White 190 (J13), the two sides cut each other fiercely on the left of the top. White keeps placing stones between Black’s, breaking up Black’s shape.","193":"Black 193 (J11) captures a white stone; Black fights hard to connect and find life. But Black’s stones on the right of the top are still uneasy.","199":"From White 194 (S14) to Black 199 (A16), White holds Black’s top-right corner from outside while Black works at the top-left edge too. Which big group weakens first? A key moment before the end.","201":"Black 201 (R14) captures a stone and a new ko begins on the right side at R14 and R15. Whether Black’s top-right group lives depends on this ko.","206":"From White 202 (E14) to White 206 (M13), White plays ko threats while slowly tightening the grip on Black’s centre stones. Even during the ko, White’s overall net is nearly closed.","213":"From Black 207 (L17) to Black 213 (K18), Black fights to connect on the top. White reaches down to the first and second lines to rob Black of eyes — the empty points a group needs to live.","222":"White 222 (L14) captures a stone. Black’s top stones remain split in two, and saving both is now very hard. The Meijin’s splitting attack is finally paying off.","225":"Black 225 (R15) resolves the right-side ko and the top-right group manages to live. But meanwhile White has taken the middle of the top, and overall White is still ahead.","232":"Black 229 (P14) and White 232 (Q14) start another small ko in the top right. Black keeps looking for chances to the end, but White answers carefully.","239":"With 237 (F12) and 239 (E13), Black captures back on the top left. Even so, it does not make up for what was lost in the middle of the top, and the overall gap does not shrink.","244":"Black 241 (P18), White 242 (P19), Black 243 (P14), White 244 (N12): small moves continue to the end, but White plays the important centre points one by one. The game is almost decided.","246":"After White 246 (L8), Intetsu resigned. Early on he took a big lead with the Inoue house’s secret line, but Jowa hit back with his brilliant moves from 68 and the ugly-but-excellent move 80, kept splitting and attacking Black’s stones and finally turned the game around. As the stones were being cleared, Intetsu is said to have coughed up blood onto the board. He had probably been ill for some time, and he died not long afterwards. For Jowa, who kept the Meijin title, it is called the greatest win of his life, and Gennan’s plan failed. It is still told today as the game young Intetsu played with his life."}},"deepblue1997":{"t":"Deep Blue Wins","w":"Deep Blue (IBM computer)","b":"Garry Kasparov","ev":"Game 6 of the rematch with IBM's Deep Blue (a six-game match)","d":"1997","pl":"United States (New York)","lead":"World champion Kasparov against Deep Blue, a chess computer built by IBM, in a six-game match. With the score level after five games, the last game was over in only 19 moves. It was the first time a computer beat the world champion in a full match of several games.","c":{"0":"New York, 11 May 1997. World champion Garry Kasparov against Deep Blue, a chess computer built by IBM, in a six-game match. Kasparov had won their match the year before, but in this rematch against the stronger Deep Blue the score was 2½–2½ after five games. In the last game, Deep Blue had White. The whole world watched to see whether a human or a computer was stronger.","1":"White pushes the king's pawn two squares, taking the centre and opening paths for the pieces.","2":"Kasparov plays c6, getting ready for d5 to hit e4: the Caro-Kann Defence. It is solid, and players often chose it against computers.","3":"White brings the other pawn to the centre too. Two pawns stand side by side.","4":"Black plays d5 and hits e4. The fight for the centre pawns begins.","5":"The knight goes to c3 and guards e4. A natural move: develop and defend.","6":"Black takes the pawn on e4, trading centre pawns to make White's centre smaller.","7":"The knight takes back. White's knight now stands in the middle of the board.","8":"Black puts a knight on d7. Then, when the other knight goes to f6, Black can take back with this knight if White trades knights.","9":"The knight jumps to g5, a sharp plan aiming at f7 and e6. White attacks early, before Black's king side is settled.","10":"Black brings the knight to f6. Now more pieces guard the king.","11":"Bishop to d3. It aims diagonally at h7 and points toward Black's king side.","12":"Black plays e6 to strengthen the defence and open the way for the bishop on f8. But the e6 pawn also becomes a target for White's knight.","13":"The other knight goes from g1 to f3. White is ready to castle.","14":"Black plays h6 to chase the knight on g5. But in this position a knight sacrifice on e6 was already known for White. People found it strange that a master like Kasparov chose this, and some think he mixed up the move order by accident. This is the turning point.","15":"The knight is given up on e6! In return, White breaks the pawns around Black's king and stops Black from castling. Deep Blue is said to have played this move from the opening moves it had stored in advance.","16":"Black doesn't take at once but puts the queen on e7. On the e-file it lines up on the knight on e6 and White's king behind it. If the knight moves, the king is in check, so the knight cannot escape.","17":"White castles and moves the king away. Now the knight on e6 is no longer pinned. White does not mind if the knight is taken.","18":"Black takes the knight and is ahead in material. But the pawns around Black's king are broken, and the king is poorly protected.","19":"Bishop to g6, check. With the f7 pawn gone, the diagonal reaches the king on e8.","20":"The king goes to d8. Black can no longer castle, and the king is stuck near the middle. Black's pieces get in each other's way around the king.","21":"The other bishop goes to f4, eyeing c7 and b8 and squeezing the space around Black's king even more.","22":"Black plays b5, making room on the queen's side to bring the bishop to b7 and give the king a way out.","23":"a4 hits the pawn on b5. White wants to open lines on the queen's side so the rook on a1 can join the attack.","24":"Black brings the bishop to b7, aiming down the long diagonal at g2. Black's pieces are finally starting to move.","25":"Rook to e1. On the e-file it eyes the e6 pawn and the queen on e7 behind it. All of White's pieces have joined the attack.","26":"Black's knight goes to d5, a strong central square, attacking the bishop on f4 and protecting e6.","27":"The bishop drops back to g3. It keeps aiming at c7 and b8, keeping the pressure on Black's king.","28":"Black's king goes to c8, a little safer, and lets the rook on d8 move.","29":"White takes the pawn on b5. The a-file opens, and the rook on a1 now eyes the pawn on a7.","30":"Black takes back with a pawn. But fewer and fewer pawns are left around Black's king.","31":"Queen to d3, eyeing the b5 pawn and the square f5. White's pieces slowly surround Black's king.","32":"Black brings the bishop back to c6 to guard the b5 pawn.","33":"Bishop to f5! It can be taken for free. But if a pawn takes it, the e-file pawn is gone, and the rook on e1 reaches the queen on e7 directly.","34":"Black takes with a pawn. Now nothing stands on the e-file between e1 and e7.","35":"The rook takes the queen on e7. White has given a knight, a bishop and a rook, but has won the strongest piece, the queen.","36":"Black takes back the rook with the bishop. Material is about even, but Black's king has almost no shelter.","37":"c4! The pawn hits both b5 and d5, and Black's defenders start to fall apart. White's queen is ready to join the attack at any moment. Kasparov resigned here, probably feeling his king was too open to stop the attack. Only 19 moves. It was the first time a computer beat the world champion in a full match of several games: a historic moment. After this, using computers to study and train became normal in chess."}},"seigen1933":{"t":"The 3-3, Star Point and Tengen Game","w":"Go Seigen","b":"Honinbo Shusai","ev":"Commemorative game with the Meijin (over three months)","d":"1933–1934","pl":"Japan (Tokyo)","lead":"Nineteen-year-old Go Seigen (5-dan) played the Meijin, Honinbo Shusai. With Black he opened on the 3-3 point, then a star point, then the very centre (tengen) — breaking every rule of the day and astonishing the go world. With many adjournments the game lasted over three months, and White won by 2 points.","c":{"0":"Tokyo, autumn 1933. Black is 19-year-old Go Seigen (5-dan); White is the 59-year-old Meijin, Honinbo Shusai. Go Seigen was born in Fujian, China, and came to Japan at 14 as a child prodigy. That year he and his friend Kitani Minoru were spreading a new way of playing called “Shin Fuseki” (New Opening), which valued power in the centre more than territory in the corners, and it had become the talk of the go world. Shusai was the last Meijin to hold the Honinbo name by inheritance, and stood at the very top of go. Everyone wanted to see how the new generation would challenge the old master. The game was adjourned many times (stopped and continued on a later day) and lasted more than three months. There was no komi in those days, so only the territory on the board decides the result. The game began on 16 October 1933 at the Kajibashi Inn in Kyobashi, Tokyo. Go Seigen had won the Japan Go Championship run by the Yomiuri Shimbun newspaper, and this game against the Meijin was his prize. In the Edo period the shogun’s government supported the go houses; by now newspapers were holding the big games and reporting them in their daily pages. In the wider world, Hitler had become chancellor of Germany that January, and on 17 October, the day after the game began, Einstein arrived in America. During the game, on 23 December, Japan’s crown prince (later Emperor Akihito) was born.","1":"Black 1 is the 3-3 point in the top right (R17), where the third lines from both edges cross. One stone there almost surely secures the corner territory. In return it has little power towards the outside, and at the time it was thought “not a move to play first”. Against the Meijin, Go Seigen began with a 3-3 right away. Times have changed: today the 3-3 point is an ordinary move even early in the game.","2":"White 2 is the 3-4 point (komoku) in the top left (C16), where the third and fourth lines cross. It is the most basic opening move, played since the Edo period. The Meijin showed no surprise and chose his usual move. Shusai, born in 1874 as Tamura Yasuhisa, was the 21st Honinbo and became Meijin in 1914. He was the last Honinbo to inherit the title through the house. In 1936 he handed the name “Honinbo” over to the Nihon Ki-in, and from this came the Honinbo tournament, where players win the name by strength. His retirement game in 1938 was against Kitani Minoru, Go Seigen’s friend, and it is told in the novel “The Master of Go” by the writer Kawabata Yasunari.","3":"Black 3 is the star point in the bottom left (D4), where the two fourth lines cross. Unlike the 3-3, it values spreading outwards more than territory. Diagonally opposite the 3-3, Black placed a stone of a quite different character. Go Seigen was born in 1914 in Fuzhou, Fujian, China. In 1928, at fourteen, he came to Japan at the invitation of the professional Segoe Kensaku, helped by the businessman Okura Kishichiro and by Inukai Tsuyoshi, who later became prime minister.","4":"White 4 takes the bottom-right corner with another 3-4 point (Q3). White plans to take the corners one by one and get ahead in territory. Whatever new things Black tries, the Meijin’s plan does not waver.","5":"Black 5 goes to the very centre of the board: tengen (K10). Tengen is equally close to every side, so it can lend strength to a fight in any direction. But it hardly becomes territory, so almost no one played it in the opening. 3-3, star point, tengen: with three stones Black displayed three ideas side by side — corner territory, outward spread, and central power. This daring opening caused a sensation, and it is said that some people even called it disrespectful to the Meijin. Shin-fuseki, the “new opening”, took shape that summer of 1933, when Go Seigen and Kitani Minoru studied together at Jigokudani hot spring in Nagano. In the autumn ranking games the two played it and finished first and second, and in 1934 the book “A Revolution in Go: The New Opening” came out and sold 100,000 copies. Tengen on move 1 had in fact been tried before: in 1670, Shibukawa Harumi (Yasui Santetsu), who was also an astronomer, is said to have opened on tengen against Honinbo Dosaku in a castle game at Edo Castle, and lost by 9 points. The shin-fuseki boom calmed down within a few years, but its idea of valuing the centre was handed on to later players.","6":"White 6 (Q16) is a “shoulder hit”, leaning diagonally on Black’s 3-3 stone in the top right. White wants to shut Black in low in the corner and build a thick wall outside. This is the standard way to meet a 3-3, and the Meijin went straight for the starting point of Black’s new opening.","8":"Black 7 (R16) crawls and White 8 (Q15) extends. Black widens its living space in the corner while White stretches its wall towards the right side. Black does not push outwards here because it has clearly given the 3-3 stone the job of “corner territory”.","9":"Black 9 (P18) jumps along the second line on the top side, widening the corner territory and making a firm living shape. Now the top right is clearly divided: the corner is Black’s territory, the outside is White’s thickness.","10":"White 10 (R6) sits where it works with both the wall in the top right (Q16, Q15) above and the stone in the bottom right (Q3) below. It is a calm, large move aiming to make the whole right side White’s domain.","11":"Black 11 (K4) is the middle of the bottom side, on the fourth line right below tengen. Lined up vertically with K10, it aims at a large framework from the bottom side up into the centre. All of Black’s stones stand high, showing clearly that Black values influence over territory.","12":"White 12 (M3) approaches from the bottom-right corner, landing on the third line just right of Black’s K4. It is a move to get a foothold early so that Black cannot enclose the bottom side. Left alone, Black might play around here next and turn the bottom side into a big territory.","13":"Black 13 (D10) takes the middle of the left side. With D4 and D10 on the left and K4 and K10 in the centre, Black’s stones now spread like a net over the lower-left half of the board. Not answering White 12 at once and taking a big point first is very much the spirit of the New Opening.","14":"White 14 (E16) adds a second stone in the top-left corner, enclosing it (a shimari). The Meijin is not distracted by Black’s new style and keeps making corners surely his own. By now the plans are sharply split: White has two corners and the right side, Black two corners and the centre. Traditional go valued the order “corners first, then sides, and the centre last”. Shusai plays just as that idea says, while Black, in the new-opening way, looks to the centre first. Two eras’ ideas are colliding on a single board.","15":"Black 15 (M5) caps White’s M3 from above. Black wants to press White down low and build a wall outside. The idea: even if White gets some territory on the bottom side, Black turns that into power in the centre.","18":"White 16 (O3) widens the foothold towards the White stone at Q3, and with Black 17 (L3) and White 18 (M4) the stones clash on the bottom side. White makes a living shape while securing the third line; Black presses from above. Both sides keep to their own plans.","20":"White 20 (C12) slides from the top-left enclosure down the left side along the third line. It approaches Black’s D10 and stops the whole left side from becoming Black’s. The Meijin’s method was to take territory bit by bit from the edges before Black’s framework could grow. In this game Shusai was allowed to adjourn on his own turn, and there were as many as 13 adjournments. Some criticised this, saying he could talk things over with his pupils during the breaks, and from Shusai’s retirement game in 1938 the “sealed move” came into use: when play stops, the next move is written down and sealed in an envelope.","21":"Black 21 (G10) sits exactly between D10 on the left side and K10 at tengen. Black’s stones now stand in a row at fourth-line height, forming a big wall from the left side into the centre. Black ignores White’s territory and keeps widening its central influence.","22":"White 22 (F3) approaches Black’s star point D4 in the bottom left from the bottom side (a kakari). White already has stones in the bottom right and on the bottom side, so making territory here too would give White the whole bottom edge. It shaves the lowest rim of Black’s big framework.","25":"Black 23 (F5) caps White’s F3; when White 24 (J3) extends right along the bottom, Black 25 (G4) presses from above again. White on the third line, Black on the fourth and fifth: the pattern of this game — White takes territory, Black takes walls — repeats here too.","26":"White 26 (C3) is the 3-3 point inside Black’s star point D4 in the bottom left. Slipping in right under the star stone, White goes for the corner territory. A star point has strong outward power, but its weakness is that it cannot keep the corner when the 3-3 is invaded. This 3-3 invasion under a star point is still a common start of star-point joseki in the opening today.","28":"Black 27 (C4) blocks from outside and White 28 (D3) makes a living shape in the corner. Black gives the corner territory to White and in return gains a thick wall facing the centre: C4, D4, F5, G4. Looking over the opening, White has territory in the top-left, bottom-right and bottom-left corners and on the bottom side, while Black has the top-right corner and central influence. How much of that central influence becomes territory will decide the game from here on.","29":"Black 29 (O16) is played on the top side, to the left of White’s wall in the top right (Q16, Q15). It keeps the wall from working along the top side and widens Black’s top-right corner along the top edge. It stands at a sensible distance, not too close to White’s thickness.","30":"White 30 (Q12) extends down the right side from the top-right wall and makes a base (a foothold for life). Now the top-right White stones and R6 can link up more easily, and the right side starts to look like White territory.","31":"Black 31 (Q10) lands on the right-side star point, right between White’s Q12 and R6. It stops White from turning the whole right side into territory, and it also works together with Black’s central influence. From here the right side becomes a battlefield where Black and White stones mix.","33":"White 32 (O15) attaches under Black’s O16, shaking the root of Black’s top-side stone. Black 33 (N15) blocks from the left, closing White’s road to the left of the top side and the centre. By keeping White’s stones from getting out, Black wants the top side as its sphere of influence.","35":"When White 34 (N14) extends downwards, Black 35 (O14) cuts between White’s O15 and N14. Cut apart, White’s stones split into two, and each becomes weak. Black makes White fight inside Black’s own sphere, hoping to profit while attacking.","37":"After White 36 (O13), Black 37 (P15) captures White’s O15 — the first prisoner of the game. White 38 (P14) is answered by Black 39 (O15), connecting, and Black’s top-right stones are now firmly joined to the top side. White lost a stone but has poked out into the centre with O13, N13 and N14.","40":"White 40 (O10) jumps far into the centre from the O13 stone, landing between Black’s Q10 on the right side and tengen. White now has a foothold inside Black’s central framework. For Black, there is also the pleasure of attacking these White stones inside its own sphere.","41":"Black 41 (P7) is played low on the right side, pressing White’s R6 from above and working with Q10. Now White’s right-side stones, Q12 above and R6 below, are squeezed by Black and hard to connect. Black is trying to shrink White’s territory on the right side as well.","43":"After White 42 (N13) connects and settles the central White stones, Black 43 (L15) extends left along the top side. Black’s top-side wall grows longer, and the area from the top side into the centre becomes Black’s sphere. Black does not rush the fight and first enlarges its own domain.","44":"White 44 (H17) extends from the top-left enclosure along the top side, stopping Black’s top-side influence before it can grow further. Here too the Meijin places a stone on the side, where territory is easy to make, before Black’s framework spreads. Another rim of Black’s big framework has been taken by White.","45":"Black 45 (R7) attaches right on top of White’s R6 on the right side. This starts the first big fight of the game. Black aims to cut R6 off from the White stones above (Q12) and split White’s right side in two. White does not back down either, and from here a tricky shape of mutually cut stones continues from the right side into the bottom right.","48":"White 46 (S7), Black 47 (S6), White 48 (S8). White places stones on the second line below and above Black’s R7 to gain strength around it. Black’s S6 goes round to the lower right of White’s R6, taking away the space R6 needed to live easily along the edge. Each side is poking at the other’s weak points.","52":"Black 49 (S5), White 50 (Q7), Black 51 (R8), White 52 (R9). White’s Q7 cuts in between Black’s R7 and P7, and with Black R8 and White R9 the stones end up mutually cut. On the right side both sides’ stones remain cut, a hard fight in which whoever is left with a weak group first will suffer.","55":"Black 53 (Q8), White 54 (Q6), Black 55 (Q9). Black extends to Q8 and Q9, linking R7 and R8 to Q10 above. White’s Q6 gathers the lower White stones R6 and Q7 into one group. Black’s right-side stones are now one chain, but they are surrounded by White and do not have many liberties.","58":"White 56 (R10), Black 57 (O8), White 58 (Q11). With R10 and Q11 White joins the upper right-side stone (Q12) with R9 and S8 along the edge. Black’s O8 strengthens the central side and blocks White’s way into the centre. White’s right side links up and down, Black links to the centre, and the fight calms for a while.","59":"Black 59 (R4) now invades the bottom-right corner, going into the territory of White’s Q3. It also aims to link with Black’s S5 and S6 above, threatening the footing of White’s R6, Q6 and Q7. For White it becomes a hard choice: protect the corner territory, or protect the stones outside.","63":"White 60 (P6), Black 61 (O6), White 62 (O5), Black 63 (N5). With P6 and O5 White goes to join its right-side stones to those below, and Black presses from above and from the left with O6 and N5. Through O6, Black brings its bottom wall (M5) close to its right-side stones P7 and O8. White chose to protect the stones outside, leaving Black inside the bottom-right corner.","66":"With White 64 (P4) White firmly joins the stones that came down the right side to Q3 below. Black 65 (R2) and White 66 (R3) continue the tussle inside the corner. Black is trying to make room to live in the corner on the low first and second lines.","69":"Black 67 (S3), White 68 (Q2), Black 69 (S2). Black connects its stones along the edge of the bottom-right corner and makes a living shape. White’s bottom-right corner became much smaller than expected, but in return White’s stones are thickly connected outside (P4, P6, Q6). Black cut into territory, White strengthened the outside: a result both sides can accept.","70":"White 70 (N6) cuts in between Black’s O6 and N5, at the seam between Black’s bottom-side and right-side stones. The aim is to split Black’s stones and weaken one part. Black 71 (O7) connects at once, keeping the right-side Black stones together.","74":"After White 72 (M7) and Black 73 (M8), White 74 (L4) pushes in between Black’s K4 and L5 on the bottom side, trying to leave Black’s L3 stranded among White’s bottom stones. Black 75 (N4) and White 76 (N3) follow, and White’s bottom stones join the White stones on the right along the third line.","78":"Black 77 (J4) links Black’s bottom wall to the left, and White 78 (K3) blocks the way out for Black’s L3. The L3 stone now has just one liberty (L2). Black gives it up and puts first the long wall facing the centre: J4, K4, L5, M5, N5, N4. Rather than clinging to a small stone, Black protects the big plan.","79":"Black 79 (L9) is played below and to the right of tengen (K10), making the central Black stones thicker still. Black’s bottom wall, the right-side stones from O6 to Q10, tengen, and the left-side stones from D10 to G10 now ring a huge central framework. White earns territory in the corners and on the sides; the question is how much of the centre Black can turn into territory. The position seems close to even, and the game now enters the middle-game fighting.","80":"White 80 (C6) climbs from the bottom-left White corner up the left side and places a stone on the third line beside Black’s wall. It narrows Black’s left side and will also serve as a foothold for an invasion there later. Black 81 (E4) mends the gap between D4 and F5/G4, making the wall one piece.","82":"White 82 (C10) invades by attaching to the left of Black’s D10, on the edge side. If the whole left side became Black’s territory, Black’s central framework would be very large. White will not allow that and tries to live at the far left end of Black’s formation.","85":"Black 83 (C9), White 84 (D9), Black 85 (C8), White 86 (D8). Black blocks under White’s C10 from the edge side, while White extends D9 and D8 on the inner side of Black’s wall. Each side is pushing the other against the edge. Neither side’s stones can be called alive yet.","88":"Black 87 (C11) is a severe move that cuts between White’s C10 and C12. White 88 (B10) drops towards the edge, widening the space for the C10 stone to live. Black’s aim is to cut off the invading White stones and attack them.","91":"Black 89 (B11), White 90 (D11), Black 91 (E10). Black extends the cutting stone with B11, but White’s D11 presses on the head of Black’s D10 from above. Black’s E10 links D10 towards tengen and protects the left-side wall. Black’s cutting stones B11 and C11 are caught between White stones and running short of liberties.","96":"With White 92 (B12), Black’s two stones B11 and C11 are down to one liberty. Black does not save them; Black 93 (D7) instead blocks under White’s D9 and D8. White 94 (E11), Black 95 (F10) and White 96 (C7) follow, and on the left side both sides are now trying to capture the other’s stones. Black boldly chose capturing over rescuing.","98":"After Black 97 (A9), White 98 (A11) captures the two Black stones B11 and C11. White makes a living shape on the edge of the left side, and Black’s left-side territory is smaller than expected. In return, after Black 99 (E8) White’s D9 and D8 have only one liberty (E9), and Black can capture them whenever it likes. Two stones lost, two stones won: the left side ended roughly even.","100":"White 100 (B4) widens White’s bottom-left corner while pressing under Black’s C4. With B4 and, soon, C5, White’s corner stones C3 and D3 link up with C6 and C7 on the left side. The White stones from the bottom left up the left side become one strong group.","101":"Black 101 (S14) slides from Black’s top-right corner down the right side, reducing the top part of White’s right-side territory (Q12, Q11). While White 102 (C5) secures the bottom left, Black goes one step further with 103 (R13), and White 104 (S12) just about protects the right-side territory. Bit by bit, the borders between the two sides’ territories are being fixed.","105":"Black 105 (K17) is played in the middle of the top side, to the right of White’s H17. It stops White’s top-side territory from spreading further right and works with Black’s top-side stones L15 and N15. The top side looks set for a fight over the border: White on the left, Black on the right.","106":"White 106 (G3) now attaches on the bottom side, right under Black’s G4. White wants to fill the gap between F3 and J3 with its own stone and firm up the bottom-side territory. Black 107 (E3) goes in between White’s D3 and F3 to cut. It may look small, but this exchange decides where the borders of territory lie.","108":"White 108 (E6) is played just above Black’s bottom-left wall, a little above D4 and E4. Working with White’s C5 and C6 on the left side, it pokes at the back of Black’s wall. Black 109 (F6) immediately presses from above, stopping White from coming out into the centre.","113":"White 110 (E2), Black 111 (F2) and White 112 (D2) continue the tussle for stones on the bottom edge, and Black 113 (H3) cuts in between White’s G3 and J3. It tries to break White’s bottom-side stones apart and shrink White’s territory. Black is trying to reduce, even a little, the White territory under its own wall.","116":"Black 115 (H4) connects H3 to the wall, and White 116 (G2) links F3 and G3 from below. Black’s F2 stone is down to one liberty (F1) and is all but captured. Though poked at its gaps, White’s bottom side has held together as one.","117":"Black 117 (F17) invades on the third line of the top side, between White’s top-left enclosure (C16, E16) and White’s H17. It cuts into White’s top-side territory and, if possible, aims to split White’s stones and attack them. A new fight begins on the left half of the top side.","120":"White 118 (J17) strengthens the top-side stones, and Black 119 (D17) goes even deeper into the top-left corner. White 120 (C17) protects the corner. Black is trying to take the very top of White’s corner, around the second line. White guards the corner while getting ready to squeeze Black from outside.","124":"Black 121 (G16), White 122 (H15), Black 123 (G15), White 124 (K15). With G16 and G15 Black extends towards the centre to separate the top-left White stones from White’s H17. With H15 and K15 White links its top-side stones towards the centre and threatens Black’s K17 on the top side. In this top-side fight, Black and White stones keep poking their heads out in turn.","127":"Black 125 (H16), White 126 (J16), Black 127 (H14). Black presses on White’s H15 from above with H16 and from below with H14. The H15 stone is surrounded and has only one liberty left (J15). Black takes the upper centre for itself while weakening White’s stones one by one.","130":"White 128 (L16), Black 129 (M15), White 130 (L17). With L16 and L17 White shuts Black’s K17 in on the top side and gives its top-side stones a living shape. Black’s K17 can hardly move any more. The result: Black gains the upper centre, White keeps the top-side territory.","133":"Black 131 (P13), White 132 (P12), Black 133 (Q13). Black presses the base of White’s right-side stones and cuts White’s top-right stones Q16, Q15 and P14 off from White’s Q12 on the right side. Those top-right White stones are surrounded by Black and in a painful shape. The top-right corner is becoming fairly secure as Black territory.","135":"White 134 (E7) steps from the bottom left into the inside of Black’s wall, and Black 135 (E9) captures White’s two stones D9 and D8. Black had earlier lost two stones on the left side, so this wins back the same number. Black’s left-side stones are now safe, and Black’s territory from the left side into the centre is becoming clearer.","137":"White 136 (F7), Black 137 (G7). White lines up E6, E7 and F7 above Black’s bottom-left wall, trying to come out into the centre, and Black stops it with G7. Black’s central framework is being shaved bit by bit from below too. White does not charge in deeply; its plan is to shrink the edges one by one.","138":"White 138 (N18) goes in on the second line on the right part of the top side, to the left of Black’s P18 and O16. It shrinks Black’s top-right territory from the top-side direction. Later White 148 (O18) pushes further right, and Black’s top-side territory is shaved little by little.","139":"Black 139 (C18) now invades on the second line above White’s top-left corner. Above White’s corner (C16, C17, E16) there is still room for Black to live. If Black lives here, White’s top-left territory will shrink a lot. It is a bold move, trying to live right next to White’s thick stones.","145":"White 140 (E17), Black 141 (E18), White 142 (D16), Black 143 (D18), White 144 (G18), Black 145 (B17). Black lines up C18, D18 and E18 on the second line and goes deep into the corner with B17. White firms up the outside with E17 and D16, and stops Black from spreading right with G18. Above White’s corner, a delicate sequence continues as Black tries to live in a narrow space.","150":"White 146 (B16), Black 147 (B18), White 148 (O18), Black 149 (P19), White 150 (F18). Black gathers its stones above the top-left corner, and White tightens from outside with moves such as F18. White’s top-left corner is now only the part below the line from B16 to E16. Black has kept its stones alive inside White’s area and greatly reduced White’s top-left territory. Meanwhile White has also shaved Black’s territory in the top right with O18, and the fine exchanges go on.","152":"White 152 (G12): White finally goes inside Black’s central framework. G12 lies between White’s left-side stones (E11, D11) and the White stones above, in a gap in Black’s thickness. If White lives here or links up with the White stones around it, Black’s centre will not become territory. From here comes the biggest climax of the game.","153":"Black 153 (K14) is played right under White’s top-side stone K15, blocking the road by which White could link G12 to the top-side stones. At the same time it is a broad road joining Black’s central stones above and below. Black wants to split White into top-side and central parts and attack one of them.","156":"White 154 (J15) rescues White’s H15 on the top side, and after Black 155 (F14) comes White 156 (H11). White puts another stone in the centre and, working with G12, widens its foothold inside Black’s centre. Black’s centre is being seeped into, bit by bit, by White from above and from the left.","159":"White 158 (E5) pokes at the inside of Black’s bottom-left wall. If White went on to play F4, Black’s wall would be cut between E4 and F5. So Black 159 (F4) connects solidly. Before the central fight, Black mends the weak points of its wall one by one.","160":"White 160 (H8): the most famous move of the game. In the wide space at the bottom of Black’s central framework, surrounded by Black stones such as G7, G10, K10 and L9, White places a single stone. This stone is said to aim at several gaps in the links between Black’s stones, and Black cannot guard them all at once. With this move as a trigger, White starts to work its way into Black’s central territory one point after another. A rumour spread that a pupil, not Shusai himself, had found the move, but nothing about that is certain.","161":"Black 161 (J8) is played to the right of H8, stopping White from spreading below tengen, between it and Black’s bottom wall. It is a normal reply that guards the place that looks biggest. But White’s next aim lay somewhere else.","162":"White 162 (K12) now lands above tengen (K10). Working with White’s G12 and H11, it tries to split Black’s centre into an upper and a lower part. A shake-up below with H8, then straight round to the top: each time Black guards one spot, White touches another weak point — a skilful sequence.","165":"Black 163 (F12) is played to the left of G12, trying to separate White’s G12 from the White stones on the left side. White 164 (F11) seeks a connection, and Black 165 (H10) blocks under White’s H11 so that White’s central stones cannot come out downwards. In the centre, Black and White stones are tangled, each trying to cut the other.","168":"White 166 (J14), Black 167 (J12), White 168 (J11). With J14 White extends the top-side stones downwards, and with J11 it links to the central White stone H11. Black’s J12 is a stone that cuts White’s upper and lower parts. The centre has become a very intricate fight in which many stones are cut and connected.","172":"Black 169 (J13), White 170 (K13), Black 171 (K11), White 172 (F13). With F13 White reaches towards the White stones on the left, trying to link its central stones with the White stones on the left side and top left. What used to be Black’s central thickness is turning into a road for White’s stones to pass through.","175":"Black 173 (L14) rescues the single Black stone at K14, then White 174 (J10) and Black 175 (J9). White’s J10 enters right next to tengen on the left, and Black blocks from below with J9. White stones have reached the point just left of tengen (K10), and Black’s central territory keeps getting smaller.","178":"White 176 (H12) connects the central White stones, and after Black 177 (G14) comes White 178 (N9). This time White enters the right part of the centre, between Black’s L9 and O8. Black’s centre has been entered by White from both left and right, and it no longer looks likely to become a big territory. White does not miss Black’s weak points on the other side of the board either.","180":"After Black 179 (D14) protects the top-left Black stones, White 180 (N8) extends further down. With N9 and N8 White tries to separate Black’s right-side stones from O6 to O8 from Black’s central stones L9 and M8. Black’s right-side stones are surrounded by White and short of liberties, and this is becoming a new weak point.","186":"After exchanges on the right edge with Black 181 (T7) and White 182 (T9), Black 183 (N7) blocks under White’s N8; after White 184 (D13) and Black 185 (M11) comes White 186 (P8). It is a sharp move cutting between Black’s P7 and Q8. Black’s five stones O6, O7, O8, P7 and N7 on the right side are down to one liberty (O9), and the five Black stones from R7 to Q10 have only two. With one move White aimed at two Black groups at the same time.","189":"Black 187 (M6) captures White’s N6, and after White 188 (O9), Black 189 (L7) captures White’s M7. Now the five Black stones from O6 to P7 gain liberties and are saved by linking with the Black stones on the bottom side. Black has captured two White stones, but meanwhile, with O9, White has reached round to the other Black group on the right side.","192":"After White 190 (F8) and Black 191 (G8) comes White 192 (P10). Black’s five stones R7, R8, Q8, Q9 and Q10 on the right side are down to a single liberty, P9. Even if Black plays P9 and captures White’s P8, White can play back at P8 at once and capture the whole lot (a “snapback”), so these five stones can no longer be saved. Starting from the cut at White 186, White made a big gain on the right side. Part of the influence Black built towards the centre has become not territory but White’s prey.","197":"After Black 193 (R12), White 194 (S11), Black 195 (C14) and White 196 (E14) comes Black 197 (G11). White’s five central stones G12, H12, H11, J11 and J10 are down to a single liberty, G13. Black wants to capture White in the centre and win back the loss on the right side. Whether White’s central stones survive will decide the game.","198":"White 198 (E12) captures Black’s F12. Now White’s five central stones also have a liberty at F12, and they can link to the White stones on the left either through F12 or through G13. If Black blocks one, White connects through the other, so White’s central stones can no longer be captured. The White stones that split Black’s thickness have survived: this is the scene that decided the game.","203":"With Black 199 (E15), White 200 (E13), Black 201 (D15) and White 202 (B14) the upper left side is settled too, and from Black 203 (B3) the game enters the yose (the endgame stage in which the borders of territory are fixed). Black’s B3 enters White’s bottom-left corner on the first and second lines, trying to shave even a little off White’s territory. From here, fine moves over one or two points follow.","206":"Black 205 (Q1), White 206 (P1), Black 207 (R1), White 208 (P3). On the first line of the bottom-right corner Black shaves a little off White’s territory, and White firmly protects its corner. In the endgame, a single move on the first line makes a difference of one or two points. Even with the big fights over, neither the Meijin nor Go Seigen relaxes.","214":"With Black 209 (B5) and White 210 (A3), White captures Black’s B3; with Black 211 (A6), White 212 (B6), Black 213 (A7) and White 214 (A5), White captures Black’s B5 as well. On the bottom-left edge White takes two stones and fully protects its territory. Black also trims White’s territory higher up the left edge (A6, A7), so each side gains a little.","220":"Black 215 (S13), White 216 (N11), Black 217 (R11), White 218 (M12), Black 219 (L12), White 220 (M10). On the right side of the centre too, White advances with N11, M12 and M10, making the territory left in Black’s centre even smaller. The Black territory that was supposed to spread around tengen is now just a thin strip.","224":"With Black 221 (S10), White 222 (S9), Black 223 (T12) and White 224 (T10), White captures Black’s S10 on the right edge. White’s right-side territory has been completed without giving up a single point. Even when Black tries to win something back, White answers carefully and shows no gap at all.","232":"After Black 225 (B13), White 226 (A15), Black 227 (C13), White 228 (A13), Black 229 (F15), White 230 (G17) and Black 231 (F16) comes White 232 (C15). With this C15, Black’s large group of 18 stones stretching from the left of the top side into the centre is down to a single liberty, G13. While playing endgame moves on the left side, White has created an unexpected danger for Black’s big group. Black must act at once.","233":"Black 233 (L13) captures White’s two stones K12 and K13. Now Black’s big group gains liberties at K12 and K13, links up with the central Black stones and is safe. It was a close call, but by taking these two stones Black protected the big group. Capturing it would have won the game outright for the Meijin, but Go Seigen did not miss the danger.","246":"White 234 (N17), Black 235 (L11), White 236 (F9) … White 244 (G13), Black 245 (F19), White 246 (G19). With G13 White links its central stones to F13, and with F19 and G19 the border on the top edge is settled as well. Only small endgame moves worth one or two points remain. Counting the territory on the board, the difference is tiny.","249":"After White 248 (A16), Black 249 (M13) puts White’s M12 in atari (one move from capture), and White 250 (N12) connects. The last border in the centre is fixed too. Black 251 (R5) tidies up Black’s shape in the bottom right, and only a few moves remain.","252":"With White 252 (M9) the game ends. White won by 2 points. With the new opening of 3-3, star point and tengen, Black, Go Seigen, built great influence in the centre. But the Meijin, Shusai, took solid territory in the corners and on the sides, and in the late middle game, from move 152, he entered Black’s centre. Shaking the centre from move 160 at H8, and turning the cut at P8 on move 186 into the capture of five Black stones on the right side, were decisive, and Black’s central influence did not become as much territory as hoped. In the no-komi era Black, moving first, was thought to have the advantage, so winning with White shows the Meijin’s true strength. On the other hand, by playing the Meijin to within just 2 points at 19, Go Seigen made the New Opening leave a deep mark on the go world. Later, in ten-game matches, Go Seigen beat the top players of the day one after another and became the leading player of the Showa era."}},"century1956":{"t":"The Game of the Century","w":"Donald Byrne","b":"Bobby Fischer","ev":"The third Rosenwald Trophy tournament (Rosenwald Memorial)","d":"1956","pl":"United States (New York)","lead":"Thirteen-year-old Bobby Fischer beats Donald Byrne, one of America's strongest masters. On move 17 Fischer lets his queen be taken for nothing, then wins piece after piece back, and at the end his small pieces hunt down White's king. Fischer later became world champion.","c":{"0":"October 17, 1956: a game from the Rosenwald Trophy tournament (the Rosenwald Memorial), held at the Marshall Chess Club in New York. It was a strong event for invited top American players, and Bobby Fischer, playing Black, was only 13 years old, the youngest player there. White is Donald Byrne, 26, winner of the 1953 U.S. Open and one of the leading American masters of the time. Fischer’s overall result in the tournament was modest, but this one game made his name known around the world. In the magazine Chess Review, Hans Kmoch called it “The Game of the Century”, and the name is still used today. Let’s follow it move by move with Musowl and see how a 13-year-old boy beat a grown master.","1":"White plays the knight to f3. It controls the central squares e5 and d4 and helps guard the king’s side: a very calm start. White does not commit any pawns yet, so many different set-ups are still possible.","2":"Fischer also plays his knight to f6. It controls e4 and d5. Like White, he keeps his plans flexible and waits to see what his opponent does.","3":"White plays c4. This queen’s-side pawn attacks the d5 square from the side. The idea is to make it harder for Black to put a pawn on d5 and to gain space in the centre.","4":"Black plays g6. Next the bishop will go to g7 and look at the centre along the long diagonal. Fischer loved this kind of set-up from a young age: it holds back at first and then strikes back hard.","5":"White brings the other knight to c3. It controls d5 and e4 even more, and later White can line up pawns in the centre with d4 and e4.","6":"The bishop goes to g7. For now it stands behind the knight on f6, but as soon as the knight moves, the long diagonal from h8 to a1 opens up. Black is also ready to castle.","7":"White plays d4. With pawns on c4 and d4, White takes the centre. If Black answers with d5, the game becomes an opening called the Grünfeld Defence.","8":"Black castles. The king moves to safety on g8, and the rook on f8 comes closer to the centre. Young Fischer makes his king safe first, before the fighting starts.","9":"White plays the bishop to f4. It eyes c7 and b8 along the diagonal and also controls e5. But White’s king is still in the middle on e1, and White is not yet ready to castle.","10":"Black plays d5 and hits the pawn on c4 in the centre. Now it is a Grünfeld Defence. Black’s plan is to let White have the centre for a while and then attack it later.","11":"White’s queen comes out early to b3. It puts more pressure on d5 and also aims at the pawn on b7 down the b-file. It is a strong move, but a queen that comes out early can easily be chased around by enemy pieces.","12":"Black takes the pawn on c4. The pawn that captured now attacks the queen on b3 diagonally, so White has to take it back. In this way Black removes one of White’s central pawns.","13":"White’s queen recaptures on c4. The queen now stands near the middle of the board and looks at d5 and f7. But this queen will be chased by Black’s pieces again and again.","14":"Black plays c6. It controls d5 and b5, so White’s queen and knight cannot go there. It also prepares b5 later to chase the queen.","15":"White plays e4. Two pawns, on d4 and e4, now stand side by side in the centre. Just from the look of it, White has lots of space and seems to be playing comfortably.","16":"Black plays the knight to d7. From here it plans to jump to b6 and chase the queen. It also helps prepare the pawn move e5 to hit White’s centre.","17":"White plays the rook to d1. It supports the pawn on d4 from behind and makes the d-file strong. But because the rook moved before White castled, the king is still stuck in the middle on e1.","18":"The knight jumps to b6 and attacks the queen on c4. White’s queen has to move again. Black is bringing pieces forward while making White waste moves.","19":"The queen escapes to c5. From c5 it eyes the pawn on e7 and also tries to hold back Black’s queen’s side. But the queen is now far forward on the board, in a place where Black’s pieces can easily attack it.","20":"The bishop goes to g4 and attacks the knight on f3. Behind the knight is the rook on d1, so if the knight moves, the rook will be taken: this is called a pin. The knight on f3 guards important squares like d4 and e5, so now White can hardly move it.","21":"White plays the bishop to g5. It attacks the knight on f6 and tries to stop Black from moving that knight and playing e5. But White still has pieces at home and has not castled. Many people say it would have been better to play the bishop to e2 and get ready to castle.","22":"The knight goes to a4! This is the first big surprise of the game. The knight on a4 attacks both the queen on c5 and the knight on c3. If White takes it with the c3 knight, Black takes the pawn on e4 with the f6 knight and attacks the queen on c5 and the bishop on g5 at the same time. White’s king would still be in the middle under attack, so White cannot take the knight. It is said that, after this move, people gathered around the board to watch.","23":"White does not take the knight and moves the queen to a3 instead. From there it still eyes the pawn on e7 along the diagonal. White picked the way of defending that does the least damage.","24":"The knight takes the knight on c3. That knight was the piece that guarded the pawn on e4. So Black has now removed the defender of e4.","25":"White recaptures with a pawn. The material is equal, but White’s pawns on c3 and d4 are now a little loose. And nothing is guarding the pawn on e4 any more.","26":"The knight takes the pawn on e4! From e4 it attacks the pawn on c3 and the bishop on g5. White’s neat pawns in the centre have broken up, the e-file is opening, and the space in front of White’s king in the middle is now wide open.","27":"White takes the pawn on e7 with the bishop. From e7 it attacks Black’s queen on d8 and the rook on f8 at the same time: a double attack. White wins back the pawn and attacks Black’s big pieces. A strong counterattack.","28":"Black moves the queen to b6. It is a bold move that says: you can have the rook on f8. If White takes the rook, Black recaptures with the bishop, and that bishop then chases the queen on a3. Black thinks that White’s king still being in the middle matters more than a rook. The queen on b6 also eyes the pawn on d4.","29":"White does not take the rook and brings the bishop to c4 instead. This empties f1, so White can castle next move and take the king to safety. White also knows that king safety is the biggest problem.","30":"The knight takes the pawn on c3! From here it attacks the rook on d1. If White takes this knight with the queen, Black can bring a rook to e8 and attack the bishop on e7 and the king behind it in a straight line. Black’s pieces are gathering one after another around White’s king.","31":"White plays the bishop to c5 and attacks Black’s queen on b6. If Black simply moves the queen away, White can castle next move. But because the bishop left e7, the e-file is now open for Black’s rook.","32":"The rook moves to e8: check along the e-file! The bishop on e7 has gone to c5, so the e-file was open all the way from e8 to e1. Before saving the queen, Black first goes after White’s king.","33":"The king goes to f1. Now White can never castle. The rook on h1 is also stuck behind the king and has trouble getting out. Black has gained a moment, but the queen on b6 is still attacked by the bishop on c5.","34":"The bishop goes to e6! This is the most famous move of the game: Black lets the queen be taken for nothing. The bishop on e6 attacks White’s bishop on c4, and taking it will be check. If White takes on e6 with the c4 bishop, Black checks with the queen from b5, keeps checking with the knight, and at the end the king is surrounded by its own pieces and cannot move: a “smothered mate”. Fischer wrote that moving the knight to b5 instead would have lost to White taking the pawn on f7. A 13-year-old boy had looked far ahead, well past giving up his queen.","35":"White takes Black’s queen. If you only count pieces, White is far ahead. But now Black’s pieces will chase White’s king and win back piece after piece.","36":"The bishop takes White’s bishop on c4, with check! From c4 it aims through d3 and e2 at the king on f1. Black’s counterattack begins here.","37":"The king escapes to g1. Black’s rook controls the e-file, so there is almost nowhere else to go. On g1 the king is shut in by the rook on h1 and the pawn on g2 and can hardly move.","38":"The knight jumps to e2 with check. The king has to go back to f1. Black ties White’s hands with checks while getting ready for the next capture.","39":"The king goes back to f1. Now the knight on e2 stands in front of the bishop on c4. If this knight moves, the bishop behind it will give check to the king on f1: a “discovered check”.","40":"The knight takes the pawn on d4: discovered check! Because the knight moved, the bishop on c4 now checks the king on f1. When you are in check, you must deal with the king first, no matter what the knight is doing.","41":"The king goes to g1. White keeps getting checked, so there is no time to do anything else.","42":"The knight jumps to e2 again with check. The king has to go back to f1. Black can give check again and again, and each time looks for a chance to win something.","43":"The king goes to f1 again. White can only repeat the same moves. This pattern of checking, grabbing a piece and coming back is called a “windmill”, because it goes round and round.","44":"The knight moves to c3: another discovered check! The bishop on c4 checks the king on f1, and at the same time the knight on c3 attacks the rook on d1. White is in check, so the rook cannot be saved. The windmill is ready to grab another piece.","45":"The king goes to g1. Black could keep checking, but here Black stops for a moment to win back White’s bishop on b6.","46":"The pawn takes the bishop on b6. Now the bishop that took Black’s queen is gone too. Because the a7 pawn moved, the a-file is open, and the rook on a8 attacks White’s queen on a3. Count the pieces: Black has two rooks, two bishops and a knight; White has a queen, two rooks and a knight. Black can also take the rook on d1 next, so Black is getting back more than the queen was worth.","47":"White’s queen escapes to b4. From here it attacks the bishop on c4, the knight on c3 and the pawn on b6 all at once: a sharp escape. White is trying hard to win something back.","48":"The rook goes to a4! It chases the queen again. The rook on a4 is guarded by the knight on c3, and once the queen moves away, it also guards the bishop on c4. White’s queen cannot take any of Black’s pieces and has to run.","49":"White’s queen takes the pawn on b6. At least White wins back one pawn. But meanwhile it is the c3 knight’s turn to take the rook on d1.","50":"The knight takes the rook on d1. Black has two rooks, two bishops and a knight against White’s queen, rook and knight, and is clearly ahead. White’s rook on h1 is still shut in.","51":"White plays h3. This makes an escape square on h2 for the king. Once the king goes to h2, the rook on h1 can finally come out. It also stops Black from threatening White’s back rank forever.","52":"The rook takes the pawn on a2. White has no pawns left on the queen’s side, and nothing can stop Black’s pawns there any more. The rook has entered White’s second rank, where it can also aim at f2 near White’s king.","53":"The king moves to h2. Now the rook on h1 can finally move freely along the first rank. White is trying somehow to bring the remaining pieces into the game.","54":"The knight takes the pawn on f2. From f2 it attacks the rook on h1 and the pawn on h3. Another pawn near White’s king is gone.","55":"White moves the rook to e1 to escape and offers to trade it for Black’s rook on the e-file. The idea is to remove one of Black’s attacking pieces.","56":"Black trades rooks. When you have more pieces, trading usually helps you. Before taking back on e1 with the knight, White first gives check with the queen.","57":"The queen moves to d8 with check. With the rook on e8 gone, the eighth rank was open all the way to Black’s king on g8. White puts in this check first and plans to take back the rook afterwards.","58":"Black blocks the check with the bishop on f8. The bishop comes next to the king, and the defence becomes solid.","59":"The knight takes back the rook on e1. White has a queen and a knight; Black has a rook, two bishops and a knight. Adding up the strength of the pieces, Black is clearly better.","60":"The bishop goes to d5. It eyes the f3 square and the pawn on g2, and from behind along the diagonal it also guards the rook on a2. Black is gathering the pieces one by one on good squares where they support each other.","61":"The knight goes back to f3. Guarded by the pawn on g2, it is the only piece defending White’s king. With the queen and knight, White is trying to hold out against Black’s attack.","62":"The knight goes to e4. It steps into the centre and comes closer to White’s king. Next Black wants to play the f8 bishop to d6 and give check to the king on h2.","63":"White’s queen goes to b8. It attacks the pawn on b7 and, along the eighth rank, pins the f8 bishop to the king on g8. If the bishop moved, the king would be in check, so this stops Black’s plan of checking with the bishop.","64":"Black plays b5. The pawn moves two squares and escapes from the queen’s attack. On b5 it is guarded by the pawn on c6. One day Black may also push this pawn forward.","65":"White plays h4. The h-pawn goes forward, hoping to break up the pawns around Black’s king with h5 if possible. White does not have much left to do.","66":"Black plays h5 too. This stops White’s pawn and keeps the shape around Black’s king solid. Black is in no hurry and plays safely.","67":"The knight goes to e5. From the centre it attacks f7. Also, if the knight jumped to d7, the pinned bishop on f8 would be attacked by two pieces, the queen and the knight. This is White’s last hope of a counterattack.","68":"The king goes to g7. It frees the f8 bishop from the pin and also guards it. Now the bishop can move freely. Black is ready for the final attack.","69":"The king goes to g1. On h2 it could be checked by the freed bishop from d6, so it seems White stepped away first. But there are almost no defending pieces near White’s king.","70":"The bishop moves to c5: check! From c5 it aims through d4, e3 and f2 at the king on g1. Now Black’s small pieces begin to hunt White’s king.","71":"The king goes to f1. White’s king looks for a way out towards the left side of the board. But Black’s rook and bishops are waiting there, and the king is slowly driven to the other side of the board.","72":"The knight jumps to g3 with check. The king is driven to e1. The knight also blocks the king’s way back to the right side of the board.","73":"The king goes to e1. It cannot go back to f2 or g1, so it can only run to the left. Black’s net is slowly getting tighter.","74":"The other bishop comes out to b4 with check. From b4 it aims through c3 and d2 at e1. The king is driven further left, to d1.","75":"The king goes to d1. White’s queen and knight are on the far side of the board and cannot come to help the king.","76":"The bishop moves from d5 to b3 with check. From b3 it aims through c2 at d1. This bishop will later have an important job: guarding the square of the final rook move.","77":"The king goes to c1. There are almost no squares left to run to. One by one, Black’s pieces are closing the squares around White’s king.","78":"The knight jumps to e2 with check. From e2 it attacks c1. The king can only escape to b1.","79":"The king goes to b1. It has been chased almost into the corner of the board.","80":"The knight jumps to c3 with check. From c3 it attacks b1 and also controls d1. The king has to go back to c1.","81":"The king goes to c1. This will be its last square. The knight on c3 covers b1 and d1, and the rook that is about to arrive will cover b2 and d2.","82":"The rook moves to c2: checkmate! The rook on c2 is guarded by the bishop on b3, so the king cannot take it; the knight on c3 covers b1 and d1, and the rook covers b2 and d2. Three pieces, a rook, a bishop and a knight, make the mating net, and the other bishop helped chase the king here. White’s king was hunted from g1 all the way to c1 on the other side of the board, and White’s queen could do nothing to the end. Byrne could have resigned earlier, but it is said he played on to checkmate out of respect for the boy’s brilliant play. The knight leap on move 11, the decision to give up the queen on move 17, the “windmill” that won the pieces back, and a mating net woven by small pieces: after this game, Fischer became U.S. champion at 14 and world champion in 1972."}},"oshiro1849":{"t":"Shusaku’s First Castle Game","w":"Honinbo Shusaku","b":"Yasui Sanchi IX","ev":"Castle Game (the yearly game at Edo Castle)","d":"1849","pl":"Japan (Edo)","lead":"The Castle Games were the yearly showcase where players of the official go houses played at the shogun’s castle. Twenty-year-old Shusaku made his debut this year and won with Black by 11 points — the start of a perfect record of 19 wins in 19 Castle Games.","c":{"0":"Edo, 1849. Black is 20-year-old Honinbo Shusaku; White is Yasui Sanchi, the ninth head of the Yasui house. The Castle Games were official games that players of the go houses (the four families Honinbo, Inoue, Yasui and Hayashi) played for the shogun, once a year at Edo Castle. It was the grandest stage for a house player, and a loss could stain the name of his house. In practice the game was often played beforehand in another residence and only replayed at the castle, or so it is said. Shusaku was born on the island of Innoshima in Bingo (today Onomichi, Hiroshima). The year before, he had been named heir of the Honinbo house, and this was his first Castle Game. From here he would play 19 Castle Games and win all 19, never losing once. There was no komi in those days, so only the territory on the board decides the result.","1":"Black 1 (R16) is the komoku in the top right. Komoku is the point where the third and fourth lines from the corner cross, a move that makes it easy to take the corner as territory. With Black, Shusaku often began with komoku, and the opening later called the “Shusaku style” was also built from komoku. With no komi, Black, who moves first, wins simply by keeping a small lead safely to the end. Shusaku knew this well and played calm go that never forced things. In the Shusaku style, Black’s seventh move, a diagonal step from a komoku called the “Shusaku kosumi”, is famous, and today’s go AIs have also re-evaluated it as a good move. In this game too, at move 45, Black plays a kosumi from the top-right komoku.","3":"White 2 (R4) is the komoku in the bottom right, and Black 3 (D17) the komoku in the top left. Black now holds both corners along the top. The top side between those two corners becomes a place Black can later turn into territory. White does not take the remaining corner at once; with the next move he approaches one of Black’s corners instead. Playing two komoku in a row is the same idea as the start of the later Shusaku style. By the way, in the spring of this year, 1849, the ukiyo-e artist Katsushika Hokusai, famous for “Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji”, died in Edo.","4":"White 4 (C15) approaches Black’s D17 in the top left (a kakari). It is the “small knight’s approach”, coming in at a knight’s-move distance, and it stops Black from enclosing the corner widely. Before taking the empty bottom-left corner, White goes to disturb Black’s plan for the top. Black can choose to answer quietly or to pincer. White’s Yasui house was one of the four go houses. The second head, Yasui Santetsu, later took the name Shibukawa Shunkai and is also known as the astronomer who made the first calendar created in Japan (the Jōkyō calendar).","5":"Black 5 (C11) pincers the approaching white C15 from below. With three empty points between, on the third line, it is a “three-space low pincer”. It stops White from spreading down the left side and making a base (a foothold for living). Pincering instead of answering quietly is an active choice that gives White no easy time. In the Shusaku style, move 5 would also be a komoku in another corner, but in this game Black answers the approach at once with a pincer, and here the game leaves the Shusaku-style pattern. By the way, the name “Honinbo” comes from the small temple at Jakkō-ji in Kyoto where the first head, Honinbo Sansa, lived.","6":"White 6 (F16) approaches D17 from the top side as well. With C15 on the left and F16 on the right, White now sandwiches Black’s corner stone from both sides (a double approach). Instead of answering the pincer directly, White puts pressure back on Black’s corner. Black will have to split one of the white stones off. The double approach is a shape found in joseki books, and the sequence that follows depends on which white stone Black turns toward. As a child, Shusaku is said to have had his talent recognized by the Asano family, lords of Mihara Castle (today Mihara, Hiroshima), and to have studied go with their support.","7":"Black 7 (D15) pokes out between the two white stones. Played right next to C15, it keeps C15 and F16 from joining hands. If Black did not play here, White could play around D15 and shut Black’s corner into a small space. From here the stones start to collide in the top left.","9":"White 8 (D14) blocks the head of D15, and Black 9 (E15) extends sideways so two black stones stand in a row. D15 and E15 now have plenty of liberties, so there is no danger of a quick capture. The row also works as a wall that keeps White’s F16 apart from the white stones in the top left. In 1837, at the age of eight, Shusaku went to Edo and became a pupil of the Honinbo house under Honinbo Jowa. Jowa is said to have praised him as “the greatest go talent in 150 years”.","12":"White 10 (C16), Black 11 (E17), White 12 (C17). White lines up stones along the edge and takes the top-left corner and the left side. In return for giving up the corner, Black has D17, E17, D15 and E15 facing the top. White’s F16 on the top is almost surrounded by black stones and has been left on its own. It is a result both sides can accept. This result closely resembles a joseki in which, after a double approach, the corner goes to White and the outside to Black. Across the seas, in this year of 1849 people were flocking to California in search of gold (the Gold Rush), and in Europe the composer Chopin died.","13":"Black 13 (J17) makes a wide extension along the top. The idea is to use the outside stones from the top left and turn the top side into Black’s area. It also closes off the right of White’s F16, which makes that stone even harder to move. F16 stays on the board to the end, but as a dead stone inside Black’s territory. In the same year, 1849, the British warship Mariner entered Edo Bay, carried out surveys and left. It was a time when foreign ships were coming near Japan more and more often; four years later Perry’s “black ships” would arrive.","14":"White 14 (P17) approaches Black’s R16 in the top right from the top side (a kakari). It stops, from the right, Black’s plan to make the whole top into a big area with J17 and R16. Black would like to answer here, but Shusaku chooses another place next. Shusaku had become heir the year before, in 1848, and is said to have married a daughter of his teacher’s house at that time.","15":"Black 15 (C5) goes to the last empty corner, the bottom left (a mokuhazushi). Black does not answer White’s P17 at once and takes the open corner first. A corner is easy to turn into territory with few stones, so in the opening it is a very big place. Black judged that R16 would not get into trouble even if the top right waited a little. Now all four corners of the board are filled. The mokuhazushi stands a little toward the outside of the corner and was often used in Edo-period go. The players of the go houses received stipends from the shogunate, and the Castle Game was the most important place to show their skill.","16":"White 16 (E4) is an approach that comes straight at Black’s C5. It keeps Black from enclosing the bottom-left corner easily, and stones begin to collide here too. White will now line up stones outside the corner and build a shape facing the bottom side. Around this time the shogunate was ordering stronger coastal defences, in preparation for the foreign ships coming near Japan.","19":"Black 17 (D3), White 18 (E3), Black 19 (D2). Black places stones low in the corner and comes down to the second line, securing the corner territory firmly. White lines up E4 and E3 outside and gains power toward the bottom side. Black takes territory, White takes outside strength. Making a small but certainly living shape first is Shusaku’s solid way of playing.","20":"White 20 (C7) closes in on Black’s corner from above. It stops Black from coming up the left side and aims to make the left side White’s area. Black’s corner is not fully alive yet, so Black needs to get his head out upward.","23":"Black 21 (C6), White 22 (D7), Black 23 (E5). After stretching upward, Black plays right on top of White’s E4 and blocks White’s head. E4 and E3 now find it hard to come out upward, and Black’s corner stones gain a way to spread outward. The stones are tangled together, and the shape in the bottom left is gradually settling.","24":"White 24 (C13) extends White’s left-side stones down from C15. It strengthens White’s top-left stones and at the same time presses Black’s move-5 stone C11 from above. C11 is now caught between white stones above and below, and Black must decide whether to treat it lightly or to save it.","26":"Black 25 (E6) stretches upward to strengthen the corner stones, and White 26 (E7) blocks its head. The bottom left is settling into a shape where Black has the corner territory and White an outside wall of C7, D7 and E7. White’s wall keeps watch over both the left side and the bottom side.","27":"Black 27 (F15) is played right below White’s lone F16 on the top. F16 has its way out downward blocked and is left with only three liberties (empty points next to it). Black’s stones on the top also join into one solid group, and the top side area becomes clearer. A quiet move, but an important one that leads to the later territory on top.","28":"White 28 (G7) jumps two points right from E7 and extends the outside wall. White is spreading his influence (power that is not yet territory) outside the bottom left, looking at the bottom side and the centre. Black’s left-side stone C11 is close to this white power and starts to feel a little lonely.","29":"Black 29 (F11) jumps two points right from C11 and gives the left-side black stone a partner. It keeps C11 from being attacked on its own and also becomes a foothold for heading into the centre. At the same time it limits White’s power from below from spreading widely into the centre.","30":"White 30 (H4) extends two points to the right along the bottom from E4 and E3. White begins to build an area on the bottom side and firms up the outside of Black’s bottom-left corner. The left half of the board is now coloured in: Black has the corners and the top, White the left and bottom sides. Next Black heads for White’s bottom-right corner.","32":"Black 31 (O3) approaches White’s R4 in the bottom right from a little further away (a large knight’s approach). White 32 (L3) at once pincers that black stone from the left. L3 also looks to connect with White’s H4 on the bottom, and White would like the whole bottom side as his area. Black’s O3 is caught between White’s corner and L3, and Black must decide how to play here.","33":"Black 33 (R3) attaches right below White’s komoku R4, on the 3-3 point (the point on the third line from both edges). Instead of running away with the pincered O3, Black goes into White’s corner to break up White’s shape. If White blocks, Black will line up stones on the outside. It is the start of Shusaku’s plan: give the corner territory to White and gain outside thickness (a strong wall) in return.","36":"White 34 (S3) blocks R3 from the corner side, and Black 35 (Q3) stretches left toward O3. White 36 (S2) guards the bottom of the corner. Black’s R3 and Q3 now lead to the outside of White’s corner, while White chooses to live inside the corner. From here Black will advance upward along the outside of the white stones.","37":"Black 37 (Q5) jumps one point up from Q3. It holds down the outside (left side) of White’s R4 and goes to stop White from spreading up the right side. If this continues, White can only crawl upward along a line close to the right edge. Black is getting ready to build a wall.","42":"White 38 (R5), Black 39 (Q6), White 40 (R6), Black 41 (Q7), White 42 (R7). White crawls up the third line from the right edge one step at a time, while Black lines up stones on the fourth line just outside. Crawling along the third line gives White some territory, but it is thin and small. The black stones on the fourth line, on the other hand, grow into a strong wall facing the centre and the bottom side.","43":"With Black 43 (Q8), a thick black wall now stretches up the right side from Q3 to Q8. White has the bottom-right corner and a thin strip of territory along the right edge, while Black has a big outward-facing power. This wall will later become the base of a large black area on the right, and it also glares at White’s L3 on the bottom. It shows clearly the way Shusaku liked to play: give up territory to gain thickness. The result so far seems slightly easier for Black to play.","44":"White 44 (L17) plays into the top between Black’s J17 and White’s P17. The idea is to stop, in advance, Black making a huge area on the top to go with the wall on the right. White’s right-side stones are not yet capped from above, but White judged the size of the top more important. Rather than attack this stone at once, Black first sets the top right in order.","45":"Black 45 (Q15) steps one point diagonally down from R16 (a kosumi). Here Black finally answers White’s approach at P17. It strengthens the corner stone and at the same time turns toward the right side, aiming to work together with the Q8 wall below. A well-thought-out move that guards the top-right corner while keeping the right side wide in view.","48":"White 46 (P15), Black 47 (P14), White 48 (O15). White bumps into Q15 and makes shape on the top side, while Black stretches downward toward the right side. White’s stones in the top right (P17, P15, O15) put down roots on the top, and Black gets stones that head for the right side. Each side has moved in the direction it wanted.","49":"Black 49 (R8) blocks the head of the white stones that have crawled along the third line (a hane). White’s R4–R7 can no longer extend up the right side. White’s bottom-right stones must now live in a small space. Black’s wall now runs from Q3 to R8 and becomes even stronger, and it grows likely that the upper right side will be Black’s area. You could say Shusaku left White’s invasion at move 44 alone for a while just to play this move.","50":"White 50 (R18) slides in under Black’s top-right corner on the second line. Linked to White’s P17, it trims a little off Black’s corner territory. Black’s R16 and Q15 now have a smaller corner, but they are looking down toward the right side instead. White has made his living shape on the top clear.","51":"Black 51 (P13) stretches down from P14 and sets up a large area on the right side. With P13 and P14 above and the Q8–R8 wall below, the shape seems to enclose a wide space on the right. If White does not go in, it will become a big black territory. White has to enter the right side somewhere. From here, when and where White enters Black’s area becomes the focus of the game.","52":"White 52 (D10) plays in under Black’s C11 and F11 on the left side. With the white wall in the bottom left at his back, White goes to attack Black’s still-weak left-side stones. C11 and F11 are only two points apart and not yet connected. White wants to split and attack them and widen his left-side area.","56":"Black 53 (D11), White 54 (E11), Black 55 (E12), White 56 (E10). Black connects to C11 with D11, and White cuts between C11–D11 and F11 with E11. Black 55 puts E11 in atari (one move from capture), but White connects to E10 and gets away. Now Black is split into three: C11–D11 on the left, F11 on the right and E12 above. For a while the left side becomes a difficult place where weak stones of both sides are tangled.","57":"Black 57 (B14) slides in on the second line under White’s top-left stones (C13–C17). It goes to take away the room those white stones need to live (the space to make eyes). It also aims to widen the space where Black’s C11 and D11 can live along the left edge. Now White’s own stones are weaker too, so White must fight while defending.","60":"White 58 (D12), Black 59 (C12), White 60 (D13). White wedges between Black’s stones with D12, but Black puts it in atari with C12, and White connects with D13 to the white stones above. Black’s C11, C12 and D11 become one group, and White’s top-left stones come together with D12–D14. Both sides have connected their stones and tidied up their shapes for now.","63":"Black 61 (B13), White 62 (C14), Black 63 (B10). Black lines up B13 and B14 on the left edge, and White connects with C14, making his top-left stones solidly one. Black 63 places a stone on the left edge below C11, stopping White from coming in from underneath. Black’s left-side stones have now secured room to live along the edge. Next Black turns his eyes to the bottom side a little further away.","64":"White 64 (F12) puts Black’s E12 in atari. E12 has only one liberty left at E13, and F11 is down to two liberties. Black does not save E12; it stays inside White’s territory to the end as a sacrificed stone. Shusaku made a clear-cut decision: playing a bigger place is worth more than saving one stone.","65":"Black 65 (G3) plays in between White’s E3 and H4 on the bottom side. It enters the area White is trying to build there and aims to split the white stones in two. E4 and E3 sit outside Black’s bottom-left corner, and H4 is in the middle of the bottom side, so if they are cut apart both become a bit weaker. After defending on the left, Black at once strikes back inside White’s area.","70":"White 66 (H3), Black 67 (G4), White 68 (G2), Black 69 (F2), White 70 (H2). White gets under the black stones with H3 and G2, and Black stands up and then reduces G2’s liberties with F2. White connects with H2 and gathers his stones on the right of the bottom (H4, H3, H2, G2) into one group. Black’s G3 and G4 have pushed in between the white stones, but they still have no base and are not strong.","71":"Black 71 (G5) stretches one more point up from G4. Black’s three stones G3–G5 now stand in a vertical line between White’s E4–E3 on the left and H4–H2 on the right. The two white groups now find it hard to join, but these three black stones are also surrounded by white and are not yet safe. Later these black stones become the seed of a big fight.","72":"White 72 (F13) adds a stone above F12 to tidy its shape. F12 was a weak stone with only two liberties, so White reinforced it before Black could aim at it. White’s stones running from the top left down the left side can now come out toward the centre more easily. Next Black turns to White’s L17 on the top.","75":"Black 73 (K17) attaches right onto White’s L17, and White 74 (L16) stands downward, making a shape to escape into the centre. Then Black 75 (H14): Black pushes his top area firmly down toward the centre. This stone also sits where it hinders White’s left stones (F12, F13) from linking with the white stones on the top (L16, L17). It aims to kill two birds with one stone: widen the top and keep the opponent’s stones apart.","76":"White 76 (P11) finally jumps into Black’s area on the right side. It lands between P13–P14 above and the Q8 wall below, the place Black most wants as territory. Whether this white stone lives or is captured will change the territory count a great deal. With the strong wall on the right, Black is the attacker, but trying too hard to capture could open a weakness somewhere. The next few dozen moves are a big turning point of this game.","77":"Black 77 (Q11) attaches right next to White’s P11. Answering from the side of his own wall, Black stops White from making a base (a place to live) along the right edge. This leaves White only the way out into the centre, where Black has few strong stones. Black chose a sensible attack: chase White out while guarding territory. Had Black attacked from the centre side, White could have lived along the right edge and Black’s territory would have shrunk a lot.","80":"White 78 (Q10) hanes below Black’s Q11, and Black 79 (Q12) stretches upward, making a shape that links toward P13. White 80 (N10) jumps out into the centre from P11 with a knight’s-move shape. White gives up living on the right edge and runs to the centre to break out of Black’s net. Black’s right-side territory is protected, but whether Black can capture the white stones is not yet clear.","81":"Black 81 (R10) goes round below White’s Q10, toward the right edge. Q10 is left with only two liberties (P10 and Q9) and can no longer put down roots on the right edge. Black firmly encloses the right-side territory all the way to the edge, while turning Q10 into a weak stone he can capture whenever he likes. In fact, Black does capture it later, at move 233.","84":"White 82 (J14) bumps into the right of Black’s H14, and Black 83 (H15) stretches upward, making an escape route toward the black stones on the top. White 84 (H13) holds down the bottom of H14. With J14 White looks to link with L16 on the top, and with H13 with F13 on the left. Black’s H14 and H15 are squeezed by white stones, but they are close to the black stones above and are in no immediate trouble.","85":"Black 85 (N12) is played above White’s N10 and P11, which are running into the centre. It goes to stop those stones from moving up and linking with White’s O15 and P15 on the top. At the same time it widens Black’s area from the top right down the right side even more. A skilful way to gain territory while attacking. White now looks for an escape route to the left, through the centre.","87":"White 86 (B9) is played below Black’s B10 on the left edge. It tries to narrow the space Black’s left-side stones have to live along the edge. Black 87 (B15) stretches the other way, upward, widening the room for those black stones to live. Black now has enough length up and down along the left edge. White’s top-left stones, too, can now only go outward.","90":"White 88 (M12) bumps into the left of Black’s N12, and Black 89 (M13) holds the top, leaving M12 with two liberties. White 90 (L12) stretches left and escapes. White is heading left through the centre, looking for a way to link with his J14 and L16 stones on the top. Black presses White from above while firming up his territory on the top and top right.","91":"Black 91 (J7) jumps into the space between the bottom side and the centre. Black boldly shows himself between White’s wall at G7 and White’s H4 and L3 on the bottom. It hinders White from building a big area from the bottom up to the centre, and also aims to join hands with Black’s wall on the right. It enters White’s area, but with friendly black stones around, it is not a reckless invasion.","94":"White 92 (J15) closes in on Black’s H14 and H15 from the right, cutting them down to three liberties. Black 93 (H16) stretches upward and secures an escape route close to the black stones on the top. White 94 (N11) connects to N10 in the centre to tidy his shape and at the same time reduces Black’s N12 to two liberties. In the centre, white and black stones are now pressing each other in a complicated tangle.","99":"Black 95 (M8), White 96 (L9), Black 97 (H7), White 98 (J9). Low down, Black places M8 and H7 and widens the group of black stones around J7. White places L9 and J9 in the centre, going over the top of the black stones and hindering their connections. Then Black 99 (S8): Black extends his wall to the right edge and completely blocks the way for White’s bottom-right stones to spread up the right side. The centre is tangled, but Black’s territory on the right keeps steadily firming up.","101":"White 100 (S17) goes into Black’s top-right corner, near the edge just right of R16. White reduces the room for Black’s corner to make eyes and hopes to break the corner if possible. Black 101 (S16) holds it from below, leaving S17 with three liberties. Without panic, Black guards the corner’s shape firmly.","106":"White 102 (N3), Black 103 (N2), White 104 (M2), Black 105 (N4), White 106 (M3). On the bottom, White attaches right to the left of Black’s O3, and Black places stones below and above it, putting N3 in atari. White connects with M3 and makes L3, M3, N3 and M2 on the bottom into one strong group. Black’s O3 has also gained friends at N4 and N2 and is safe near the wall on the right. A quiet exchange in which both sides firm up the border on the bottom.","112":"Black 107 (B8), White 108 (A9), Black 109 (A10), White 110 (C10), Black 111 (B12), White 112 (C8). On the left edge, Black goes in below White’s B9, and White squeezes the black stones with A9 and C10. White 110 was a sharp move that cut both B10–A10 and C11 and its friends down to two liberties, but Black connects with B12, gathering the main left-side stones into one and removing the big danger. White 112 reduces Black’s B8 to two liberties and keeps Black’s edge stones small.","116":"Black 113 (T5), White 114 (T4), Black 115 (T7), White 116 (T2). Black places stones on the very edge of the board right next to White’s bottom-right stones, trying to reduce White’s room for eyes. White guards the edge with T4 and T2 and makes sure of a living shape in the corner. Even if Black cannot capture, this keeps White’s territory small and prepares for the yose (the endgame of settling borders).","121":"Black 117 (H9) bumps into White’s J9 in the centre, making White’s central stones heavier. White 118 (Q14) is played below Black’s Q15 in the top right, trying to cut the black stones apart. Black 119 (R17) cuts the corner stone S17 down to two liberties, and when White 120 (S18) connects, Black 121 (R15) firmly joins Q15 to R16. White’s aim is gone, and Q14 becomes a stone left inside Black’s territory. Black’s top-right stones are now one strong group.","122":"White 122 (G6) is played right on top of Black’s G5. It goes to cut the link between Black’s stones on the bottom, G3–G5, and Black’s H7 and J7 in the centre. G3–G5 were stones that had pushed in between white stones, and they are surrounded by white. If their link to the outside is cut here, these black stones will find it very hard to live. From here the fiercest fight of the game begins, from the bottom left across the bottom side.","128":"Black 123 (H5), White 124 (J5), Black 125 (K5), White 126 (H6), Black 127 (J6), White 128 (J4). Black looks for an exit to the right with H5 and K5, but White blocks the way with J5 and H6. Black 127 put J5 in atari, but White connects with J4 and makes his stones on the bottom one large group. Now Black’s four stones G3, G4, G5 and H5 have almost lost their way out.","131":"Black 129 (G8) is played above White’s G7, G6 and H6, cutting them to two liberties. White 130 (F7) connects them to the white wall on the left and removes the worry. Then Black 131 (M5): Black does not tend the four stones below right away, but lines up K5 and M5 on the right part of the bottom side, building his own shape above White’s L3. The idea is to take a big place first rather than cling to weak stones.","134":"White 132 (E2), Black 133 (D4), White 134 (F3). White goes round below Black’s corner in the bottom left, and Black strengthens his corner with D4 while cutting White’s E4, E3 and E2 down to three liberties. White 134 is a severe move: it reinforces the white stones on the left, puts Black’s F2 in atari and also cuts Black’s four stones G3–H5 down to two liberties. In the bottom left, Black’s corner, White’s stones and Black’s four stones are all entangled, in a struggle over which group has how many liberties.","137":"Black 135 (L8) is played below White’s L9 in the centre, making White’s central stones heavier. White 136 (D6) wedges in between Black’s corner stones C6 and E6 in the bottom left. If Black did not answer, his corner stones would be cut in two and the corner itself would be in danger. Black 137 (D5) connects at once, making eight corner stones into one group with eight liberties. Black chose to protect the big corner group rather than the four stones on the bottom.","138":"White 138 (F5) puts Black’s four stones G3, G4, G5 and H5 in atari. Their last liberty is F4, but Black cannot play there: everything around it is white, so the stone would have no liberties of its own. White can play F4 and capture the four stones whenever he likes. In other words, these black stones can no longer be saved. In exchange for protecting his bottom-left corner, Black has given up the four stones on the bottom. But Shusaku does not mourn; with the next move he heads for a big place in the centre.","139":"Black 139 (M9) bumps into the right of White’s L9 in the centre. L9 drops to two liberties (K9 and L10), and White has to spend a move helping his central stones. Black lost four stones on the bottom, but meanwhile he firmed up his corner and gained the move to attack White in the centre. In this trade, giving up stones to take a big place elsewhere, Black is thought not to have lost much. Calm trades like this were one of Shusaku’s strengths.","145":"White 140 (B4), Black 141 (B3), White 142 (B2), Black 143 (C3). White places stones on the edge of Black’s bottom-left corner to reduce Black’s room for eyes. Black answers calmly with B3 and C3, gathering ten corner stones into one group with seven liberties. Next, White 144 (B16) hanes above Black’s B15 on the top-left edge, narrowing the eye space of Black’s left-side stones from above, and Black 145 (A15) stops it on the edge. Both black groups still have plenty of room to live.","151":"White 146 (B6), Black 147 (B5), White 148 (A5), Black 149 (B7), White 150 (A7), Black 151 (A6). On the left edge, White puts stones between Black’s corner and Black’s B7, and a small, detailed fight begins. Black 151 plays A6, captures the single white stone B6 and at the same time puts both A5 and A7 in atari. This is a ko shape. In a ko, stones can be taken back and forth again and again, but you may not retake at once; you must first play somewhere else.","154":"White 152 (B11) is a ko threat: a threatening move elsewhere that the opponent must answer, played in order to retake the ko. It put Black’s B10 and A10 in atari, so Black 153 (A11) answers by capturing that white stone. Now White can retake with 154 (B6), capturing the black stone on A6. Play a ko threat, make the opponent answer, then retake: that is how a ko fight works. Black’s B7 and B8 are in atari again.","157":"Black 155 (A3) does not continue the ko but firms up the corner. White 156 (A8) captures Black’s two stones B7 and B8, but Black 157 (A4) puts both White’s B4 and A5 in atari. As a result of this ko, White has taken two black stones on the left edge, and Black has made his big bottom-left corner safe. White’s B4 and A5 are left behind inside Black’s corner and later become the seed of another small ko.","162":"White 158 (L10) extends White’s L9 in the centre, which was short of liberties, and saves it. Black 159 (H11) strengthens Black’s stones on the left of the centre, and White 160 (N13) puts Black’s N12 in atari. Black does not save N12; instead, 161 (N14) puts White’s N13 in atari, and White 162 (O12) captures N12. In return for handing over one stone, Black firmly connects his top-right stones with N14 and gains a shape that holds down White’s O15 and P15 on the top. Shusaku keeps making decisions that do not cling to small stones.","167":"Black 163 (O14) lines up below White’s O15 and P15 on the top, cutting them to three liberties. White 164 (N17) defends, as if to connect the white stones on the top. Black 165 (R2) goes into the eye space of White’s corner in the bottom right, White 166 (S1) answers, and Black 167 (O2) connects O3, O2 and N2 on the bottom. The big fights have calmed down, and the game is moving into the stage of settling borders all over the board.","171":"White 168 (O8) heads down from White’s central stones and peeps into Black’s area in the bottom right. Black 169 (O7) closes the way below it, and with White 170 (N8) and Black 171 (N7), whenever White stretches sideways, Black lines up below. White has widened the shape of his central stones, but Black has not given up a single step of his area from the bottom right to the bottom side. Black’s M8, L8 and M9 also have five liberties and are not yet in danger.","179":"From here on it is yose. White 172 (K18), Black 173 (J18) and White 174 (L19) settle the border on the top; Black 175 (D18) in the top left; and White 176 (D1), Black 177 (C1), White 178 (E1) and Black 179 (C2) on the bottom-left edge. In yose, players compare how many points each move gains and play the biggest places first. Black 177 put D1 in atari, White connected with E1, and with Black 179 the black corner is firmly closed. Solid yose continues, with neither side making a big mistake.","185":"White 180 (C18) and Black 181 (J19) are also yose on the top. White 182 (K4) cuts Black’s K5 down to two liberties on the bottom, and Black 183 (L4) wedges between White’s stones on the bottom, going to reduce the liberties of L3, M3, N3 and M2. White 184 (M14) put Black’s M13 in atari, but Black does not save it; with 185 (N15) he chooses instead to cut White’s O15 and P15 on the top down to two liberties. Black valued attacking the opponent’s weak stones more than a small stone.","189":"White 186 (T16), Black 187 (T15) and White 188 (T17) settle the border on the top-right edge. Black 187 put T16 in atari, White connected with T17, and White’s stones in the top-right corner are left in a small living shape. Then Black 189 (R14): it puts the white stone Q14, left inside, in atari, removing any power of that white stone inside Black’s right-side territory. Black’s big territory from the top right down the right side is now nearly settled.","192":"White 190 (H10) wedges between Black’s H9 and H11 on the left of the centre. H9 drops to two liberties, and there is a danger that Black’s central stones may be cut apart. Black 191 (G10) puts H10 in atari, and White 192 (J10) connects and escapes. On the left of the centre, black and white stones remain cut into each other, and neither side is completely safe yet.","199":"Black 193 (O17) is quietly placed between White’s P17 and N17 on the top. When White 194 (O18) puts it in atari, Black 195 (O16) stretches and in turn puts White’s O15 and P15 in atari. White 196 (P16) connects to save them and at the same time puts Black’s O16 and O17 in atari, but Black 197 (N16) connects and escapes, leaving White’s N17 with two liberties. When White 198 (M17) defends, Black 199 (M15) puts M14 in atari. On the top, Black puts white stones in atari one after another while connecting his own, and pushes into White’s area.","204":"White 200 (L14) connects and saves M14. Black 201 (M1) plays on the very edge of the bottom, cutting White’s L3, M3, N3 and M2 down to three liberties. When White 202 (L1) puts M1 in atari, Black 203 (N1) connects, and the black stones join the black stones on the right. White 204 (K2) tidies the shape of White’s stones on the bottom so Black cannot get in. The border on the bottom has been settled in a way that gains Black a little at a time.","208":"Black 205 (Q18) is played above White’s corner stones R18, S18, S17, T17 and T16 in the top right, cutting them to three liberties. When White 206 (P18) connects the white stones on the top and leaves Q18 with two liberties, Black 207 (Q17) connects and gathers the top-right black stones. White 208 (Q19) plays on the very top edge and keeps the living shape of his corner stones. The top-right corner is settled: White lives small, and Black firmly surrounds it.","211":"Black 209 (J12) places a stone in the centre to strengthen the links between Black’s central stones. White 210 (G14) approaches Black’s H14, H15 and H16 from the left, leaving them four liberties. Black 211 (G15) connects with Black’s stones on the top and cuts G14 down to two liberties. The black stones reaching from the top into the centre have now become one large group.","219":"From White 212 (F9) to Black 219 (G11), Black and White chase each other on the left of the centre. With Black 213 (G9), White 214 (K8), Black 215 (K7), White 216 (J8) and Black 217 (H8), each side reduces the other’s liberties. White 218 (F10) put Black’s F11 in atari, but Black 219 (G11) connects, and Black’s central stones become one large group of twelve. It is a close exchange in which both the black and the white central stones must watch their liberties to survive.","226":"White 220 (D19), Black 221 (E19) and White 222 (C19) are yose on the top-left edge. Then Black 223 (P10): it puts White’s Q10 on the right side in atari and also cuts P11 down to two liberties. White 224 (O10) in turn puts Black’s P10 in atari, Black 225 (P9) stretches and escapes, and White 226 (P8) cuts Black’s P9 and P10 down to two liberties. On the right side it has become a tight reading battle: will Black capture Q10, or must he protect his own P9 and P10?","233":"Black 227 (M10) is played between White’s N10, O10, N11 and L9, L10 in the centre, reducing the liberties of both. From White 228 (J11) to Black 231 (J13), on the left of the centre, Black puts H13 in atari while gathering his central stones into one large group of fifteen. White 232 (O9) put Black’s P9 and P10 in atari, but now Black 233 (Q9): it captures the single white stone Q10, and P9 and P10 jump to nine liberties, safe at a stroke. Q10, a weak stone ever since move 81, is finally captured here, and Black’s territory on the right side is protected in every corner.","238":"White 234 (G13) connects and saves H13. Black 235 (O13) puts N13 in atari, and when White 236 (N12) connects, White’s central stones become a large group of thirteen. Black 237 (K13) links his central stones further, and White 238 (L13) captures the single black stone M13. In the centre, both the big white group and the big black group now clearly have living shapes. What remains are small yose and ko.","242":"Black 239 (A6) captures the single white stone A5, and the left edge becomes a ko again. This was the stone Black had put in atari with A4 at move 157. White 240 (D16) is a ko threat: it cuts Black’s D17, E17 and D18 in the top left down to three liberties and threatens to cut the black stones apart. Black 241 (E16) answers by connecting, gathering the black stones on the top into one. White 242 (A5) now retakes the ko.","245":"Black 243 (P12) is a ko threat that puts White’s P11 on the right side in atari. But White 244 (A6) ignores it and connects at A6, ending the ko on the left edge. Black does not take P11 at once; with 245 (A16) he places a stone on the top-left edge, reducing White’s top-left stones to five liberties. He probably judged this bigger than capturing one stone. White later connects P11 with 266 (O11).","248":"White 246 (A17) cuts Black’s stones on the top-left edge to four liberties. Then Black 247 (F8): a sharp move that cuts White’s F9, F10, E10, D10, E11 and C10 on the left down to three liberties. White 248 (E8) connects them to the white wall in the bottom left and protects them. Black’s attack is parried, but by making White spend a move on defence, Black gets to the next yose first.","253":"Black 249 (M4) cuts White’s L3, M3, N3 and M2 on the bottom down to two liberties. White 250 (R1) reduces the liberties of Black’s R2, R3 and Q3 on the bottom-right edge, and Black 251 (Q1) gets in below that white stone. White 252 (Q4) cuts Q3, R3 and R2 down to two liberties, and Black 253 (P4) answers by reducing the liberties on the side of White’s R4. In the bottom-right corner it has become a close race in which White and Black take away each other’s liberties one by one.","259":"White 254 (P7) goes inside Black’s wall in the bottom right, and Black 255 (P6) stops it. White 256 (M11) cuts Black’s M10, M9, M8 and L8 in the centre down to three liberties. Then Black 257 (S7): it cuts White’s R4–R7 and Q4 in the bottom right down to three liberties. White 258 (S5) put Black’s T5 in atari, but with Black 259 (S6) six white stones are in atari (their only liberty left is S4). Late in the game, White’s bottom-right stones are suddenly in unexpected danger.","263":"White 260 (T6) captures Black’s T5 and gives the white stones more liberties. A ko now appears on the bottom-right edge. Black 261 (K19) is a ko threat that puts White’s K18 on the top in atari, and White 262 (L18) answers by connecting. When Black 263 (T5) retakes the ko, the six white stones are in atari again. If White loses this ko he will lose a lot of stones in the bottom right, so he must defend with his next move.","267":"With White 264 (S4) connecting, White’s bottom-right stones live with two eyes at T3 and T1. Black 265 (J16) and White 266 (O11) take small points, Black 267 (T6) fills in where the ko was, and the game ends after 267 moves. Black wins by 11 points. Shusaku had the thick wall he built in the bottom right by making White crawl along the third line (up to move 43) at his back, and set up a big area on the right side. From move 76 White went into the right side and ran into the centre; the white stones did live, but Black, while chasing them, firmed up his territory on the top and the right, and at move 233 he finally captured Q10, the stone he had been aiming at since move 81. In the bottom left Black gave up four stones, but in return he protected his corner and got to the big places in the centre first. No great adventures: he attacked his opponent’s stones and naturally increased his territory, a truly Shusaku-like way to win. From this game on, the 20-year-old Shusaku would build a record still told today, 19 wins in 19 Castle Games, until his early death in 1862, while still in his thirties."}},"shilin":{"t":"The Shilin Guangji game","w":"Red (name not recorded)","b":"Black (name not recorded)","ev":"The everyday encyclopedia Shilin Guangji: “a game won after giving the first move, with same-side cannons”","d":"13th century","pl":"China (Song–Yuan)","lead":"This record comes from the Shilin Guangji, an everyday encyclopedia compiled by Chen Yuanjing around the end of the Song dynasty and the start of the Yuan. It is said to be one of the oldest complete xiangqi game records that survive. It is a model game in which the stronger side lets the other move first, and in only nine moves Black’s horse traps Red’s chariot.","c":{"0":"This record is more than 700 years old, from the time between the Song and Yuan dynasties. It appears in the games chapter of the encyclopedia Shilin Guangji, and it is said to be one of the oldest complete xiangqi games we still have. The players’ names are not recorded. The title says the stronger player “gave the first move” to the weaker one, so this is a lesson showing how Red, who moves first, loses. Red is at the bottom and starts. Xiangqi is thought to have taken almost its present form, with the river and the cannons, in the Song dynasty, especially the Southern Song. Chen Yuanjing, who compiled the Shilin Guangji, lived at the end of the Southern Song. Later, at the end of the 13th century, the Mongol Yuan dynasty overthrew the Song. While dynasties were changing, a book for everyday life already carried a model xiangqi game.","1":"Red moves the right cannon to the middle file. This is the Central Cannon, aimed straight at Black’s general, and still one of the strongest openings today. It shows that this opening was already played 700 years ago.","2":"Black answers with a cannon to the middle too, so the two cannons face each other. When both sides move the cannon on the same side, it is called Same Direction Cannons. Cannon against cannon has been a popular fight for centuries. Bringing the cannon from the opposite side to the opponent is called \"Opposite Direction Cannons\", and old records are full of games with both Same and Opposite Direction Cannons. It was an age that loved fast fights where the cannons clash right away. In professional games today, Black usually answers the Central Cannon with the \"Screen Horses\", and Same Direction Cannons is said to be less common than it used to be.","3":"Red jumps a horse out to guard the middle soldier. That soldier is under attack from Black’s cannon, so it needs a defender. The horse is also getting ready to come forward.","4":"Black does the same and guards the middle soldier with a horse. Both sides are building a firm base first.","5":"Red moves the right chariot one step sideways onto an open file. The chariot is the strongest piece on the board, so it is good to get it working early.","6":"Black brings out the other horse and leaves the chariots at home for now. The plan is to bring pieces forward first. But a chariot that comes out late can be hard to use in the attack later. Model games in old books often show a mistake to avoid. This one teaches that even if Black is slow to bring out the chariots, Black can still win by using the other pieces well.","7":"Red’s chariot rushes across the river into Black’s camp. It wants to threaten Black’s pieces, but Red’s other pieces are still at home, so this attack is a bit hasty. Bringing out the left horse or chariot first was also possible.","8":"Black pushes the soldier on file 3 one step. This opens a path for the horse so it can jump forward later. Black is almost inviting Red’s chariot to come.","9":"Red’s chariot takes a soldier and now eyes the black horse beyond it. Winning a piece and threatening another looks great. But the chariot has run far ahead with no friends around it. A chariot alone in the enemy camp is easy to chase.","10":"Black’s horse jumps forward. It escapes from the chariot and moves toward Red’s camp at the same time — two jobs with one move. This horse will be the star of the game.","11":"Red pulls the chariot back one step to attack the horse from the side. A chariot can capture from far away, so the horse cannot rest. Still, this move does not help Red develop any other piece.","12":"Black’s horse goes further forward and crosses the river into Red’s camp. Each time it runs from the chariot, it gets closer to Red’s general.","13":"Red pushes a soldier to open a path for Red’s own horse. The idea is to bring that horse out and trade it for Black’s horse. But this gives Black time to go even further.","14":"Black lifts an elephant and attacks Red’s chariot. Using a defending piece to chase the enemy chariot away is very efficient. Red must move the chariot again, and Black gains another move.","15":"Red’s chariot slides across and takes another soldier. Red is now two soldiers up, but this is the move that loses. The chariot is now far from its general and cannot come back to defend. Red needed a move to guard against the horse, such as bringing the chariot home.","16":"Black’s horse jumps deeper and attacks Red’s chariot. And there is more: if the horse jumps once again, it will check Red’s general and at the same time attack the chariot in Red’s corner. One horse has made two threats at once.","17":"Red chases the horse with the chariot. But the horse can no longer be stopped. Around Red’s general there is nobody left to help.","18":"The horse leaps in with check! It also attacks the chariot in Red’s corner, so when the general steps away, the chariot will be lost. Only nine moves, but the game teaches what happens to a chariot that runs ahead alone, and why pieces should fight together. Players 700 years ago were thinking about the same things on the same board."}},"qima13":{"t":"The Thirteen-Move Horse Sacrifice (Juzhongmi)","w":"Red (name not recorded)","b":"Black (name not recorded)","ev":"Juzhongmi, Volume 1, Game 1","d":"1632 (published)","pl":"China (Ming)","lead":"This is the very first game in the Juzhongmi (“The Secret in the Tangerine”), a xiangqi book published by Zhu Jinzhen of the Ming dynasty in 1632. Red lets Black capture a horse for free, rushes in while Black is busy, and mates in thirteen moves. Under the name “The Thirteen-Move Horse Sacrifice” it is still loved almost 400 years later.","c":{"0":"This is Game 1 of the Juzhongmi, a book printed in 1632, near the end of the Ming dynasty. Together with the later Plum Blossom Manual, it is called one of the two great classics of xiangqi. This is not a real match but a model game written to teach a plan, so there are no players’ names. Red is at the bottom and moves first, and the game is over in just thirteen moves. 1632 was the 5th year of the Chongzhen era of the Ming. Rebellions were breaking out across the land, just 12 years before the Ming fell (1644). In Japan it was the early Edo period, 25 years after the oldest shogi record, Sōkei against Sansa. The name \"Ju Zhong Mi\" (Secrets inside the Tangerine) is said to come from a Tang-dynasty tale: when a huge tangerine was cut open, old men were found inside enjoying a board game.","1":"Red moves the right cannon to the middle. This is the Central Cannon, staring straight at Black’s general. The Juzhongmi is famous for studying how to attack with this opening. In this book the author, Zhu Jinzhen, wrote down move by move how to attack with the Central Cannon. The later Plum Blossom Manual (Meihua Pu), compiled by Wang Zaiyue in the Qing dynasty, is known for the opposite: answering the Central Cannon with the \"Screen Horses\". Ju Zhong Mi for attack, Meihua Pu for defence: these two books are the roots of today's Central Cannon openings.","2":"Black also moves the cannon on the same side to the middle. This is Same Direction Cannons, where the cannons clash head-on. Each side is aiming at the other’s middle soldier.","3":"Red jumps a horse out to guard the middle soldier. It protects the soldier the black cannon is aiming at, and the horse starts to work too.","4":"Black also guards the middle soldier with a horse. So far both sides have the same shape.","5":"Red moves the right chariot just one step forward. Now it can run sideways next move. A chariot that moves sideways like this is called a “horizontal chariot”, and it can quickly swing to either wing.","6":"Black moves the chariot one step sideways so it can drive straight up the board. This is a “straight chariot”. Horizontal chariot against straight chariot — two different ways of using the chariot face off. \"Same Direction Cannons, horizontal chariot against straight chariot\" is a standard Same Direction Cannons shape that appears again and again in the old manuals. Even today it is one of the first branches you meet when you study this opening.","7":"Red’s horizontal chariot swings over to a file on the left. That file runs right beside Black’s advisor, where Black’s defence is thin. Red is getting ready to break in from here.","8":"Black’s straight chariot crosses the river and goes deep into Red’s camp. Black wants to fight back with an attack of its own. But it is a move that hurries to attack before defending.","9":"Red’s chariot drives deep into Black’s camp. It stands on the elephant’s “eye”, the point an elephant must pass through, so Black’s elephant cannot move. Right next to the advisor, it is now very close to the general.","10":"Black brings the other horse out to the edge. Part of the idea is to stop Red’s chariot from sliding in sideways.","11":"Here is the horse sacrifice! Red lifts the other chariot one step. This leaves the horse in the left corner with no defender, so Black’s cannon can take it for free. But in return for one horse, Red gains time to bring both chariots into the attack quickly.","12":"Black’s cannon takes Red’s horse. It looks like Black has won a piece. But this cannon is now far from home and cannot help defend. It is said that strengthening the defence by moving up an advisor, instead of capturing, was better here.","13":"Red’s cannon moves forward. Using Black’s middle cannon as a “screen” to jump over, it now aims at Black’s horse beyond it. The cannon is a strange piece that captures by jumping over exactly one piece.","14":"Black retreats the horse to escape. But that horse was the important piece guarding the middle soldier. By running away, it leaves a hole in Black’s centre. Black had other defences, but this was the worst choice.","15":"Red’s cannon takes the middle soldier — check! With Black’s horse gone, the centre was wide open. From here Red’s attack rushes forward.","16":"Black moves an advisor up to block in front of the general. This is the only good way to stop the check.","17":"Red’s second chariot arrives on the same file. Two chariots lined up on one file are extremely strong. The plan is to break into the back of Black’s camp.","18":"Black’s general steps to the side. Otherwise it would be stared at straight down the file by Red’s two chariots.","19":"Red’s front chariot takes Black’s advisor — check! This chariot will be captured by the other advisor at once, but that is part of the plan. Red throws away a chariot to smash the guards around Black’s general. What a bold move!","20":"Black takes the chariot with the advisor. In pieces, Black is now far ahead. But the general’s house is even emptier than before.","21":"Red’s last chariot moves to the general’s file — check again. Black’s general has almost no pieces left to protect it.","22":"Black puts a cannon in front of the general to block the check. Putting a piece in between like this is called interposing.","23":"Red’s chariot takes that cannon — check again. The chariot keeps capturing Black’s pieces while chasing the general into a corner.","24":"Black’s general can only go back to the middle. Every other point is covered by Red’s pieces.","25":"Finally Red’s cannon lines up in the middle, two cannons on one file — check. The front cannon is the screen and the back cannon hits the general: a “double cannon” mate. Black’s general has nowhere to go, and Red wins. By giving up just one horse, Red won in thirteen moves. For 400 years this game has been teaching that time and active pieces can matter more than the number of pieces."}},"sokei1607":{"t":"The oldest shogi record that survives","w":"Ōhashi Sōkei (I)","b":"Hon'inbō Sansa","ev":"The oldest game on record","d":"June 1607","pl":"Japan (the place is not recorded)","lead":"Of all shogi games written down move by move, this is the oldest one we still have. Sente is Sōkei, who later founded the Ōhashi house of shogi masters. Gote is Sansa, founder of the Hon'inbō house of Go, who was also a strong shogi player. Sōkei wins in 133 moves.","c":{"0":"Welcome, I am Musowl. This game was played in 1607, just as the Edo period was beginning. Sente, Ōhashi Sōkei, would later be called the first Meijin, the top shogi master of the land. Gote, Hon'inbō Sansa, founded the Hon'inbō house of Go and was very good at shogi too; the two often played in front of important people. Eight of their even games survive, and Sōkei is said to have won seven of them. More than 400 years later, we can still replay every move. 1607 was the fourth year after Tokugawa Ieyasu set up his government in Edo. Having handed the title of shogun to his son Hidetada, Ieyasu began building a castle in Sunpu (today's Shizuoka City) that very year. This game comes from the moment Japan was moving from the age of warring states into the Edo period.","1":"P-7f. This opens the bishop's diagonal, and it is still the most common first move today. So shogi already began this way 400 years ago.","2":"P-3d. Gote opens the bishop's diagonal too. Now the two bishops face each other across the middle of the board. Sansa was born in Kyoto in 1559 and became a Buddhist monk at the age of eight. \"Hon'inbō\" was the name of the small hall he lived in at Jakkōji temple, and it became the name of his Go house. It is said that Oda Nobunaga once praised him as a true master.","3":"S-4h. Sente lifts the right silver. It is getting ready to bring the silver forward. Sōkei was born around 1555 and is said to have come from a family of Kyoto townspeople. In 1603 he presented a book of tsume-shogi problems he had made to the Emperor. It is said to be the oldest tsume-shogi collection on record.","5":"P-4f. Sente pushes the pawn on file 4 to make room for the silver. It goes together with S-4g next.","6":"S-4b. Gote also lifts a silver. Defenders are gathering on Gote's file 4. Five years after this game, in 1612, Ieyasu began paying the best Go and shogi players a stipend in rice. This was the start of the \"iemoto\" houses that made shogi their profession, and Sōkei's Ōhashi house went on for the whole Edo period.","10":"R-4b. Gote moves the rook to file 4. Today this is called the Fourth File Rook, one of the Ranging Rook plans where the rook swings to the left. People were already playing it 400 years ago. So this game is what we now call an \"opposing\" game: a Static Rook Sente against a Ranging Rook Gote. Four hundred years later, the Fourth File Rook is still a favourite of both professionals and amateurs. It is fun to see a popular plan already in the very oldest record.","11":"R-4h. Sente's rook goes to file 4 too, so both rooks face each other there. Sente will move this rook many more times, looking for a good place to attack. In today's words, Sente's rook stands on the \"Right Fourth File Rook\" square. The rook faces Gote's Fourth File Rook head-on and attacks straight up file 4, and it is still played today as one way to break the Fourth File Rook.","12":"B-3c. The bishop steps up to add strength to file 4. Gote has made file 4 very solid.","16":"K-6b. Gote moves the king to the left. In Ranging Rook, the king usually hides on the side away from the rook. The farther the king is from the fighting, the safer it is.","17":"K-6h. Sente moves the king to the left as well. It will go on to 7h and then 8g. In those days, named castles such as today's Mino Castle had probably not yet taken a clear shape. You can see both players feeling their way as they guard their kings.","19":"K-7h. The king reaches file 7. The next goal is to gather golds and silvers around it to build a castle.","21":"G-5h. The right gold comes toward the king. The gold is a great defender, and having one close by makes the king feel safe.","24":"S-5c. Gote brings a silver to file 5. With silvers on 5c and 4c, the center is solid too.","29":"S-6g. Sente's silver arrives near the king. With the silver on 6g and the gold on 7h, a wall is forming around the king.","30":"G-6c. Gote lifts a gold to thicken the front of the king. Both players are building their camps slowly and carefully.","33":"P-8f. Sente pushes the pawn on file 8 to make a place for the king. It is preparing K-8g.","35":"K-8g. The king goes to 8g. It is not a castle we use today: the king sits high, near the edge. We can see how players of that time thought about defence.","36":"K-8b. Gote's king settles on 8b. Each king is now tucked away deep in its own corner.","39":"N-7g. The knight jumps up so that it can leap to 6e at any time. Sente wants the castle's pieces to help in the attack too.","41":"R-3h. Sente shifts the rook to file 3. Gote's file 4 is too strong, so Sente is changing the place to attack. At that time there were no books yet that wrote down the set ways to play the opening (joseki). Masters were still finding good shapes one game at a time. Later players of the Ōhashi and Itō houses studied records like this one and grew the joseki.","42":"R-3b. Gote at once moves its rook to file 3 as well. Wherever Sente's rook goes, Gote's rook follows.","43":"P-2e. Now Sente pushes the pawn on file 2. The plan is to bring the rook to file 2 and attack there.","44":"B-5a. Gote pulls the bishop back. It rearranges its pieces so that other pieces can meet an attack on file 2.","45":"R-2h. The rook is back on its starting file 2. File 4, file 3, file 2: Sente's rook has made a long journey.","46":"R-2b. Gote's rook again follows to file 2. Wherever Sente sends its rook, Gote meets it on the same file. This 'rook chase' is the highlight of the opening of this game.","47":"P-6e. Now Sente starts the fight on file 6, where no rook stands. Instead of attacking where the rooks stare at each other, Sente strikes where Gote is thinner. The middle game begins. Starting a fight on a file where the enemy rook is not is an idea still used in opposing games today. The master of 400 years ago had already noticed it.","49":"Px6d. Sente pushes the pawn and takes. The idea is to lure Gote's gold forward and loosen the defence around the king.","50":"Gx6d. Gote takes back with the gold. The gold has moved a little away from its king, so a small gap opens in Gote's defence.","51":"P-4e. Next Sente pushes the pawn on file 4. It shakes Gote's defence on file 4 as well.","53":"N-3g. The other knight jumps up to join the attack. Sente's attackers are moving forward one by one.","54":"P*6e. Gote drops a pawn on 6e, the square where Sente's knight wants to jump. A pawn is cheap, but it is very useful for blocking an enemy piece's path.","56":"P-7e. Gote strikes back. It pokes at the file right above Sente's king. If the pawns above a king are broken, the king becomes hard to defend.","57":"Px7e. Sente takes with the pawn. If not, Gote's pawn would push on to 7f.","58":"Gx7e. Gote takes back with the gold, which now stands on 7e. Gote is bringing the gold forward to press on Sente's king.","59":"P*7f. Sente drops a pawn to block the gold. This pawn guards the head of the king.","61":"R-6i. Sente's rook travels all the way to the bottom of file 6. It wants to add the rook's power to the fight on file 6. Later this rook will be taken by Gote's silver.","63":"Nx6e. The knight from 7g jumps and takes the pawn on 6e. The knight Sente prepared long ago finally gets to work.","64":"Nx6e. Gote takes back with its own knight. The knights are traded.","66":"P-8e. Gote presses on file 8 too. It tries to strip away the pawns in front of Sente's king one by one.","68":"P*7g. Gote drops a pawn on 7g, right next to the king. If Sente takes it, the taking piece is pulled out of place; if not, the pawn will promote. It is a common trick to break a defence with a small pawn.","69":"G-7i. Sente steps the gold back out of the way. It chooses to keep its defensive shape instead of taking the pawn now.","70":"N*7e, check. Gote drops a knight. The knight reaches the king on 8g by jumping over other pieces. Gote's attack is getting strong.","72":"Sx7e. After Sente's pawn takes the knight, Gote takes back with the silver and moves forward. Gote's attacking pieces are now right next to Sente's king.","74":"P*8f, check. Gote drops another pawn right in front of the king on 8g. The king cannot stay there any longer.","75":"K-9h. Sente's king escapes to 9h on the edge. It steps away from danger and rebuilds its defence.","77":"Px7e. Now Sente's pawn on 7f takes the silver on 7e. Sente removes the centre of Gote's attack and gets a chance to strike back.","79":"S*7f. Sente drops a silver to clash with the gold on 7e. It guards the king's head and tries to drive the gold away.","81":"Sx7f. Silver and gold have been traded. Sente is cutting down Gote's attackers one at a time.","83":"Px6e. The pawn on 6f takes the knight on 6e. Gote loses another attacker, and Sente's pieces in hand grow.","84":"S*7h. Gote drops a silver that aims at the rook on 6i. Sente will lose the rook, but in the meantime it hurries its own attack.","85":"P*9b. Now Sente's counterattack begins. A pawn dropped on 9b, near Gote's king, pulls the lance forward. Moving the pieces around the king creates gaps.","86":"Lx9b. Gote takes the pawn with the lance. But now the lance stands on 9b, and it becomes a target for the next knight.","87":"N*8d. Sente drops a knight that attacks both the lance on 9b and the gold on 7b. Hitting two things at once is just what a knight does best.","88":"B-5a. Gote pulls the bishop back so that it aims along the diagonal at the knight on 8d. Gote wants to take the knight and stop the attack.","89":"N*7d, check. Sente drops its other knight, and it checks Gote's king. A knight can check by jumping over other pieces, so it is hard to defend against.","90":"K-8c. Gote's king steps up. Moving forward also brings it near Sente's knights.","91":"Nx9b+. The knight on 8d takes the lance and promotes, becoming a piece that moves like a gold. Sente has broken into the area near Gote's king.","92":"Kx9b. Gote takes the promoted knight with the king. Gote's king now stands on the edge at 9b.","93":"Bx7g. Sente's bishop takes the pawn on 7g. The bishop has not moved all game, but now it starts working. It will play a big part later.","94":"Sx6i+. Gote's silver takes Sente's rook and promotes. Losing a rook is a big loss, but Sente judges that its attack on the king is faster.","95":"Gx6i. Sente takes back the promoted silver with the gold. Sente's defending gold is still standing firm.","97":"P*9c, check. A pawn is cheap, but dropped right in front of a king it becomes very strong.","98":"K-8a. Gote's king retreats to 8a. Its escape routes are getting fewer.","99":"Bx8f. The bishop takes the pawn on 8f. Along its diagonal it now sees deep into Gote's camp. It is preparing the next big move.","101":"G*9b, check. The gold on 9b and the pawn on 9c work together to drive Gote's king off the edge.","103":"B-5c+. The bishop on 8f flies all the way to 5c and promotes into a horse. A horse moves like a bishop plus one step in any direction, a very strong piece. Sente's big piece has broken in close to Gote's king.","104":"K-6a. Gote's king runs on to file 6. It is being pushed from the edge toward the middle of the board.","105":"L*6d. Sente drops a lance. A lance moves straight forward as far as it likes, so it watches the whole of file 6.","106":"P*6b. Gote drops a pawn to block the lance's path.","107":"+Bx4c. The horse takes the silver on 4c. Gote loses another defender.","108":"N*5b. Gote drops a knight to strengthen its defence. The position is hard, but Gote keeps fighting.","109":"K-8g. Here Sente moves its own king up to 8g once. It seems to be giving the king more room before Gote strikes back.","110":"R*8i, check. Gote strikes back, dropping a rook to give check. Gote is trying to catch Sente's king too.","112":"P-7g+, check. The pawn promotes into a tokin and gives check. A tokin moves like a gold, yet if it is captured the opponent only gets a pawn.","114":"Rx6i+. The rook takes the gold and becomes a dragon. Sente's king has lost its defenders, and the game becomes a race: who catches the other king first?","115":"S*5c. Sente drops a silver to tighten the net around Gote's king. Pressing the enemy king even when your own king is in danger is the heart of such a race.","116":"S*6f, check. Gote drops a silver and checks. Sente's king must run.","117":"K-8f. The king escapes to 8f. Sente's king has plenty of room upward.","119":"K-9e. The king goes on to 9e at the edge. Gote's pieces can hardly reach it here. Sente's king is safe for now, so Sente can focus on attacking.","120":"P-6c. Gote pushes a pawn to defend. But Gote's defence is no longer enough.","121":"S*6b. Here Sente begins the finish. It drops a silver right into Gote's camp to take the pieces guarding the king.","122":"Bx6b. Gote takes the silver with the bishop.","123":"Sx6b+. The silver on 5c takes the bishop and promotes. Gote loses a big piece.","125":"Nx6b+. The knight on 7d takes the gold and promotes as well. Trading piece for piece, Sente strips Gote's defence bare.","126":"Kx6b. Gote takes with the king. But there are almost no defenders left around Gote's king.","127":"B*5c, check. Sente drops the bishop it just captured. Using captured pieces right away is what makes shogi so exciting.","129":"G*8d, check. The gold closes the king's escape from above.","131":"P*7c, check. Gote's king has no choice but to go back deeper.","133":"Lx6c+. The lance takes the pawn on 6c and promotes. Seeing that a piece coming to 6b next would leave the king no escape, Sansa admitted defeat here. Sōkei wins in 133 moves. The highlights were the 'rook chase' of the opening, the middle-game tug of war with pawns and knights, and the ending, where Sente gave up its rook to race at the king. It shows how the ideas of a master 400 years ago still connect to the shogi of today."}},"hu1960":{"t":"Hu Ronghua, aged 15, beats Yang Guanlin","w":"Yang Guanlin","b":"Hu Ronghua","ev":"5th National Xiangqi Individual Championship, round 3","d":"28 October 1960","pl":"Beijing","lead":"Yang Guanlin had been national champion three times. Facing him with Black was Hu Ronghua, a 15-year-old playing his first national tournament. Hu gave up a cannon early to seize the initiative, then won a long endgame. Hu went on to win the tournament and then ten national titles in a row, so this game is remembered as the moment the crown passed from the old champion to the new.","c":{"0":"This is round 3 of the 5th National Xiangqi Individual Championship, held in Beijing in 1960. Red is Yang Guanlin. He won the first championship in 1956 and three of the four tournaments so far — the strongest player of the time. Black is Hu Ronghua from Shanghai, only 15 years old and playing his first national tournament. Everyone expected Red to win, but this game would change xiangqi history.","1":"Yang moves the right cannon to the middle. The Central Cannon aims straight at Black’s general, and it was the opening Yang had studied most deeply.","2":"Hu jumps a horse out to guard the middle soldier. Bringing the horse out early prepares both defence and attack.","3":"Red also jumps a horse out, guarding the middle soldier while bringing pieces forward. Both sides make the centre safe first.","4":"Black pushes the soldier on file 7 early. It holds back Red’s horse and opens a path for Black’s own horse. In this position most players bring out a chariot first.","5":"Red moves the right chariot one step sideways onto an open file.","6":"Black also moves a chariot sideways. Both sides want their strongest piece, the chariot, working early.","7":"Here Red pushes a soldier. Many players would send the chariot across the river, but Yang is steering toward the cannon set-up he liked best.","8":"Black’s cannon advances to the river and stands right in front of Red’s right chariot. This plan, called “left cannon blocks the chariot”, shuts the chariot in at home. It was a bold idea to stop Yang from reaching his favourite set-up.","9":"Red brings out the other horse, developing the left side and preparing to strike back.","10":"Black lifts an elephant to strengthen the defence. Elephants cannot cross the river; their job is to guard the general’s house.","11":"Red’s cannon dives deep into Black’s camp, takes a horse and gives check! It trades a cannon for a horse. Trading a cannon for a horse early often costs time, but here it is said to be a fair move.","12":"Black recaptures the cannon with the chariot. Material is level again, and Black’s chariot has come out naturally.","13":"Red brings out the left chariot too. Red would like to push the black cannons away and get the chariots moving.","14":"Black pushes the other cannon to the river as well, blocking Red’s left chariot. Now both of Red’s chariots have a cannon sitting in front of them. Red is getting cramped.","15":"Red moves the horse toward the river. Red has two threats in mind: taking the middle soldier with the horse, or pushing a soldier to attack Black’s cannon.","16":"Black lifts an advisor to guard the centre. This stops the horse from taking the middle soldier with check.","17":"Red pushes the soldier. That opens the path the soldier was blocking, so now Red’s horse can capture Black’s cannon. The cannon is under attack.","18":"This is the most famous moment of the game. Black does not save the cannon. Instead the chariot moves up to attack Red’s horse. Hu gives up a cannon to keep the attacking shape. His opponent was a three-time champion and he was only 15, yet he chose energy over material — and the spectators were amazed.","19":"Red’s horse takes Black’s cannon. Black is a piece down. In return, Black’s chariot is now pressing on Red’s chariot and horse from the side.","20":"Black’s other cannon takes the middle soldier — check! Black’s counterattack begins. Red has to lift an advisor to protect the general.","21":"Red lifts an advisor to block the check. But Black’s cannon is still sitting deep inside Red’s camp.","22":"Black’s chariot takes Red’s chariot. When the cannon moved away a moment ago, the chariot’s path opened up.","23":"Red takes back with the horse — a chariot for a chariot. Black is still a piece down, but Black’s pieces are now working better.","24":"Black’s horse advances to the river. One idea is to go after Red’s horse next.","26":"Black’s cannon slides across and stops right beside Red’s horse. A xiangqi horse cannot move in a direction if a piece stands right next to it on that side (the horse’s “leg”). With its leg blocked by the cannon, Red’s horse can hardly move.","28":"Black pushes the middle soldier. Later this soldier will become an important attacker in Red’s camp.","30":"Black’s horse takes a soldier and slips into Red’s camp. Little by little, Black wins back material.","32":"Black’s horse comes back to the middle. It avoids being traded for Red’s horse and takes a fine central point.","34":"Black’s cannon steps back one point. It is getting ready to slide across and attack Red’s horse.","36":"Black’s cannon slides over and, using Black’s own chariot as a screen, attacks Red’s horse. A cannon captures by jumping over exactly one piece.","38":"Black’s horse jumps out and attacks Red’s chariot. By moving away, it also takes away the screen Red’s cannon needed to attack Black’s chariot.","40":"Red’s cannon gave check, so Black drops the advisor back to block. Red is fighting back hard.","42":"Black’s cannon takes Red’s horse! Black wins back the cannon given up in the opening. Material is level, Black has an extra soldier, and all three of Black’s attacking pieces stand across the river. Black is now clearly better.","44":"Black pushes a soldier forward and offers it for free. If Red takes it, a hidden plan becomes possible — a move that looks far ahead.","45":"Red takes the soldier with an elephant. Now Red must watch out for Black’s plan.","46":"Black offers the horse as well. If Red’s horse takes it, Black’s cannon can give check while uncovering an attack on Red’s chariot.","47":"Red does not take the horse and pulls its own horse back instead. Yang, an old champion, does not fall into traps easily.","48":"Black’s chariot slides sideways and frees itself from the pin. Now the chariot can move freely again, for attack or defence.","50":"Black’s cannon moves to the middle, staring straight at Red’s general — a very strong post.","52":"Black’s horse jumps forward again. Cannon and horse are working together in the centre.","56":"Black’s horse goes all the way to the edge and eyes the side of Red’s general. A horse on the edge has fewer moves, but here it has the important job of threatening the general’s shelter.","58":"Black’s middle soldier moves further forward, pressing on Red’s camp. Once a soldier crosses the river it can also move sideways, so it becomes much stronger.","60":"The cannons are traded off. Red’s cannon took Black’s cannon, and Black’s soldier took Red’s cannon back. Black’s soldier is now close to Red’s camp.","62":"Black’s chariot takes one of Red’s elephants. Red’s defence loses another piece.","64":"Black’s horse gives check! Red blocks the horse’s leg with the chariot. In xiangqi you can stop a horse’s check by putting a piece right next to the horse.","66":"Black’s horse steps back for now. It keeps giving checks while looking for its best square.","70":"Black’s horse checks again. Red’s chariot cannot leave the general’s side, so it cannot join the attack.","72":"Black’s horse drops back to the edge. It keeps Red’s chariot tied down while Black plans the next step.","76":"Black’s chariot takes Red’s other elephant too. Red’s general is becoming very lonely.","82":"Black’s chariot takes Red’s horse. Red’s hard-working horse is traded off here.","83":"Red takes back Black’s horse with the chariot. With the horses traded, the board is much clearer. Now it is a fight of chariot and soldiers on both sides.","90":"Black’s soldier takes one of Red’s advisors! A soldier is a small piece, but deep in the enemy camp it is strong enough to trade itself for an advisor.","91":"Red takes the soldier back with the other advisor. A soldier for an advisor — Red’s general is even less protected.","92":"Black’s chariot takes a Red soldier. In the endgame, the number of soldiers left decides who wins.","98":"Black’s chariot takes another soldier. Red has only a few soldiers left. Step by step, Black is heading for an easier winning position.","99":"Red’s chariot takes a Black elephant in return. The two chariots are trading blows, taking one piece each.","104":"Black starts pushing the soldier on the edge. Bringing this soldier deep into Red’s camp is Black’s road to victory.","112":"Black’s chariot goes behind the soldier to protect it. A chariot and a soldier together make a very strong team.","115":"Red’s chariot takes one of Black’s soldiers. Black has one soldier left. Red has not given up yet.","123":"Red pushes the soldier forward. But it is said that pulling the chariot back to guard the general was better here. This move became the turning point of the game.","124":"Black’s soldier steps sideways toward Red’s general. A soldier moves only one step at a time, but the closer it gets to the general, the more dangerous it becomes.","126":"Black’s chariot drops back and attacks Red’s soldier. Black must keep that soldier in check, or Red could fight back with it.","134":"Red’s soldier has reached the far end of the board, but Black’s chariot has been chasing it all the way. A soldier that reaches the far end can no longer go forward, only sideways.","136":"Black’s chariot takes Red’s last soldier. Black has a chariot, a soldier and two advisors; Red has a chariot and one advisor. With an extra soldier, Black’s win is now clearly in sight.","140":"Black’s chariot gives check! Red’s general can only step back.","144":"Black’s soldier advances again and is now right next to Red’s general. With soldier and chariot working together, Red’s general is no longer safe.","148":"Black’s general also steps sideways. In the endgame the general is a fighting piece too, controlling where the enemy pieces can go.","150":"Black’s chariot drops back, ready for both defence and attack. It is getting the chariot ready for the best square for the final blow.","152":"Black’s chariot enters file 4, and Red can no longer hold. The game is decided, and 15-year-old Hu Ronghua has won. A bold cannon sacrifice in the opening, teamwork of horse and cannon in the middlegame, and precise play through a long endgame — this game had all three. Hu won the tournament and went on to be national champion ten times in a row, from 1960 to 1979."}},"soei1790":{"t":"Demon Sōei beats the Meijin in an even game","w":"Ōhashi Sōei (VI)","b":"Ōhashi Sōkei (IX)","ev":"Oshiro Shogi (the yearly game at Edo Castle)","d":"1790","pl":"Japan (Edo)","lead":"Ōhashi Sōei, nicknamed 'Demon Sōei', played this even game against the reigning Meijin, Ōhashi Sōkei IX, at the castle games. That year the two alternated even games and lance-handicap games, and this even game is remembered as a rare masterpiece. Sōei won in 121 moves.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. The castle games (Oshiro Shogi) were a great yearly event, where the shogi houses played before the Shōgun. Sente, Ōhashi Sōei, was so strong that people called him 'Demon Sōei'; he later became the ninth Meijin. Gote, Ōhashi Sōkei IX, had become Meijin only the year before. So this is the Meijin against the next Meijin. Sōei spread the idea of building a solid defence first and attacking after, close to how shogi is played today, and he is even called the father of modern shogi. 1790 was the time when Matsudaira Sadanobu, a senior minister under the shogun Tokugawa Ienari, was carrying out the \"Kansei Reforms\". People were told to stop spending on luxuries, and that year the rules on publishing books became stricter too. Even so, the castle games went on. The 8th shogun, Yoshimune, is said to have fixed the castle-shogi day on 17 November, and today's Shogi Day in Japan comes from it.","1":"P-7f. The first move opens the bishop's diagonal. From here Sōei builds a plan to attack with his right-side pieces. Sōei was born in 1756, a son of Sōjun, the 5th head of the Ōhashi branch house. As a small child he was sent to live with another family, but it is said he was brought back home after being recognised as a born master, and he went on to inherit the house.","3":"P-2f. Sente pushes the pawn in front of the rook. Pushing the rook's pawn early to press the opponent was one of the ideas Sōei valued.","4":"P-4d. Gote pushes a pawn that closes its bishop's diagonal. It avoids a bishop trade and prepares for a slow game. Gote, Sōkei IX, was born in 1744. He first played in the castle games at 12 and is said to have beaten his uncle, Itō Kanju. The year before this game (1789), at the age of 46, he took over the Meijin title, which had been empty for 27 years.","5":"S-4h. Sente lifts the right silver so it can be used both to attack and to defend.","7":"P-5f. Sente pushes the centre pawn so that silvers and golds can come forward more easily.","9":"P-3f. Sente pushes the file-3 pawn too, opening a road for the knight to jump. It prepares N-3g.","11":"P-2e. Sente pushes the rook's pawn further. Now Gote must guard file 2 with the bishop or a silver. Pushing the pawn in front of the rook and trading it for the enemy pawn is called the \"rook-pawn exchange\". Sōei is said to have shown clearly that this trade is an advantage. It is still the most basic idea in Static Rook openings today.","12":"B-3c. Gote guards file 2 with the bishop. Without it, Sente's rook would break through on file 2 after P-2d.","15":"G-5h. Sente brings a gold closer to the king and starts to build its defence.","17":"N-3g. The knight jumps up and Sente's attacking shape is ready. From here the knight can leap to 4e or 2e to reach Gote's camp. Sōei is also known for winning, as a young man (in 1779), with a new plan called the \"Bird Catcher\". He did not stick to fixed shapes; he liked to invent new ways to play.","20":"R-5b. Gote moves its rook to the centre file 5. Today this is called the Central Rook. The aim is to shake Sente's camp from the middle. Now the game is an \"opposing\" one: Static Rook Sente against Ranging Rook (Central Rook) Gote. Sōkei IX is said to have often tried new plans such as Ranging Rook. The Central Rook is still a popular plan, played today under names like \"Cheerful Central Rook\".","22":"P-5e. Gote at once pushes its file-5 pawn into Sente's. As Central Rook players like to, it starts the fight in the middle. Putting the rook in the middle and pushing the file-5 pawn straight away is the basic move of the Central Rook. It is a direct way of attacking that still lives on in today's Central Rook.","23":"Px5e. Sente takes the pawn. If not, Gote's pawn would take the pawn on 5f.","24":"S-6d. Gote brings a silver forward, aiming to win back the pawn on 5e.","25":"P-5d. Sente pushes the captured pawn on even further. Placing it right in front of Gote's rook makes Gote take it, which moves Gote's pieces out of their best squares.","26":"Rx5d. Gote takes the pawn with the rook. Gote's rook has come out to 5d and is now strong in the centre.","27":"B-7i. Sente pulls the bishop back. Stepping back once lets it work later along a different diagonal. This bishop will later come out to 2d.","28":"K-4a. Gote moves its king to 4a. With a Central Rook, the king usually hides on the right side.","29":"P*5f. Sente drops a pawn to stop Gote's rook from advancing along file 5.","30":"P*5e. Gote drops a pawn too and clashes again on file 5. The tug of war in the centre goes on.","32":"Px5f. Gote's pawn takes Sente's pawn and bites into Sente's camp.","34":"S-5e. Gote brings the silver out to take control of file 5.","35":"Gx5e. Sente takes the silver with the gold. A gold and a silver are about to be traded.","36":"Rx5e. Gote takes back the gold with the rook. After this gold-for-silver trade, the fight in the centre calms down. Sente now has a silver in hand, and Gote has a gold.","37":"P*5f. Sente drops a pawn to block file 5, so Gote's rook cannot come any further.","38":"R-5b. Gote pulls its rook back. The fight in the centre is over for now.","39":"G-5h. Sente lifts its other gold and makes the area around the king firmer.","41":"K-6i. Sente's king starts moving left, away from the centre where the fighting happened.","43":"K-7h. The king reaches 7h. From here Sente will build a castle with golds and silvers. Sōei was a player who believed in defending first and attacking second.","44":"R-8b. Gote returns its rook to file 8. Now that the centre is quiet, it plans to attack on file 8 instead.","45":"S*7g. Sente drops the silver from its hand in front of the king. It prepares in advance for Gote's attack on file 8.","47":"P-1f. Sente pushes the edge pawn. An advanced edge pawn helps later, whether for an edge attack or as an escape route for the king.","49":"P-1e. The edge pawn goes one step further. Sente is preparing carefully.","51":"P-2d. Here Sente starts its attack, pushing the rook's pawn it has been advancing all along. Sente's offensive begins.","53":"Bx2d. The bishop that retreated to 7i flies all the way along the diagonal to 2d. Now we can see why Sente pulled the bishop back on move 27.","54":"Bx2d. Gote takes the bishop with its own bishop. The bishops are traded.","55":"Rx2d. Sente takes back with the rook, which now stands on 2d glaring at Gote's camp. Each side now has a bishop in hand.","56":"P*2c. Gote drops a pawn to block the rook's path.","57":"R-2f. Sente pulls the rook back. It doesn't push too far, and brings the rook back to safety.","59":"P-4f. Sente pushes the file-4 pawn to prepare the next attack. After file 2, the plan is to attack on files 3 and 4.","60":"R-8d. Gote lifts its rook so it can also move sideways.","61":"P-3e. Sente pushes the file-3 pawn, getting ready to clash with Gote's pawn on 3d.","63":"P-4e. Sente pushes its file-4 pawn into Gote's. Sente shakes Gote on several files at once: 3, 4 and 5.","65":"P-5e. Sente pushes the file-5 pawn forward too, driving a wedge into the middle of Gote's defence.","66":"P*5c. Gote drops a pawn to guard file 5.","67":"Px3d. Sente's pawn takes the pawn on 3d. Later this pawn will reach 3c and promote, pressing Gote's king.","68":"P-8f. Gote strikes back, pushing its file-8 pawn to start a fire near Sente's king.","69":"Sx8f. Sente takes with the silver. The silver from 7g steps forward and defends while keeping the king safe.","70":"B*4d. Gote drops the bishop from its hand. Along its diagonals it watches Sente's camp, ready to defend and attack.","71":"R-2i. Sente pulls the rook all the way back. It escapes the bishop's line and still watches file 2.","72":"P-6e. Gote pushes the file-6 pawn to shake the area near Sente's king.","74":"P*3f. Gote drops a pawn that attacks the knight on 3g.","75":"B*6f. Sente drops its bishop from hand. Along the diagonal it attacks the rook on 8d. It defends and attacks a big piece at the same time: two birds with one stone.","76":"R-8a. Gote runs the rook away. With just one bishop move, Sente has pushed Gote's rook all the way back to the bottom.","77":"Nx4e. The knight jumps and takes the pawn on 4e. Next it aims to jump into 3c or 5c.","78":"G*4f. Gote drops a gold that presses on the knight from behind.","79":"P-3c+. The pawn from 3d advances to 3c and promotes into a tokin. A Sente piece has broken in near Gote's king.","80":"Nx3c. Gote takes the tokin with its knight.","81":"Nx3c+. Sente's knight on 4e takes that knight and promotes. Trading piece for piece, Sente thins out the defenders around Gote's king.","82":"Bx3c. Gote takes back the promoted knight with the bishop.","83":"S-5g. Sente moves the silver up to 5g to clash with Gote's gold on 4f, to drive away the gold that came so close.","85":"Gx5g. Silver and gold have been traded. Sente has neatly cleared away the Gote piece that came into its camp.","86":"P-7e. Gote pushes its file-7 pawn, aiming at the head of Sente's king.","87":"N*4e. Sente drops a knight again. This knight attacks the bishop on 3c.","88":"B-2d. Gote moves the bishop away to 2d.","89":"P*3c. Sente drops a pawn to drive a wedge near Gote's king.","90":"G-2b. Gote steps the gold back to defend.","91":"P-5d. Sente pushes the file-5 pawn further, luring Gote's silver to 5d.","92":"Sx5d. Gote takes the pawn with the silver. But this leaves 4c empty, which links to Sente's next moves.","93":"Rx2d. This is the turning point. Sente takes Gote's bishop with the rook. Even if the rook is lost, Sente judges it can rush at Gote's king in the meantime.","94":"Px2d. Gote takes the rook with a pawn. Sente has given up its rook but won a bishop, an attacking piece, for its hand.","95":"B*3b, check. Sente drops the bishop. Following Rx2d, this is the real start of Sente's attack.","96":"K-5a. Gote's king escapes to 5a.","97":"Bx5d+. The bishop takes the silver on 5d and promotes into a horse. Since 4c was empty, the bishop could reach it straight along the diagonal. The empty square left by Sx5d on move 92 pays off here.","98":"Px5d. Gote takes the horse with a pawn. Sente has given away all its big pieces, but Gote's defending silver is gone too.","99":"P*5c. Sente drops a pawn right in front of Gote's king as a foothold. Next it aims to promote on 5b.","100":"R*4h. Gote strikes back, dropping the rook it won into Sente's camp. The game becomes a race: whose attack is faster?","101":"S-5h. Sente pulls the silver back to defend, blocking the rook from sliding sideways toward the king.","102":"B*5f, check. Gote drops a bishop. From 5f it reaches the king on 7h diagonally through 6g.","103":"K-8h. Sente steps the king down, out of the bishop's line. With this defensive move it makes sure it won't lose the race.","104":"Bx4e. Gote's bishop takes the knight.","105":"Px5b+. The pawn promotes into a tokin and takes Gote's gold. A Sente piece is now right beside Gote's king.","106":"Kx5b. Gote takes the tokin with the king.","107":"P*5c, check. Pawns can be dropped again and again, so Sente can keep checking like this (but you may not have two of your own unpromoted pawns on the same file).","108":"K-6b. Gote's king escapes to 6b.","109":"S*6d. Sente drops a silver to close the king's escape routes.","110":"S*7d. Gote drops a silver too, to defend.","111":"P-5b+. Sente makes a tokin and attacks the king from behind as well.","112":"K-7b. Gote's king goes to 7b. Step by step, it is being chased toward the edge.","113":"Sx7c+. The silver takes the knight on 7c and promotes. Another of Gote's defenders is gone.","114":"Kx7c. Gote takes the promoted silver with the king.","115":"G*6d, check. From here Sente uses the pieces in its hand to hunt down Gote's king.","116":"K-8c. Gote's king goes to 8c.","117":"Gx7d, check. The gold takes the silver on 7d with check, stripping away the defenders around Gote's king.","118":"Kx7d. Gote takes the gold with the king. The king has been dragged out toward the middle of the board.","119":"Sx7e, check. The silver on 8f takes a pawn with check. The silver that stepped up to defend on move 69 joins the attack at the end.","120":"K-6c. Gote's king escapes to 6c.","121":"S-6d, check. The silver on 7e moves to 6d with check. Sente still has a gold, a silver and a knight in hand, and seeing that the king could not escape, Sōkei admitted defeat here. Sōei wins in 121 moves. After the fight in the centre, Sente first built a firm castle, then widened the attack across files 2, 3 and 4, and finally gave up the rook to rush the king. It is a masterpiece between Meijin that shows Sōei's shogi of 'defend first, then attack'."}},"soho1856":{"t":"Sōho the Saint and his far-seeing bishop","w":"Amano Sōho","b":"Itō Sōin (VIII)","ev":"Oshiro Shogi (the yearly game at Edo Castle)","d":"1856","pl":"Japan (Edo)","lead":"Amano Sōho, called the 'Saint of Shogi', played this castle game against Itō Sōin VIII, a future Meijin, and it became Sōho's last castle game. On move 33 he dropped a bishop into the corner of his own camp, B*1h, a move remembered in shogi history as a masterstroke. Sōho wins in 111 moves.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. Amano Sōho played near the end of the Edo period, and he was so strong that people called him 'a true 13-dan' and the 'Saint of Shogi'. But he was not born into one of the shogi houses, so he could never become Meijin. Gote, Itō Sōin VIII, was Sōho's junior in the same school and later became Meijin. This castle game of 1856 was the last castle game Sōho ever played. Sōho is said to have been a master of the bishop, so keep an eye on the bishops in this game. 1856 was three years after Commodore Perry's \"black ships\" came to Japan. That year the American Townsend Harris arrived in Shimoda, and the first American consulate in Japan was set up in a temple there called Gyokusenji. This castle game comes from a time when the end of the Edo period was near and the country was starting to change fast.","1":"P-7f. The first move opens the bishop's diagonal. Sōho's bishop will be the star of this game. Sōho was born in 1816 and was known in daily life as Tomejirō. He learned shogi as a pupil of the Ōhashi main house, but his rank never went above 7-dan. It is said that the 13th Meijin, Sekine Kinjirō, later honoured him as the \"Saint of Shogi\". Sōho died in 1859, three years after this game, at the age of 44.","3":"P-2f. Sente pushes the pawn in front of the rook. Sōho liked fast openings, pushing the rook's pawn early and getting his pieces moving.","4":"P-5d. Gote pushes the centre pawn. We can see a plan coming: the rook will swing to file 5 for a Central Rook. Static Rook for Sente against Central Rook for Gote: the same \"Static Rook vs Central Rook\" opposing game as the one between Sōei and Sōkei IX some 60 years earlier. Ranging Rook was played often in the Edo castle games too.","7":"S-4h. Sente lifts the right silver to prepare for both attack and defence.","11":"P-2e. Sente pushes the rook's pawn further. Next it plans to clash on 2d.","12":"S-6d. Gote brings a silver forward. The idea is to advance it toward 5e and attack in the centre.","13":"P-2d. Sente pushes the pawn into Gote's to trade off the rook's pawn. It is a natural way to start a fight, still common today. The rook-pawn exchange is the idea Ōhashi Sōei is said to have shown to be an advantage about 60 years earlier. By Sōho's time it had become a normal part of Static Rook play.","15":"Rx2d. The rook takes the pawn. Sente gains a pawn in hand and the rook comes forward. Trading the rook's pawn gives you an extra pawn in hand and makes the rook easier to use.","16":"R-5b. Gote moves its rook to file 5. With a Central Rook it plans to strike back in the middle.","17":"K-6i. Sente moves the king to the left, away from the centre where the Central Rook will attack.","19":"R-2h. The rook goes back home. Having won a pawn, Sente calmly pulls the rook back.","20":"P*2c. Gote drops a pawn to close the gap on file 2.","21":"Bx2b+. Here Sente trades bishops. After a bishop trade, both sides hold a bishop in hand that can be dropped anywhere. From now on, Sōho shows his skill in where he drops his bishop. Trading bishops early, keeping one in hand and fighting over where to drop it was a way of playing Sōho loved. A bishop in hand can be dropped anywhere on the board, so it can become very strong if it is used well.","22":"Sx2b. Gote takes back with the silver. Each side now has a bishop in hand.","24":"P-5e. Gote pushes its centre pawn into Sente's. It is a typical Central Rook attack.","26":"Sx5e. Gote's silver comes all the way to 5e. Gote's pieces are getting strong in the centre.","27":"P*5d. Sente drops a pawn deep into Gote's camp. The pawn on 5d blocks the front of Gote's rook and gets in the way of Gote's pieces.","29":"B*5c. Sente drops its bishop into Gote's camp. The idea is to let it run wild inside Gote's position.","30":"B*4d. Gote drops its bishop too, clashing with Sente's. It is a defensive move to get rid of Sente's bishop.","31":"Bx4d+. Sente's bishop takes Gote's bishop and promotes.","32":"Sx4d. Gote takes the horse back with the silver. The bishops have been traded again, and Sente once more has a bishop in hand.","33":"B*1h. This is the famous moment. Sōho drops his bishop in the corner of his own camp, on 1h. Along its diagonal it looks all the way across the board into Gote's camp. For now it supports the pawn on 5d from behind, and later, when the diagonal opens, it will do great work: a 'far-seeing bishop'. It is called one of the masterstrokes of shogi history, though some later Meijin think it may have been a clever way out of a difficult spot.","34":"G-4b. Gote brings a gold over to firm up the area around the king.","35":"S-5g. Sente lifts a silver to prepare for the fight in the centre.","37":"S-5f. The silver moves further forward, aiming at the centre squares 5e and 4e.","39":"R-5h. Sente moves the rook to the centre. Sente too gathers strength on file 5, in a Central Rook shape.","41":"P-6f. Sente pushes the file-6 pawn to make a path for its silver and bishop.","43":"K-7i. The king goes deeper to the left. Even Sōho puts the king in order before attacking.","45":"G-5i. Sente brings a gold near the king to strengthen its defence.","46":"P-8e. Gote pushes its file-8 pawn, preparing to attack from near Sente's king.","47":"B-2g. The bishop on 1h steps up one square. Shifting its line slightly, it now eyes 4e and 3f.","49":"S-4e. The silver advances to 4e and presses on Gote's camp. The bishop on 2g supports this silver from behind.","50":"P*5e. Gote drops a pawn to block the centre.","52":"P-8f. Gote starts a fight on file 8, pushing a pawn into the pawn near Sente's king.","54":"P-7e. Gote pushes the file-7 pawn into Sente's too. It is trying to break several of the pawns in front of Sente's king.","56":"Sx4e. Gote's silver takes Sente's silver on 4e.","57":"Bx4e. The bishop on 2g takes back the silver on 4e. The bishop's diagonal comes in handy here.","58":"S-4d. Gote lifts a silver to chase the bishop on 4e.","59":"B-1h. The bishop goes back to the corner at 1h again. Chased by the silver, it returns to its far-seeing spot. Watch how this bishop works at the end.","60":"S*5f. Gote drops a silver and attacks in the centre.","61":"Sx5f. Sente takes with its silver.","62":"Px5f. Gote takes back with a pawn, which now reaches 5f.","63":"Rx5f. Sente takes the pawn with the rook. The rook works in the centre.","64":"S*5e. Gote drops another silver to chase Sente's rook.","65":"R-5h. The rook steps back to where it was.","66":"Sx6f. Gote's silver takes the pawn on 6f and comes closer to Sente's king. Gote's attack goes on.","67":"P*6g. Sente drops a pawn so the silver cannot come in any further.","68":"P*7g. Gote drops a pawn on 7g, near Sente's king. It wants to move Sente's pieces around and make a gap.","69":"Nx7g. Sente takes the pawn with the knight. Later this knight will jump to 6e and join the attack.","70":"Sx7e. Gote's silver takes the pawn on 7e and closes in on the head of Sente's king.","71":"G-6h. Sente brings a gold over to guard the king.","72":"P*7f. Gote drops another pawn, aiming at the knight. Gote's attack is getting sharp.","73":"N-6e. Here Sente strikes back. The knight jumps to 6e and aims at 5c in Gote's camp.","74":"S-6d. Gote pulls the silver back to aim at the knight on 6e.","75":"S*5c. Sente drops a silver deep into Gote's camp. The pawn on 5d and the knight on 6e both support it. The pawn Sente dropped on 5d long ago is useful here.","76":"Gx5c. Gote takes the silver with the gold.","77":"Px5c+. The pawn on 5d takes the gold and promotes into a tokin.","78":"Sx5c. Gote takes the tokin with the silver.","79":"Nx5c+. The knight on 6e takes that silver and promotes. The captures go on, and Gote's camp grows emptier and emptier.","80":"Sx5c. Gote takes the promoted knight with the silver. But now Sente's pawn on 5d is gone, so the diagonal of the bishop on 1h is open all the way to 6c.","81":"Bx6c+. At last the bishop on 1h moves. It flies down the diagonal, takes the pawn on 6c and becomes a horse. The 'far-seeing bishop' dropped on move 33 does its big job here, 48 moves later. It is a true bishop's move, running from one edge of the board to the other.","82":"S*7g. Gote drops a silver to press on Sente's king. Gote keeps attacking too.","83":"P*2d. Sente drops a pawn on file 2 to shake the area near Gote's king.","84":"Px2d. Gote takes it with a pawn.","85":"P*2e. Sente drops another pawn. It aims at Gote's pawn on 2d and tries to pry open file 2.","86":"B*3c. Gote drops a bishop to strengthen its defence.","87":"P*5e. Sente drops a pawn to hold back the movements of Gote's silver and bishop.","88":"Sx7h+. Gote's silver takes Sente's gold and promotes.","89":"Gx7h. Sente takes back with the gold.","90":"G*7g. Gote drops a gold that presses on Sente's king. The fighting near Sente's king is fierce too.","91":"S*4e. Sente drops a silver. This silver aims at 3d, near Gote's king.","92":"R-6a. Gote moves its rook to file 6 to attack Sente's horse.","93":"+B-7d. The horse moves away to 7d. A horse is strong: it helps in its own camp and in the enemy's.","94":"Px2e. Gote's pawn takes the pawn on 2e.","95":"Sx3d. The silver takes the pawn on 3d. Sente's silver is right in front of Gote's king.","96":"B-2d. Gote moves its bishop to 2d to prepare for Sente's attack.","97":"S*4f. Sente drops another silver. It cuts the diagonal of the bishop on 2d and limits what that bishop can do.","98":"G*3c. Gote drops a gold to defend.","99":"Sx3c+. The silver on 3d takes the gold and promotes.","100":"Kx3c. Gote takes the promoted silver with the king. Gote's king has come out to 3c, where there are few defenders.","101":"S*3e. Sente drops another silver. It attacks the bishop on 2d and also blocks the king's escape.","102":"N*8g, check. Gote drops a knight to check. It is a last stand.","103":"Gx8g. Sente takes the knight with the gold.","104":"Gx8g. Gote takes back with the gold. But Sente's king is still not caught.","105":"Sx2d, check. The silver takes the bishop with check. From here Sente hunts Gote's king all at once.","106":"Kx2d. Gote takes the silver with the king.","107":"S-3e, check. The silver on 4f moves to 3e with check, pulling Gote's king out of its camp.","108":"Kx3e. Gote's king takes the silver and comes out to 3e. When a king comes out toward the middle with no defenders, it is in great danger.","109":"B*4f, check. The bishop again, right to the end. Sente drops the bishop from its hand with check. In Sōho's game, the bishops played a starring role from beginning to end.","110":"K-4d. Gote's king escapes to 4d.","111":"G*3d, check. Seeing that the king could no longer escape, Sōin admitted defeat here. Sōho wins in 111 moves. B*1h on move 33 did great work 48 moves later with Bx6c+, and the game was decided by a bishop check too. It is a masterpiece that shows Sōho's way of placing a piece while looking far into the future."}},"sakata1913":{"t":"Sakata Sankichi beats Sekine with the Side Rook","w":"Sakata Sankichi","b":"Sekine Kinjirō","ev":"A game sponsored by the Osaka Asahi newspaper","d":"14 July 1913","pl":"Japan (Osaka)","lead":"Sakata Sankichi of Osaka and Sekine Kinjirō, who later became the 13th Meijin, were rivals all their lives. This is an even game they played in Osaka in the summer of 1913. Sakata attacked with the 'Side Rook', moving his rook to file 3, and won a long battle of 143 moves.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. Sakata Sankichi was born in Sakai near Osaka, learned shogi almost entirely by himself, and became the strongest player in western Japan. Sekine Kinjirō was the top player in Tokyo and later became the 13th Meijin. They had played each other many times since they were young, more than 30 games in all: a famous rivalry. In April of this year, Sakata went to Tokyo for the first time and played Sekine. The words 'my silver is crying' are said to come from that game, which was a lance-handicap game. This July game is an even game against Sekine, who had come to Osaka. 1913 was the second year of the Taishō era: the Emperor Meiji had died, and this was the first summer of the new age. That same month (July), near Osaka, the \"Takarazuka Choral Troupe\" was formed, which grew into today's Takarazuka Revue. A game sponsored by a newspaper was also something new: it did not exist in the Edo period, when the shogi houses ran the game.","1":"P-7f. The first move opens the bishop's diagonal. From here Sakata builds a plan full of his own ideas. Sakata was born in 1870 into a poor family in Sakai that made the tops of straw sandals. It is said he left primary school after about half a year and was never good at reading and writing. He is said to have learned shogi at about 12 by watching grown-ups play on benches by the street.","3":"P-5f. Sente pushes the centre pawn, preparing to bring a silver up to 5g. Gote, Sekine Kinjirō, was born in 1868. As a young man he travelled around the country to sharpen his skill, and eight years after this game, in 1921, he became the 13th Meijin at the age of 53.","5":"P-6f. Sente pushes the file-6 pawn too and closes the bishop's diagonal. It avoids a bishop trade and builds its position slowly.","9":"S-6g. A silver moves up in front of the king and starts a defensive wall.","11":"B-7g. The bishop steps up behind the pawn on 6f. Using the bishop to defend gives a shape close to the Fortress castle. Building with the bishop on 7g, a silver on 6g and a gold on 7h leads toward the Fortress (Yagura), the classic castle of Static Rook against Static Rook. In the Shōwa and Heisei eras that followed, the Fortress became one of the most played plans in professional shogi.","13":"S-4h. Sente lifts the other silver too, ready to use it in the attack.","16":"K-4b. Gote, Sekine, moves his king toward the right side.","17":"G-7h. A gold comes toward the king to make the defence firmer.","19":"P-3f. Sente pushes the file-3 pawn. This is where Sakata's plan begins.","21":"R-3h. Sente moves the rook to file 3. This is the 'Side Rook'. Instead of file 2, the rook stands on the file next to it, file 3, to attack there. Sakata often used unusual plans like this. The Side Rook is still played now and then in Static Rook games, as a plan to attack file 3, close to the enemy king, from early on. Sakata's name also lives on in the name of an opening: the \"Sakata Opposing Rook\".","23":"K-6i. Sente's king goes left, away from file 3, where the attack will be.","26":"K-3b. Gote's king goes to 3b. But that is right next to file 3, which Sente's rook is aiming at.","27":"P-3e. The Side Rook attack begins. Sente pushes the file-3 pawn into Gote's and tries to break through on file 3 with the rook.","28":"Px3e. Gote takes with the pawn.","29":"Rx3e. The rook takes the pawn. Sente's rook comes out to 3e and glares at the area near Gote's king. Sente has also won a pawn.","30":"B-3a. Gote pulls the bishop back to prepare to defend file 3.","31":"R-3h. Sente pulls the rook back. Having won a pawn, it calmly brings the rook home.","32":"P-8f. Gote strikes back on file 8.","34":"Bx8f. The bishop that went back to 3a comes all the way along the diagonal to 8f and takes the pawn. A clever use of the bishop's long line.","35":"Bx8f. Sente takes the bishop with its bishop.","36":"Rx8f. Gote takes back with the rook. The bishops have been traded.","37":"P*8g. Sente drops a pawn to chase Gote's rook.","38":"R-8b. Gote's rook goes back home. Each side now has a bishop in hand.","39":"S-4f. Sente brings a silver forward to prepare for fights in the centre and on file 4.","43":"K-7i. Sente tucks the king in deeper and puts its defence in order.","47":"K-8h. The king settles on 8h, with golds and silvers gathered around it.","51":"P-5e. Sente pushes the centre pawn into Gote's. From here the real fighting starts.","52":"Px5e. Gote takes with the pawn.","53":"B*6c. Sente drops its bishop into Gote's camp. The idea is to let it run wild inside Gote's position.","55":"R-5h. Sente swings the rook to file 5 to join the fight in the centre.","56":"B*9b. Gote drops its bishop too. Along its diagonal it stares at Sente's camp.","57":"B-3f+. The bishop on 6c comes back along the diagonal and promotes as it leaves the enemy camp, becoming a horse on 3f. Back in its own camp, it can both defend and attack. A horse in your own camp is a very reliable defender.","58":"P-4e. Gote pushes a pawn to chase the silver on 4f.","59":"S-5g. The silver steps back to 5g.","60":"Px7f. Gote's file-7 pawn takes the pawn in front of Sente's king.","61":"Sx7f. Sente takes back with the silver.","62":"S-5d. Gote brings a silver to the centre to firm up its defence.","63":"+B-3g. The horse steps back to guard Sente's camp.","64":"R-7b. Gote swings its rook to file 7, aiming at the head of Sente's king.","65":"P*7e. Sente drops a pawn to block the rook's path.","66":"G-4d. Gote lifts a gold.","68":"K-4c. Gote's king climbs to 4c. It is an unusual shape, with the king close to the middle of the board. This king position will matter a lot in the race later on.","69":"G-6g. Sente lifts a gold next to the silver on 5g, building a defensive wall.","71":"N-7g. The knight jumps up, ready to join the attack.","73":"P-6e. Sente pushes its file-6 pawn into Gote's to shake Gote's camp.","75":"P*5f. Sente drops a pawn to firm up the centre.","76":"P-3f. Gote pushes its file-3 pawn and pokes at Sente's horse.","77":"+Bx3f. The horse takes the pawn.","78":"Px5f. Gote's pawn takes Sente's pawn and presses into the centre.","79":"Sx5f. Sente takes back with the silver.","80":"P*5e. Gote drops a pawn to block the silver's path.","81":"Sx6e. The silver takes the pawn on 6e and approaches Gote's camp from the side.","82":"P*6d. Gote drops a pawn to chase the silver.","83":"Sx5d. The silver takes the silver on 5d, removing one of Gote's defenders.","84":"Gx5d. Gote takes back with the gold.","85":"P*6e. Sente drops another pawn and keeps attacking on file 6.","86":"P*3e. Gote drops a pawn to chase Sente's horse.","87":"+B-3g. The horse goes back to 3g. It is an important defender of Sente's camp, so Sente does not force it forward.","88":"S-4d. Gote lifts a silver to guard the king.","89":"P*5f. Sente drops a pawn into Gote's pawn on 5e, trying to pry open the centre.","91":"Px6d. The pawn on 6e takes the pawn on 6d. A Sente pawn has made its way into Gote's camp.","92":"P-4f. Gote pushes its file-4 pawn and strikes back.","95":"P-6c+. The pawn promotes into a tokin. A tokin moves like a gold, yet if it is captured the opponent gets only a pawn.","96":"Gx6c. Gote takes the tokin with the gold.","97":"P*6f. Sente drops a pawn to fill a gap in its own camp.","98":"N-3c. Gote jumps its knight up to help defend.","99":"S*6e. Sente drops a silver from its hand. This silver is aiming at the gold on 5d.","100":"P*6d. Gote drops a pawn to try to stop the silver.","101":"Sx5d. The silver takes the gold on 5d. Another defender of Gote's king is gone.","102":"Gx5d. Gote takes back with the gold.","103":"P*3d. Sente drops a pawn and pokes at the area near Gote's king.","104":"Kx3d. Gote's king takes the pawn and steps out to 3d. The king is coming further forward.","105":"P*3f. Sente drops a pawn to hold down the front of Gote's king.","106":"S*4d. Gote drops a silver to protect the king.","107":"R-3i. Sente swings the rook back to file 3. Gote's king is on file 3, so the Side Rook is back at work.","108":"K-4c. Gote's king steps back to 4c.","109":"Px3e. The pawn on 3f takes the pawn on 3e. A Sente pawn moves right up close to Gote's king.","110":"P*3h. Gote drops a pawn that attacks the rook on 3i.","111":"G*3d, check. Sente drops a gold from its hand and drives Gote's king further.","112":"K-5c. Gote's king escapes to 5c.","113":"R-5i. Attacked by the pawn on file 3, the rook moves away to 5i.","115":"+B-2f. The horse steps aside to 2f. If Gote's silver came to 4f, it would hit the horse on 3g, so this seems to be dodging in advance.","116":"Sx4f. Gote's silver takes the pawn on 4f and comes closer to Sente's camp.","117":"Gx3c. The gold takes the knight. Sente shaves away Gote's pieces one by one.","119":"G-3d. The gold goes back to 3d, a square that blocks the king's escape.","121":"N*7d. Sente drops a knight. It aims at 8b, where Gote's rook is, and at 6b.","122":"R-4b. Gote runs the rook away to file 4.","123":"P*4d. Sente drops a pawn to pull Gote's defending gold out of place.","124":"Gx4d. Gote takes the pawn with the gold.","125":"Gx4d. Sente takes the gold with its gold.","126":"Rx4d. Gote takes back with the rook.","127":"Gx5f. The gold takes the pawn on 5f.","128":"Sx5f. Gote takes the gold with the silver.","129":"Rx5f. Sente takes back the silver with the rook. The trades go on, and the game reaches its final stage.","130":"S*5e. Gote drops a silver to chase Sente's rook.","131":"R-5i. The rook steps back.","132":"P*5h. Gote drops a pawn to chase the rook again.","133":"R-6i. The rook goes to 6i. Gote keeps chasing Sente's rook with pawns.","134":"K-5d. Gote's king comes out all the way to 5d. A king in the middle of the board has few defenders around it and is easy to target.","135":"+B-3f, check. The horse moves to 3f and gives check to the king on 5d along the diagonal through 4e. The horse that had guarded Sente's camp all this time joins the attack here.","136":"G*4e. Gote drops a gold to block the horse's line.","137":"G*6e, check. Sente drops a gold and closes in from right beside the king.","138":"Px6e. Gote takes the gold with a pawn.","139":"Sx6e, check. The silver on 7f takes that pawn with check. The silver that had stood in front of the king since the middle game joins the attack.","140":"K-4c. Gote's king goes back to 4c.","141":"+Bx4e. The horse takes the gold on 4e. Sente keeps taking the pieces around Gote's king.","142":"Rx4e. Gote takes the horse with the rook.","143":"S*5d, check. Seeing that the king could no longer escape, Sekine admitted defeat here. Sakata wins in 143 moves. He attacked on file 3 with the Side Rook, defended his camp with the horse through a long middle game while shaving away Gote's pieces little by little, and at the end caught Gote's king after it had come forward. It is a stubborn, determined game, just like the self-taught Sakata. After his death, Sakata was given the titles of Meijin and Ōshō."}},"nanzenji1937":{"t":"The Nanzen-ji Showdown: Sakata's Edge Pawn","w":"Kimura Yoshio","b":"Sakata Sankichi","ev":"Special match sponsored by the Yomiuri Shimbun","d":"February 1937","pl":"Japan (Nanzen-ji temple, Kyoto)","lead":"Sakata Sankichi had been cut off from the Tokyo shogi world for years after calling himself Meijin. In this game, at the age of 66, he returned to the big stage. His opponent was 31-year-old Kimura Yoshio, who would soon become the first Meijin chosen by strength. They played for 7 days at Nanzen-ji temple in Kyoto, with 30 hours of thinking time each. On his very first move, Sakata pushed the pawn on the far edge, and the whole country was astonished.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. Sakata Sankichi was born in Sakai near Osaka and became the great master of western Japan by teaching himself almost everything. At the end of the Taishō era he named himself 'Meijin' on his own, so the Tokyo shogi world cut ties with him, and for a long time he could not play Tokyo professionals officially. Later, Sekine Kinjirō stepped down as Meijin, and a new system began in which the Meijin would be decided by strength (the Meijin-sen). As part of that story, the Yomiuri newspaper arranged a game between Sakata and Kimura Yoshio, the leading candidate for the new title. The game was played from February 5 to 11, 1937, over 7 days at Nanzen-ji temple in Kyoto. Each player had an amazing 30 hours of thinking time, something unthinkable today.","1":"P-7f. The most ordinary first move, opening the bishop's diagonal. Kimura Yoshio was born in Honjo, Tokyo, and grew up as a disciple of Sekine Kinjirō. He valued the 'straight' style of piling up correct moves, and in this game too he sets up calmly and solidly.","2":"P-9d. This is the famous 'Sakata's edge pawn'. Gote is already one move behind, so by the common sense of the time, pushing the pawn on the very edge on move 2 was like throwing away another move. People say Sakata used this move to show his pride after years without a stage. The writer Oda Sakunosuke later wrote in one of his works how moved he was when he read about this move in the newspaper.","3":"P-5f. Kimura pushes the central pawn. Since his opponent has lost a move, he wants to take wide space in the center and grab the initiative. Kimura later wrote, in his own words, that the edge pawn surprised him greatly, but that he quickly started thinking about what his opponent intended.","4":"P-3d. Sakata opens his bishop's diagonal too. So far Gote has pushed the pawns on file 9 and file 3. Whether the edge pawn will ever be useful is one of the things to watch in this game. 1937, the year of this game, was also the year the war between Japan and China began, in July. Newspapers gave big shogi matches lots of space, and this one was called 'the match of the century'.","5":"P-5e. Kimura pushes the file-5 pawn one more step and takes 'the position on file 5'. Taking a position means advancing a pawn to the fifth rank and claiming the area around it. If you control the center, your opponent's pieces have fewer places to go.","6":"P-4d. A move that blocks his own bishop on purpose. Sakata meets the central pawn on 5e with a pawn on 4d and prepares to build his camp slowly. Blocking your own bishop like this is an idea often seen later in 'ranging rook' openings.","7":"S-4h. The right silver comes up to support the file-5 position. A position you have taken can easily collapse, so supporting it from behind with a silver is important.","8":"S-3b. Sakata also lifts a silver. This silver plans to go to 4c and then 3d, working on file 3. The next month, Sakata also played Hanada Chōtarō at Tenryū-ji temple in Kyoto, and that time he pushed the pawn on the opposite edge, P-1d, on move 2. Hanada won that game in 169 moves.","9":"S-5g. The silver now stands right behind the pawn on 5e. The file-5 position is firmly supported, and the 'central pawn position' setup is complete. The Meijin-sen, which decides the Meijin by strength, began when Sekine Kinjirō decided in 1935 to step down. At the time of this game, the league to choose the first Meijin was going on, and Kimura was leading it.","10":"S-4c. The silver comes to 4c. It watches the 5d square and keeps Sente's file-5 pawn from pushing further. Sakata's life later became the play 'Ōshō' by Hōjō Hideji, and became widely known through songs and films too.","11":"P-2f. Now Kimura pushes the pawn in front of his rook. On top of the central position, he prepares an attack on file 2 as well: a greedy and powerful plan. In even games of that time, battles where neither side moved the rook ('static rook' against 'static rook') were common, and Kimura is playing in that mainstream style.","12":"P-3e. Sakata stretches his file-3 pawn. Against Kimura's file-2 attack, he takes a position on file 3 to push back.","13":"P-2e. The rook's pawn moves forward again. Soon it will poke at the pawn on 2d, so Gote must decide how to use his bishop.","14":"B-3c. The bishop steps up to help guard file 2. Sakata will use the bishop for defense on file 2 and is getting ready to move his rook next.","15":"K-6h. Kimura starts moving his king to the left. A battle seems likely on the right side, so the basic idea is to move the king away to the other side.","16":"R-2b. Sakata swings his rook to file 2. Because it faces the opponent's rook directly, this opening is called the 'Opposing Rook'. Edge pawn plus Opposing Rook is a bold plan to meet the opponent's rook head-on, very much in Sakata's style. In even games between top players at that time, ranging rook openings were a minority, which also shows Sakata's individuality.","17":"P-4f. Pushing the file-4 pawn opens a path for the silver and gold to move forward. Kimura aims for a thick formation from the center to the right.","18":"G-3b. Sakata brings his left gold next to the rook, preparing for the fight on file 2. In the Opposing Rook, guarding the side of the rook with a gold is a common shape.","20":"S-3d. The silver advances to 3d and supports the pawn on 3e. Gote's pieces are moving forward bit by bit on files 3 and 2.","21":"K-7h. The king moves one more square to the left. Kimura's king is getting farther away from the right side, where the battle will happen.","22":"G-6b. Sakata lifts the gold from 6a to 6b. It is preparation for castling his king on the left side.","24":"K-6a. Sakata's king goes to 6a. In ranging rook openings, the king is usually castled on the side opposite the rook, toward files 7 and 8, and Sakata starts moving his king in that direction.","25":"G-4g. Kimura lifts his right gold to 4g. It supports the pawn on 4f and the 3f square, and the idea is to make the gold join the fight on the right. This setup values attack and central thickness more than the king's defense.","26":"P-2d. At last Sakata starts the fight. In the Opposing Rook, the basic plan is to clash pawns on file 2 like this and make the two rooks glare at each other.","27":"Px2d. Kimura takes the pawn. If he didn't, Gote would play P-2e and take it, and Gote's rook file would open first.","28":"Bx2d. The bishop takes back. It comes out to 2d and watches the right side of Sente's camp.","29":"P-5d. Kimura pushes the central pawn even further. The idea is to use the file-5 position to counterattack from the center; if Gote takes with the pawn, Sente's silver and bishop start working in the center. Because Gote's bishop has gone to 2d, the center has become a little thin.","30":"N-3c. Sakata doesn't take the pawn on 5d. Instead he jumps his knight to 3c and adds another attacking piece. His idea is to leave the center for now and hurry his attack on the right.","31":"P-3f. Kimura pushes the file-3 pawn, aiming to clash with Gote's pawn on 3e. The idea is to remove Gote's file-3 position and open a road for his own gold and silver.","32":"P*2f. Sakata drops a pawn from his hand on 2f. If Gote next plays P-2g+, the file 2 where Sente's rook stands will break open. It is a sharp move that stabs into the opponent's camp with a single light pawn.","33":"Px3e. Kimura ignores the pawn on 2f for now and takes the pawn on 3e. He probably thought that pushing a pawn to file 3 here would tie up Gote's bishop and silver.","34":"Bx3e. The bishop takes back the pawn on 3e. Since it is protected by the silver on 3d, it can't be captured right away for free.","35":"G-3f. Kimura lifts his gold and confronts the bishop. It offers a gold-for-bishop trade; if they trade, Sente gets a bishop in hand and also gains time to take Gote's pawn on 2f.","36":"K-7b. Sakata leaves the bishop for now and moves his king to 7b. He decides to move the king to a slightly safer place before the fighting gets fierce.","37":"Px5c+. Kimura's pawn on 5d captures and promotes, creating a tokin. It is a harsh move that promotes a pawn right next to Gote's king.","38":"Gx5c. Gote takes with the gold. This pulls Gote's gold to 5c, so the gold that was guarding the king has shifted out of place.","39":"P*2e. Kimura drops a pawn on 2e. It cuts the connection between Gote's pawn on 2f and the rook on 2b and leaves the pawn on 2f isolated. If he captures that pawn later, Sente's rook will be free.","41":"G-5i. Kimura pulls back his left gold to strengthen the defense around his king. It is a calm move that adds one defensive move before the race of attacks begins.","43":"Gx3e. Here the gold-for-bishop trade happens. Kimura gets a bishop, and Gote gets a gold in hand. The bishop is a major piece that works from far away, so it will be a trump card later when aiming at the king.","44":"Sx3e. The silver takes back the gold. Gote's silver comes out to 3e and presses toward Sente's camp.","45":"P*3d. Kimura drops a pawn that hits the knight on 3c. If he can play P-3c+ next, a hole will open in Gote's defense.","46":"Nx2e. Sakata jumps his knight while capturing the pawn on 2e. The knight escapes from the pawn's attack and at the same time comes closer to Sente's camp.","47":"P-4e. Kimura pushes the file-4 pawn, and together with the pawn on 3d it restricts Gote's pieces. A wall of Sente pawns is forming from the center to the right.","48":"R-2d. Gote's rook floats up to 2d. It works sideways, aims at the pawn on 3d, and joins the attack.","49":"Px4d. Kimura captures the pawn on 4d and moves forward again. If he gets to play P-4c+ next, Gote's camp will crumble badly.","50":"P*5e. Gote drops a pawn on 5e, right in the path of Sente's bishop. It is a lure: by making Sente take it with the bishop, Gote shifts the bishop's position.","51":"Bx5e. Kimura takes with the bishop. The bishop comes out to 5e in the center and watches far across the board.","52":"Gx4d. Sakata's gold from 5c captures the pawn on 4d and stops Sente's pawn attack. At the same time, the gold is now attacking the bishop on 5e.","53":"S-4f. Kimura lifts the silver to 4f and ties it to the bishop on 5e. It protects the bishop while adding the silver to the front line.","54":"Gx5e. Sakata takes the bishop with the gold. Sente could take back with the silver on 4f, but Kimura chooses a different move. He probably thought that taking the piece at the center of Gote's attack was more important than recapturing on 5e.","55":"Sx3e. Kimura does not recapture the gold on 5e; instead he takes Gote's silver on 3e. With a bishop traded for a silver, Gote is a little ahead in material. Even so, it means a lot that Sente's silver has come out near Gote's rook and knight.","56":"R-2a. The rook, which was attacked by the silver, retreats to 2a. It stays safe while still watching file 2.","57":"B*7g. Kimura drops the bishop he got onto 7g. The 7g square protects Sente's king, and from there the bishop also looks along a long diagonal through the center deep into Gote's camp. It is a drop square that helps both defense and attack.","58":"G*4e. Gote drops his gold from hand on 4e. Lining up two golds on 5e and 4e, he controls 5f and 4f in the center: a powerful move.","59":"P*5c. Kimura drops a pawn on 5c. If he plays P-5b+ next, a tokin will be born right next to Gote's king.","60":"P*5a. Sakata drops a pawn on the bottom rank to guard 5b. A defensive pawn like this is called a 'bottom pawn'. It is a quiet but important move that stops the opponent's tokin attack.","61":"S-4f. Kimura pulls the silver back to 4f, hitting Gote's golds on 4e and 5e. If a gold comes out to take it, Sente can use the time to push his attack on the right side.","62":"Gx4f. The gold from 5e takes the silver. Gote keeps gaining material, but in exchange his gold goes deep, close to Sente's camp.","63":"P-3c+. The pawn on 3d promotes and becomes a tokin. At last, Sente's pieces break into the right side of Gote's camp.","64":"R-2d. Gote floats his rook to 2d to keep the tokin in check from the side.","65":"+Px3b. The tokin captures the gold on 3b. A tokin is as cheap as a pawn but moves like a gold, and if it is captured the opponent gets only a pawn. Taking a gold with a tokin is a very happy gain for the attacker.","66":"R-3d. The rook comes out to 3d and presses on the right side of Sente's camp. Sakata also joins the race of attacks.","67":"+P-4b. The tokin moves even closer to the king. A tokin moves only one step at a time, but it surely closes in on Gote's king, as the saying 'a tokin is slow but sure' goes.","68":"P-7d. This makes an escape route for Gote's king. Before the tokin arrives, Sakata opens file 7 so the king can run upward.","69":"Rx2f. Kimura finally captures the pawn on 2f with his rook. The rook comes out to the sixth rank, works widely sideways, and also looks at the gold on 4f. The rook that couldn't move for so long has come back to life.","70":"R-3i+. Sakata drives his rook deep into Sente's camp and promotes it to a dragon. A dragon moves like a rook and can also move one square diagonally: a strong piece that threatens Sente's bottom rank.","71":"S*6a. Kimura drops a silver for free, giving check. By making Gote take with the king, he pulls the king back to the bottom rank and prepares to drop a gold there. Making the king's shape worse even at the cost of a piece is one of the standard techniques for catching the king.","72":"Kx6a. The king takes the silver. The king steps down to 6a, and fewer defenders are around it.","73":"G*8b. Kimura drops a gold on 8b. It blocks the escape route on the left of the king and also aims at the knight on 8a and the 7a square.","74":"S-7a. The silver steps back to stop the gold's attack.","75":"Rx4f. Here the rook captures the gold on 4f. Between attacking moves, Kimura wins pieces back one by one, recovering what Gote had taken earlier.","76":"Sx8b. Gote's silver also takes the gold on 8b. Both sides are grabbing each other's pieces in a fierce race.","77":"Rx4e. The rook takes the other gold on 4e as well. Now Sente is clearly ahead in material. The rook works sideways along the fifth rank and watches the area in front of Gote's king.","78":"K-7b. The king climbs to 7b and gets a little distance from the tokin and silver.","79":"+P-5b. The tokin advances again, closing in on Gote's king step by step.","80":"S*7a. Gote drops a silver from hand on 7a to strengthen the area under the king.","81":"G*6b, check. The king can only run upward and is being chased out into open space.","82":"K-7c. The king escapes to 7c. The escape route made by P-7d on move 68 is now useful.","83":"Rx2e. The rook captures the knight on 2e. Between checks, Kimura collects pieces one by one and saves them for the final attack.","84":"G*5f. Sakata drops a gold on 5f. It blocks the sideways path of Sente's rook and also puts pressure on Sente's camp: a stubborn move.","85":"G*6f. Kimura also drops a gold and attacks the gold on 5f. It goes after Gote's gold while also guarding above his own king: two birds with one stone.","86":"P-8d. Gote pushes the file-8 pawn, opening more room for the king to run.","87":"Gx5f. The gold takes the gold on 5f. Sente has more and more pieces, and Gote has fewer and fewer attacking pieces.","88":"B*4g. Sakata drops his bishop from hand on 4g, aiming at the gold on 5f. He never gives up and keeps looking for counterattacks.","89":"R-6e. The rook swings to 6e. It watches file 6 and aims to attack Gote's king from above.","90":"Sx6b. The silver takes the gold on 6b and clears an attacker near the king.","91":"+Px6b. The tokin takes back the silver. Even if a tokin is taken, the opponent gets only a pawn, so it is a reliable piece you can keep pushing forward.","92":"S*5d. Gote drops a silver and attacks the rook on 6e. It tries to chase the rook away and make the area above the king thicker.","93":"G*7b, check. Gote's king can only run to 8c now.","94":"K-8c. The king goes to 8c. It has been chased right next to the pawn on 9d, the edge pawn pushed on move 2.","95":"B-5e. The bishop on 7g comes out to 5e. Its diagonal watches 6d and 7c near Gote's king, and together with the rook, gold, and tokin there is no defense left. Sakata admitted defeat here, and Kimura won in 95 moves. The edge pawn on move 2 could not be put to use in this game, and later Naitō Kunio wrote that this was one of the greatest games in the history of shogi. In December of that year, Kimura became the first Meijin chosen by strength, and Sakata's edge pawn is still told as a legend marking the turning point of an era."}},"sokan1725":{"t":"Sōkan's brilliant knight drop N*3e","w":"Itō Sōkan III","b":"Ōhashi Sōmin (later Ōhashi Sōyo IV)","ev":"Castle shogi (the yearly game at Edo Castle)","d":"17th day of the 11th month, 1725 (Kyōhō 10)","pl":"Japan (Edo Castle)","lead":"A castle game between Itō Sōkan III (20), who would later become the 7th Meijin, and Ōhashi Sōmin (17), heir of the Ōhashi branch house and later Ōhashi Sōyo IV. Sente's Yagura castle meets Gote's wall of two silvers. Sōkan's knight drop N*3e on move 81 is a famous move that the later 15th Meijin Ōyama Yasuharu is said to have called 'a brilliant move'. Sōkan won in 101 moves.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. Today we watch a castle game from the middle of the Edo period, in 1725 (Kyōhō 10). Castle shogi was a yearly game at Edo Castle, played before the shōgun by the masters of the three shogi houses: the Ōhashi main house, the Ōhashi branch house and the Itō house. The 8th shōgun, Tokugawa Yoshimune, is said to have fixed it on the 17th day of the 11th month, and today's 'Shogi Day' (17 November) comes from this. Sente, Itō Sōkan III, was 20 and had taken over the Itō house only two years before, when his father Sōin II died. Gote, Ōhashi Sōmin, was 17 and would later take the name Ōhashi Sōyo IV. Watch this battle between two young heirs of the shogi houses.","1":"P-7f. This opens the bishop's diagonal and is the most common first move. In this year Arai Hakuseki died in Japan, and Peter the Great died in Russia. Yoshimune's 'Kyōhō reforms' were under way, and in Edo shogi was enjoyed by samurai and townspeople alike.","2":"P-3d. Gote opens its bishop's diagonal too. It is said that as a boy Sōkan loved swordsmanship and martial arts more than shogi. He is said to have thrown himself into shogi after his elder brother Indatsu died at 15 in 1712.","3":"P-2f. Sente pushes the pawn in front of the rook to prepare an attack on file 2. This start, P-7f, P-3d, P-2f, is still one of the most common openings today.","4":"P-4d. Gote pushes the pawn in front of its bishop and closes the diagonal again. It avoids a bishop trade and builds its camp slowly. In the castle games of this era, this P-4d, with Gote choosing to defend, appears very often.","5":"P-2e. Sente pushes the rook's pawn one more step. Next, P-2d would trade pawns on file 2, so Gote needs a move to stop it.","6":"B-3c. The bishop rises to guard the square 2d. Stopping the rook-pawn trade like this was Gote's normal defence in those days. Because the bishop is used for defence, Gote's game becomes a slow one.","7":"S-4h. Sente lifts the right silver. The silver is useful in attack and in defence, and it first heads for the centre.","8":"S-3b. Gote lifts a silver too and starts building the king's defence.","9":"P-5f. Sente pushes the centre pawn to make a path for the silver to 5g.","10":"S-4c. Gote's silver goes to 4c, and soon a second silver will stand beside it on 5c. Two years earlier, in the 1723 castle game between the same two players, Sōmin answered the same opening by moving his rook to 4b: a Fourth-File Rook. This time he leaves the rook on file 8 and fights with a Static Rook.","11":"P-4f. Sente pushes the pawn on file 4 so the pieces on the right can move easily. Sōkan is said to have played his first castle game at the age of 11, and won it.","12":"G-3b. Gote lifts a gold to strengthen the bishop on 3c and the king's defence.","13":"S-5g. The silver comes to the centre. This silver will move from square to square and become the leader of the attack.","14":"P-5d. Gote pushes its centre pawn too, preparing to bring a silver to 5c.","16":"S-6b. The left silver moves as well. Gote aims for a shape with two silvers side by side on 4c and 5c. This wall of silvers belongs to the family of shapes later called 'Gangi' (the 'wild-geese' formation).","17":"G-5h. Sente lifts a gold and starts building its king's castle.","18":"S-5c. Now the two silvers stand side by side. The silvers on 4c and 5c guard the centre, and Gote's camp has become very solid.","19":"K-6h. Sente moves its king to the left. Keeping the king away from the right side, where you will attack, is the basic rule of Static Rook play.","21":"P-6f. Sente pushes the pawn on file 6. This opens the way for the silver to come to 7g and build a 'Yagura castle'.","22":"P-8d. Gote pushes its rook's pawn and prepares to attack toward Sente's king.","25":"S-7g. The left silver goes to 7g. With the silver on 7g, a gold coming to 6g and a gold on 7h, the king will be wrapped in a Yagura castle. Yagura has been played since the Edo period and is still one of the most famous castles today.","28":"K-4a. Gote moves its king to the right. Each side castles its king on the opposite wing, so the fighting will happen far from each king.","29":"G-6g. The gold rises, and the Yagura shape is almost complete.","31":"B-7i. Sente pulls the bishop back. This empties 8h for the king, and the bishop will later go to 6h to attack along a diagonal.","33":"K-8h. The king enters the Yagura. From here on, how solid each king is will decide the game.","35":"G-7h. The other gold joins in, and the Yagura castle is complete: silver 7g, gold 6g, gold 7h and king 8h. A sturdy shape.","36":"N-7c. Gote jumps its left knight to aim at 6e. A knight is just the piece for attacking the silver of an enemy Yagura.","37":"S-4h. Sente pulls the silver from 5g back to 4h. The plan is to swing this silver round to the right, via 3g to 2f, and attack on files 2 and 3. It looks like a detour, but it is an important way of gathering attackers.","38":"R-8d. Gote lifts its rook one square so it can also move sideways: a 'floating rook'.","41":"S-3g. The silver reaches 3g. Sente is now ready to clash pawns on file 3 and start the attack.","43":"P-3e. The attack begins. Sente pushes the pawn on file 3 into Gote's, to break up the defence in front of the silver on 4c and the bishop on 3c.","44":"Px3e. Gote takes the pawn.","45":"S-2f. The silver goes to 2f. Next it wants to take on 3e with the silver and push it forward.","46":"S-3d. Gote lifts a silver to guard the pawn on 3e. The two sides' silvers glare at each other on file 3.","47":"R-3h. The rook swings to file 3. Sente gathers silver and rook on file 3 to break through there.","50":"P-6e. Gote starts a counter-attack on the other side. It pushes the pawn on file 6 into Sente's, aiming at the silver and gold of the Yagura. Both sides now attack on the side they like best: a lively fight.","51":"Px6e. Sente takes the pawn.","52":"Nx6e. Gote takes with the knight from 7c, and the knight lands on 6e. This knight aims at the silver on 7g and the square 5g, a very unpleasant piece for Sente.","53":"Sx3e. Sente does not give in either: the silver takes the pawn on 3e and comes forward. Leaving Gote's knight alone and putting its own attack first is a bold move.","54":"Sx3e. Gote's silver takes the silver.","55":"Rx3e. The rook takes back. The silvers have been traded and Sente's rook has come out to 3e.","56":"P*3d. Gote drops a pawn to block the rook.","57":"R-3h. The rook goes back home. Where Sente will use the silver it gained is something to look forward to.","58":"P-7e. Gote pushes the pawn on file 7 to strike at the head of Sente's Yagura. Together with the knight on 6e, Gote's attack is gathering near Sente's king.","59":"S-6f. Sente lifts the silver from 7g to 6f and hits the knight on 6e. It is a move that defends while trying to remove an attacking piece.","60":"S-6d. Gote lifts a silver to protect the knight on 6e.","61":"P*3e. Sente drops a pawn on file 3 again, aiming at the pawn on 3d.","62":"Px7f. Gote's pawn advances and takes Sente's pawn on 7f. The pawn in front of Sente's Yagura is gone, and the area around the king becomes a little draughty.","63":"Px3d. Sente takes in the pawn on 3d. This pawn now stands close to Gote's king and will do great work later.","64":"S-7e. Gote's silver goes to 7e. It hits the silver on 6f and presses further into Sente's camp.","65":"P-4e. Sente pushes the pawn on file 4 into Gote's pawn on 4d. It chips away, little by little, at the pawns that defend Gote's camp.","66":"Sx6f. Gote's silver takes the silver on 6f. Checked with this site's shogi AI, Gote, Sōmin, has the easier game around here. The knight on 6e and the silver on 7e are pressing on Sente's king, and balancing defence and attack is hard for Sente.","67":"Gx6f. Sente takes back with the gold. The gold comes to the centre and glares at the knight on 6e.","68":"S*7e. Gote drops a silver from its hand to aim at the gold on 6f.","69":"Gx6e. Sente's gold takes the knight on 6e. Sente removes Gote's attacking knight and gains material.","70":"P-8f. Gote pushes the pawn on file 8 to strike at the head of Sente's king.","71":"Gx7e. The gold takes the silver on 7e. Sente's gold is doing wonderful work.","72":"Bx7e. Gote takes back the gold with the bishop from 4b. This bishop aims at Sente's camp along a long diagonal.","73":"Bx8f. Sente's bishop from 6h takes the pawn on 8f. It meets Gote's bishop head-on and offers a bishop trade.","74":"Bx8f. Gote trades the bishops.","75":"Px8f. Sente takes back with the pawn. Through these captures Sente has gained silvers and a bishop, and this site's shogi AI now sees the game as roughly level again. But Gote also has a bishop in hand and comes at Sente's king.","76":"B*6f. Gote drops the bishop to aim at the area around Sente's king. This bishop points toward the king on 8h and is the base for the next attack.","77":"P*7g. Sente drops a pawn to cut the bishop's line.","78":"P*8g. Gote drops a pawn on 8g, pressing on the head of Sente's king.","79":"Gx8g. Sente takes the pawn with the gold. But because the gold moved to 8g, the square 7h has become empty.","80":"S*6g. Gote drops a silver right next to Sente's king. If Gote follows with something like S-7h+, Sente's king will be in real danger. Normally this is the moment to think about defending.","81":"N*3e. This is the highlight of the game, Sōkan's famous move. Instead of defending, he dropped a knight on 3e, near Gote's king. Next the knight threatens to jump into Gote's camp with N-4c+ or N-2c, and it cannot be taken because the rook on 3h guards it from behind. What is more, Sente's pawn on 3d means Gote cannot chase it away with a pawn. With his own king in danger, Sōkan found an attack one step faster than his opponent's, and the later 15th Meijin Ōyama Yasuharu is said to have called it 'a brilliant move'.","82":"G-4b. Gote pulls the gold from 4c back to 4b to prepare for the knight's jump.","83":"S*4c. Sente drops a silver from its hand on 4c, aiming at the gold on 3b. The knight on 3e reaches 4c, so this silver is supported from behind.","84":"Px7g+. Gote attacks too. It takes the pawn on 7g, makes a tokin and presses on Sente's king.","85":"Nx7g. Sente takes the tokin with the knight.","86":"P*7f. Gote drops a pawn to attack the knight on 7g. Gote is trying to strip away the pieces around Sente's king one by one.","87":"Sx3b+. Sente's silver takes the gold on 3b and promotes. One of the golds guarding Gote's king is gone.","88":"Gx3b. Gote takes back the promoted silver with the gold from 4b.","89":"B*5c. Sente drops a bishop from its hand on 5c. Because the gold on 4b has moved to 3b, the diagonal from 5c through 4b to 3a is open, so this is check. Gote has to block it with something.","90":"S*4b. Gote drops a silver to block the bishop's line.","91":"S*4c. Sente drops another silver on 4c, aiming once more at the gold on 3b.","92":"G*5b. Gote drops a gold, which attacks the bishop on 5c and strengthens the defence.","93":"N-2c (no promotion). The knight dropped on 3e on move 81 finally jumps in. Not promoting is the key: an unpromoted knight still reaches 3a, so this is check. A promoted knight moves like a gold and would not reach 3a, so it would not be check.","94":"K-2b. Gote's king escapes to 2b.","95":"Sx3b+. The silver on 4c takes the gold on 3b and promotes.","96":"Kx3b. Gote takes the promoted silver with the king. The king has come out to 3b and has almost no defenders left.","97":"G*3c. A gold drop with check, driving Gote's king out.","98":"Nx3c. Gote takes the gold with the knight.","99":"Px3c+. The pawn on 3d takes the knight and becomes a tokin. The pawn Sente took in on move 63 does great work here.","100":"Sx3c. Gote takes the tokin with the silver.","101":"B-3a+. The bishop on 5c jumps to 3a, promotes to a horse and gives check. Here Sōmin admitted defeat. Even when his own king was in danger, Sōkan did not turn to defence; from N*3e he chased Gote's king down in one go. Three years later, in 1728, Sōkan became the 7th Meijin at 23, and in 1734 he presented his tsume-shogi collection 'Shōgi Musō' to the shogunate. The loser, Sōmin, later took the name Ōhashi Sōyo IV and fought the Meijin Sōkan many times. The N*3e born from this battle of two young players is still told as a move that impresses professional players today."}},"alekhine1930":{"t":"Alekhine's Gun","w":"Alexander Alekhine","b":"Aron Nimzowitsch","ev":"San Remo international tournament, round 3","d":"January 1930","pl":"Italy (San Remo)","lead":"The world champion Alekhine squeezes his opponent's camp little by little until he wins. At the end White lines up two rooks and the queen one behind another on the c-file, a formation later called “Alekhine's gun”. Black resigns before losing any piece, because every one of his pieces has run out of good moves. Some records continue the game for a few more moves before it ends.","c":{"0":"January 1930, San Remo, a seaside town in Italy. This is round 3 of a 16-player round robin. White is Alexander Alekhine, world champion since he beat Capablanca in 1927. Black is Aron Nimzowitsch, the thinker whose 1925 book My System spread new ideas about chess. Alekhine won this tournament with an amazing 14 points from 15 games (13 wins, 2 draws). At the end of this game you will see White's “gun” lined up on the c-file.","1":"White plays e4. It takes a central square and opens lines for the queen and bishop: the most common first move since long ago. Alekhine was born into a wealthy family in Moscow, left Russia after the revolution, and later became a French citizen.","2":"Black plays e6: this is the French Defence. The name is said to come from a correspondence match between London and Paris around 1834, which the Paris side won with this move. Nimzowitsch was a master of this defence.","3":"White plays d4. The pawns on e4 and d4 build a big wall in the centre. In the French, White's theme is how to keep these two pawns supported.","4":"Black plays d5. He hits the e4 pawn at once and makes White choose: take, defend, or push ahead. This is the basic French position.","5":"The knight goes to c3. It is the most natural way to defend the e4 pawn. Black now chooses a move that ties this knight down.","6":"Black plays ♗b4: the Winawer Variation, named after the 19th-century Polish master Szymon Winawer. The bishop pins the c3 knight against the king and weakens its guard of e4. Tying down the opponent's pieces was a plan Nimzowitsch loved most.","7":"White plays e5. The pawn steps forward so that Black's knight cannot come to f6. White's pawns on d4 and e5 now form a diagonal “pawn chain”. In My System, Nimzowitsch taught that you should attack such a chain at its base.","8":"Black plays c5. Just as his book says, he attacks d4, the base of the chain. This is Black's most common counterattack in the French.","9":"White plays ♗d2. Today 5.a3 is more common, asking the bishop to take or retreat, but White calmly offers a bishop exchange instead. Later this line also became known under Bogoljubow's name. White wants to fight without spoiling his pawn structure.","10":"Black plays ♘e7. The knight will go on to c6 or f5, a typical French setup. The f6 square is covered by the e5 pawn, so the knight takes this road.","11":"White plays ♘b5! An unusual move: the knight hits the bishop and heads for d6. The d6 square is protected by the e5 pawn and no black pawn can chase a piece away from it: a “hole”. Alekhine loved to make opponents think hard with surprising moves away from the usual theory.","12":"Black plays ♗xd2+. Before his bishop is taken, he trades it for White's bishop. With this, Black gives up the piece that was pinning along the diagonal.","13":"White plays ♕xd2. The queen recaptures and lands on a square where it works well. White keeps his plan of aiming at d6.","14":"Black castles. The king moves to safety on g8. With the centre closed, Black first makes his defence firm.","15":"White plays c3. It supports the d4 pawn and also prepares b4 later. White has not castled yet, but since the centre is closed his king is in no hurry.","16":"Black plays b6, preparing to bring the c8 bishop to a6. In the French this bishop is blocked by its own pawns and is called the “bad bishop”. Black wants to trade it for one of White's good pieces.","17":"White plays f4. It gives the e5 pawn a second defender and weakens Black's counter-push f6. White has fixed the centre very solidly.","18":"Black plays ♗a6. The idea is to trade the bad bishop for the b5 knight or the f1 bishop. It is a logical move, typical of Nimzowitsch.","19":"White plays ♘f3. The last knight comes out to support d4 and e5. White still does not agree to the bishop trade.","20":"Black plays ♕d7. The queen also aims at the b5 knight. If Black takes with ♗xb5 next, White must recapture with a pawn or the bishop.","21":"White plays a4. If ♗xb5, then axb5 opens the a-file and grabs lots of space on the queenside. The tournament began on 16 January this year and lasted until 4 February. It was a big stage for Alekhine as the new world champion.","22":"Black plays ♘bc6. The other knight comes out and eyes b4 and d4. Black does not hurry the bishop trade and guards the centre with numbers.","23":"White plays b4! He pushes the queenside pawns and widens the battle there. Opening lines on the side where you have more pieces makes your advantage grow.","24":"Black plays cxb4. If he does not take, White pushes further with b5 or c5, so he takes and the lines open.","25":"White plays cxb4. Now the c-file is open from top to bottom. This file will become the stage of the whole fight. White's b4 pawn also controls c5 and a5.","26":"Black plays ♗b7, pulling the bishop back. Perhaps he thought that trading on b5 would give White strong play on the a- and c-files. But White's knight heads straight to its next target.","27":"White plays ♘d6! The knight lands in the d6 hole it was aiming for. No black pawn can drive it away. It eyes the b7 bishop and sits like a giant in the middle of Black's camp.","28":"Black plays f5. He stops the kingside pawns and prevents White's f4-f5 attack. But now the e6 pawn can never move, and Black's camp becomes even more frozen.","29":"White plays a5. He pushes again and shakes the b6 pawn. White keeps squeezing Black on the queenside.","30":"Black plays ♘c8. The knight heads over to trade itself for the d6 knight. White's powerful knight is the piece Black hates most.","31":"White plays ♘xb7. Before he can be traded, White takes Black's good bishop himself. Now Black has lost the piece that guarded the light squares.","32":"Black plays ♕xb7, recapturing with the queen. The material is equal, but White's bishop can now attack Black's weak light squares.","33":"White plays a6! The pawn hits the queen and freezes the a7 pawn. Squares like b7 and c6 are becoming White's.","34":"Black plays ♕f7. The queen retreats to the kingside. There is little room left for her on the queenside.","35":"White plays ♗b5. The bishop travels along the light squares and stares at the c6 knight. Black will have to use many pieces to guard c6.","36":"Black plays ♘8e7. The other knight defends c6. Black's pieces are being gathered on passive squares, one after another.","37":"White finally castles. With the centre closed there was no need to hurry. With the king safe, the rook can join the fight.","38":"Black plays h6. He makes an escape square near the king in case White attacks on the kingside. But it is not a real plan.","39":"White plays ♖fc1. The rook enters the open c-file. From now on White's pieces gather on the c-file one by one.","40":"Black plays ♖fc8. Black also puts a rook on the c-file to guard c6 and c7. The tug of war over the c-file begins.","41":"White plays ♖c2. The rook steps up one square to make room for another piece behind it. The plan of stacking pieces on the file is becoming clear.","42":"Black plays ♕e8. The queen also comes to guard c6. Black now defends c6 with two knights, the queen and a rook.","43":"White plays ♖ac1. The second rook comes to the c-file too. Two white rooks are now stacked on it.","44":"Black plays ♖ab8. His rook goes to the b-file and eyes the b4 pawn. Black is looking for a way to hit back on the queenside.","45":"White plays ♕e3. The queen moves aside for a moment to make a path to the back of the rooks. From here White slowly rearranges his pieces.","46":"Black plays ♖c7. The rook on c7 guards c6 once more. It prepares for White's stacking on the c-file.","47":"White plays ♖c3. The front rook steps out to c3. Now c2 and c1 are free, so the queen can come behind.","48":"Black plays ♕d7. The queen stays near c6 and c7 to keep the number of defenders. Almost every black piece is now working to guard c6.","49":"White plays ♖1c2. The back rook moves up to c2. In front is c3, behind it c2, and the empty c1 square is for the queen.","50":"Black plays ♔f8. The king walks toward the queenside to join the defence. Black is so hard pressed that even his king must become a defender.","51":"White plays ♕c1. The rook on c3, the rook on c2 and the queen on c1 now stand in a column on the c-file. This is the formation later called “Alekhine's gun”. Historians found that the name only appears in books from the 1990s, and earlier Russian books are said to have called it “Alekhine's arrangement”. When rooks and queen are stacked on one file, the pieces behind can recapture if the front one is taken, so they push on a square with great force.","52":"Black plays ♖bc8. Black also puts his last rook on the c-file to guard c6 and c7. By counting, everything is defended, but Black's pieces can barely move.","53":"White plays ♗a4. The idea is b5 next, attacking the c6 knight. The knight stands in front of the c7 rook, so if it runs away, the c3 rook takes on c7.","54":"Black plays b5. He gives up a pawn to stop White's b5. Any other move would let White win the knight with b5.","55":"White plays ♗xb5. He takes the pawn, and the bishop again eyes c6 and e8. White is a pawn up and the squeeze goes on.","56":"Black plays ♔e8. The king heads to d8 to protect the c7 rook. Black has nothing better than to bring his king toward the dangerous centre.","57":"White plays ♗a4. The bishop goes back and again threatens to push a pawn to b5. This time the b4 pawn is free to go to b5.","58":"Black plays ♔d8. The king now protects c7. So if b5 comes, the knight can leave c6.","59":"White plays h4: just one pawn step, a waiting move. But Black has almost no move left. If the queen or a knight moves, c6 cannot be held; if a rook moves, the c-file collapses; if the king moves, c7 becomes weak. It is said that his few pawn moves would soon run out too, and Nimzowitsch resigned here. A position where having to move is itself a loss is called “zugzwang”. After the tournament Alekhine put no fewer than seven games from it into his collection of best games."}}};
